/* Generated by align-meier.py from meier-auszug.js and meier-en.json — do not hand-edit.
   MEIER_ALIGNED[§] = [ { o: [start, end) in the § text of MEIER_AUSZUG.paras, en: the English,
   m: [n German, m English sentences] only where several were joined } … ]
   The pairing is inferred (length and shared anchors); the English is machine-translated. */
const MEIER_ALIGNED = {"1":[{"o":[0,207],"en":"The doctrine of reason or the art of reason (logica, philosophia instrumentalis, philosophia rationalis) is a science that treats of the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation."}],"2":[{"o":[0,359],"en":"So that the doctrine of reason may contain no wholly arbitrary, contrived and unnatural laws, its rules must be derived 1) from the experiences of the effects of human reason, 2) from the nature of human reason, 3) from the general fundamental truths on which all human cognition rests."}],"3":[{"o":[0,240],"en":"The aim of the doctrine of reason is either the perfection of a learned cognition and of a learned presentation, which suit only professional scholars, or which are also fitting and useful for other learned persons."}],"4":[{"o":[0,206],"en":"The doctrine of reason is a means without which no learned cognition and science can be attained, and through whose proper use a learned cognition and science is attained §.1."}],"5":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"Philosophy (philosophia) is a science of the more general properties of things, insofar as they are cognized without faith."},{"o":[143,426],"en":"Since learned cognition and learned presentation now comprise many kinds under themselves, their perfections and imperfections are more general properties of things, which are fully proved in the doctrine of reason without deriving their truth from testimony."},{"o":[427,487],"en":"The doctrine of reason is accordingly a part of philosophy."}],"6":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"The doctrine of reason treats either of a wholly certain learned cognition and the presentation of it, or of probable learned cognition and the presentation of it §.1."},{"o":[195,379],"en":"The former is the doctrine of reason of wholly certain learned cognition (analytica), and the latter the doctrine of reason of probable learned cognition (dialectica, logica probabilium)."},{"o":[380,419],"en":"We treat of the first doctrine of reason."}],"7":[{"o":[0,160],"en":"In the doctrine of reason, the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation are either applied to their particular kinds or not."},{"o":[161,287],"en":"The former is the practicing doctrine of reason (logica practica, utens) and the latter the teaching doctrine of reason (logica theoretica, docens)."}],"8":[{"o":[0,149],"en":"When the doctrine of reason is constituted as it can and must be by virtue of its nature, it has, among others, a threefold utility."},{"o":[150,251],"en":"1) It promotes the learning and dissemination of all sciences, and of learnedness as a whole."},{"o":[252,349],"en":"Every student should therefore properly begin with the learning of the doctrine of reason."},{"o":[350,508],"en":"2) It improves the understanding and reason, and shows how these powers of cognition must be used in order to cognize the truth in a proper manner."},{"o":[509,721],"en":"3) It promotes virtue as a whole, in that it improves the free will; provides that cognition on which virtue rests; and has an indispensable influence on the improvement of conscience."}],"9":[{"o":[0,77],"en":"The doctrine of reason treats I. Of learned cognition."},{"o":[80,124],"en":"1. Of learned cognition in general."},{"o":[127,181],"en":"2. Of the extensiveness of learned cognition."},{"o":[185,230],"en":"3. Of the magnitude of learned cognition."},{"o":[233,280],"en":"4. Of the truth of learned cognition."},{"o":[284,331],"en":"5. Of the clarity of learned cognition."},{"o":[335,384],"en":"6. Of the certainty of learned cognition."},{"o":[388,434],"en":"7. Of practical learned cognition."},{"o":[437,465],"en":"8. Of learned concepts."},{"o":[469,497],"en":"9. Of learned judgments."},{"o":[500,537],"en":"10. Of learned inferences of reason."},{"o":[540,543],"en":"II."},{"o":[545,587],"en":"Of the method of learned cognition."},{"o":[591,595],"en":"III."},{"o":[597,624],"en":"Of learned presentation."},{"o":[627,659],"en":"1. Of the use of words."},{"o":[663,697],"en":"2. Of the learned style of writing."},{"o":[701,730],"en":"3. Of a learned speech."},{"o":[733,761],"en":"4. Of learned writings."},{"o":[764,767],"en":"IV."},{"o":[769,803],"en":"Of the character of a scholar."},{"o":[808,826],"en":"The doctrine of reason."},{"o":[826,856],"en":"Of learned cognition."},{"o":[856,896],"en":"Of learned cognition in general."}],"10":[{"o":[0,68],"en":"Experience teaches that we represent to ourselves infinitely many things."},{"o":[69,222],"en":"A representation (repraesentatio perceptio) behaves as a picture, which the painterly skill of the soul draws in its interior."}],"11":[{"o":[0,151],"en":"Cognition (cognitio) is either a sum total of many representations, or that action through which a representation of a thing is effected."},{"o":[152,260],"en":"One may also, without fearing a noticeable error, hold representations and cognition to be one and the same."}],"12":[{"o":[0,114],"en":"Distinct from representation and cognition is that which we represent to ourselves and which we cognize."},{"o":[115,234],"en":"The latter is called the object of cognition and of representation (obiectum cognitionis et repraesentationis)."}],"13":[{"o":[0,187],"en":"We are conscious of our representations and of our cognition (conscium esse, adpercipere) insofar as we distinguish them and their object from other representations and things."},{"o":[188,323],"en":"Consciousness is a double representation: a representation of the object, and a representation of its difference from others."},{"o":[324,434],"en":"Consciousness behaves like light in the corporeal world, which discloses to us the difference of bodies."}],"14":[{"o":[0,246],"en":"When we are conscious of a representation, we are conscious of it either merely as considered as a whole, so that we distinguish nothing from one another in it itself; or we are also conscious of the manifold in it."},{"o":[247,606],"en":"In the first case we have an indistinct or a confused cognition (cognitio indistincta et confusa), in the other, however, a distinct one (cognitio distincta); for example, when we see a human being from afar, we have an indistinct cognition of his face for as long as we do not catch sight of the parts and features of the face."},{"o":[607,785],"en":"But if he comes nearer to us, and we begin to become aware of his eyes, his nose and the features of his face, we attain a distinct cognition of his face."}],"15":[{"o":[0,247],"en":"That from which a thing, be it either a cognition or the object of one, can be cognized, is its ground (ratio), and what can be cognized from the ground is its consequence (rationatum)."},{"o":[248,350],"en":"Light is the ground of the visibility of bodies, and this visibility is a consequence of light."},{"o":[351,491],"en":"The connection of things (nexus, consequentia) consists in this, when the one is the ground of the other, or contains it in itself."},{"o":[492,767],"en":"The ground of a thing is either so constituted that we can derive our whole cognition of it from it, such that nothing further besides it is required in order to cognize everything found in the thing; or it is not so constituted."},{"o":[768,877],"en":"The former is the sufficient ground (ratio sufficiens), and the latter the insufficient ground (ratio insufficiens)."}],"16":[{"o":[0,102],"en":"Everything that is possible and actual has a ground, and everything also has a sufficient ground."}],"17":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"When we cognize something, we cognize it either in a distinct manner from grounds, or not."},{"o":[101,185],"en":"If the first is the case, we have a rational cognition (cognitio rationalis)."},{"o":[186,386],"en":"Three things are required for such a cognition: 1) a cognition of a thing, 2) a cognition of its ground, and 3) a distinct cognition of the connection of the thing with its ground."},{"o":[387,750],"en":"For example: when I cognize that all human beings can err because they have a limited understanding, and I think: whoever has a limited understanding can err; now all human beings have a limited understanding, therefore they can all err: then I have a rational cognition of the truth that all human beings can err."}],"18":[{"o":[0,155],"en":"Every cognition, insofar as it is not rational, is called a common or a historical cognition (cognitio vulgaris, historica)."},{"o":[156,390],"en":"All things can be cognized historically, and one may even cognize their grounds; so long as one does not distinctly see the connection of the consequences with their grounds, one has only a merely historical cognition."}],"19":[{"o":[0,236],"en":"A more perfect historical cognition is a beautiful cognition (cognitio pulcra, aesthetica), and the beautiful sciences occupy themselves with the rules through whose observance historical cognition is beautified."}],"20":[{"o":[0,86],"en":"Although historical cognition is very different from rational cognition §.17."},{"o":[87,418],"en":"18, such that the most beautiful historical cognition does not even deserve to be called a rational cognition §.19; yet the former is indispensable to the latter, in that a human being cannot attain a rational cognition of a thing unless he previously possesses a historical cognition of it."}],"21":[{"o":[0,174],"en":"Learned and philosophical cognition (cognitio erudita et philosophica) is a rational cognition that is perfect in a higher or more noticeable degree."}],"22":[{"o":[0,204],"en":"When the manifold in a cognition agrees toward an aim, or contains the sufficient ground of it: the perfection of cognition (perfectio cognitionis) consists in this."},{"o":[205,338],"en":"The perfections of cognition occur either in it insofar as it is distinct or insofar as it is indistinct §.14."},{"o":[339,523],"en":"The former are called the logical perfections of cognition (perfectio cognitionis logica), and the latter the beauties of it (pulcritudo et perfectio aesthetica cognitionis)."},{"o":[524,656],"en":"E.g. mathematical certainty is a logical perfection, and painterly liveliness a beauty of cognition."}],"23":[{"o":[0,128],"en":"Insofar as a cognition is not perfect, insofar it is an imperfect cognition (imperfectio cognitionis)."},{"o":[129,265],"en":"The imperfections of cognition occur either in it insofar as it is distinct, or insofar as it is indistinct §.14."},{"o":[266,458],"en":"The former are called the logical imperfections of cognition (imperfectio cognitionis logica), and the latter the uglinesses of it (deformitas, imperfectio cognitionis aesthetica)."},{"o":[459,635],"en":"E.g. the swinish and bawdy in everyday jests is an ugliness of cognition; a false inference of reason, however, is a logical imperfection of it."}],"24":[{"o":[0,95],"en":"Learned cognition must be adorned with the perfections of cognition §.21."},{"o":[96,348],"en":"22. Consequently it possesses either merely the logical perfections of cognition, in that it is either not beautiful at all or is at the same time even ugly; or it possesses, besides the logical perfections, the beauties of cognition §.22."},{"o":[349,521],"en":"23. The former is a merely learned cognition (cognitio mere erudita), and the latter a cognition that is beautiful and learned at the same time (cognitio aesthetico-logica)."},{"o":[522,612],"en":"The latter is more perfect than the former, and the former must not be sought alone."}],"25":[{"o":[0,69],"en":"The more we cognize, the more perfect is our cognition §.22."},{"o":[70,294],"en":"The first perfection of learned cognition therefore consists in its extensiveness (vastitas, ubertas cognitionis eruditae), which is ascribed to a cognition insofar as it represents many objects to us."}],"26":[{"o":[0,129],"en":"The greater and more important our cognition is, the more perfect it is, because a great thing comprehends much in itself §.22."},{"o":[130,358],"en":"The second perfection of learned cognition accordingly consists in its magnitude and importance (dignitas, magnitudo et maiestas cognitionis eruditae), which belongs to a cognition insofar as it is great and important."},{"o":[359,449],"en":"E.g. the cognition of God is more important than the cognition of the clothes of the Romans."}],"27":[{"o":[0,160],"en":"Because a false cognition is no cognition at all, the truth of cognition (veritas cognitionis eruditae) is the third perfection of it."},{"o":[161,331],"en":"It can be called the fundamental perfection of cognition, because without it the cognition is no cognition at all, and hence also not capable of any perfection."}],"28":[{"o":[0,182],"en":"Since in a distinct representation we represent to ourselves more than in an indistinct one §.14, the distinctness of learned cognition is the fourth perfection of it §.25."}],"29":[{"o":[0,99],"en":"The consciousness of the truth of a cognition is its certainty (certitudo subiective spectata)."},{"o":[100,208],"en":"Since now both the truth of cognition and the consciousness of it are a perfection §.13."},{"o":[209,297],"en":"17, the certainty of learned cognition is the fifth perfection of it."}],"30":[{"o":[0,211],"en":"A learned cognition is practical insofar as it contributes its share to the arrangement of our free actions (cognitio erudita practica), and in this consists the sixth perfection of it §.22."}],"31":[{"o":[0,143],"en":"The more extensive, important, correct, distinct, certain and practical a learned cognition is, the more perfect it is §.25-30."},{"o":[144,502],"en":"Since now every rational human being must at all times strive toward the greatest perfection that is possible for him; so, if he strives to attain a learned cognition, he must 1) seek to reach all logical perfections of it, 2) each of them in the highest possible degree, and besides this 3) the beauties of cognition §.22."}],"32":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"Whoever accordingly wants to attain the most perfect learned cognition must not be content with a merely learned cognition §.24."},{"o":[145,426],"en":"31. Rather, although not all his learned representations can be beautiful at the same time, his learned cognition, considered as a whole, must nevertheless at the same time be a beautiful cognition, if it is to be improved to as high a degree as is possible."}],"33":[{"o":[0,84],"en":"A learned cognition can 1) be logically perfect and imperfect at the same time."},{"o":[85,489],"en":"E.g. a correct, distinct and certain cognition can have the fault that it is not practical; 2) be logically perfect in a higher degree than imperfect, or more imperfect than perfect; 3) be logically perfect and beautiful or ugly at the same time; 4) be logically perfect and neither beautiful nor ugly; 5) be logically imperfect and at the same time beautiful or ugly §.22."},{"o":[490,493],"en":"23."}],"34":[{"o":[0,510],"en":"Whoever wants to attain a learned cognition that is at the same time beautiful §.32 must 1) not make it logically perfect in such a manner and to such a degree that all beauty of it is thereby prevented; 2) he must not beautify it in such a manner and to such a degree that the required logical perfection of it is thereby made impossible; 3) he must seek chiefly to preserve the logical perfections, and he must introduce the beauties only more sparingly, as an ornament."}],"35":[{"o":[0,211],"en":"If some perfections in learned cognition cannot be attained at the same time, one must let go the smaller and less necessary perfections in order to retain the greater and more necessary ones."},{"o":[212,412],"en":"Accordingly, in a learned cognition that is not to be merely learned, one must often relax logical strictness in trifles in order to attain the greater beauty of cognition."}],"36":[{"o":[0,82],"en":"The imperfections of learned cognition are either deficiencies or faults."},{"o":[83,259],"en":"A deficiency of learned cognition (defectus cognitionis eruditae) arises when certain rules of its perfection are neither observed nor transgressed."},{"o":[260,381],"en":"E.g. when a principal concept is not explained at all, the rules of explanations are neither observed nor transgressed."},{"o":[382,520],"en":"A fault of learned cognition (vitium cognitionis eruditae) arises when the rules of its perfection are transgressed."},{"o":[521,564],"en":"E.g. when one explains a concept falsely."},{"o":[565,825],"en":"Although one must avoid all deficiencies and faults, one must nevertheless guard more against the latter, because one can say that every fault is bound up with a deficiency, and a fault is accordingly a greater imperfection than a mere deficiency."}],"37":[{"o":[0,133],"en":"Common and historical cognition can be much more perfect than the merely learned, namely when it is very beautiful §.22.23."},{"o":[134,273],"en":"E.g. an unlearned general and minister can possess a much more perfect cognition than a learned and pedantic bookworm."},{"o":[274,431],"en":"This advantage belongs to common cognition only accidentally, when the learned cognition is not as perfect as it could and should be."}],"38":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"Learned cognition is always necessarily more perfect than common cognition, when they are equal to each other in the remaining respects §.18."},{"o":[145,148],"en":"21."}],"39":[{"o":[0,350],"en":"Although common cognition is very useful, and in infinitely many cases sufficient to promote our welfare, indeed although some common cognition can accidentally be more useful than some learned cognition: yet learned cognition is necessarily more useful than common, when they are equal to each other in the remaining respects."},{"o":[351,810],"en":"For 1) since it is more perfect §.38, it also improves the power of cognition in a higher degree than the common; 2) for the very same reason it affords a greater pleasure than the common; 3) it is more fitting and more becoming to the character of humanity than the common; 4) it promotes the discovery of new truths more than the common; and 5) it can be applied and practiced much more skillfully and better than common cognition."}],"40":[{"o":[0,137],"en":"A learned cognition that is at the same time beautiful affords all the utilities of the learned §.39, and all the utilities of beautiful cognition."},{"o":[138,339],"en":"And since it at the same time prevents all the harm of the merely learned and of the merely beautiful cognition: it is among all kinds of human cognition the most useful and serviceable cognition."},{"o":[339,389],"en":"Of the extensiveness of learned cognition."}],"41":[{"o":[0,333],"en":"The imperfection of learned cognition that is opposed to its extensiveness §.25 is the paltriness of learned cognition (angustia eruditae cognitionis), and it always arises from ignorance (ignorantia) or from the total lack of cognition of things and of their grounds."},{"o":[334,551],"en":"To the degree that the extensiveness of a human being's learned cognition increases, his ignorance decreases, and the greater a human being's ignorance is, the more paltry is his learned cognition."}],"42":[{"o":[0,378],"en":"A twofold ignorance is opposed to the extensiveness of learned cognition: 1) a total ignorance (ignorantia totalis), when we do not even have a historical cognition of a thing; and 2) an ignorance of the grounds of things (ignorantia rationum), in which an excellent historical cognition of them can still take place."}],"43":[{"o":[0,367],"en":"A human being's ignorance is 1) an absolutely necessary and unavoidable ignorance (ignorantia absolute necessaria et invincibilis), which he cannot avoid on account of the limits of his power of cognition; and 2) a voluntary one (ignorantia arbitraria et vincibilis), the opposite cognition of which he could attain if he wished."}],"44":[{"o":[0,263],"en":"The sum total of all those things which a human being can cognize in a learned manner without detriment to the rest of his entire perfection is the horizon, or the field of vision, of his learned cognition (horizon seu sphaera cognitionis eruditae)."},{"o":[264,413],"en":"All things are therefore excluded from it with respect to which a human being must remain ignorant, whether of necessity or voluntarily §.43."}],"45":[{"o":[0,283],"en":"A thing is elevated above the horizon of human learned cognition (res supra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita) when ignorance of it in a human being is absolutely necessary, although it is not unworthy of a learned cognition."},{"o":[284,402],"en":"The human understanding is too weak to be in a position to cognize these important things in a learned manner."},{"o":[403,800],"en":"Although now one must not despise such things as go beyond our horizon, and although one must not without sufficient ground pass off as a thing something that is elevated above the horizon of our cognition; yet one must seek as far as possible to avoid the vain and harmful striving after a learned cognition of such things as go beyond our horizon."}],"46":[{"o":[0,283],"en":"A thing is lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition (res infra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita) which indeed could be cognized by a human being in a learned manner, but which is not great enough to be worth such a cognition."},{"o":[284,613],"en":"Just as one must not without sufficient ground pass off a thing as something that is lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition, so one must also not trouble oneself to cognize in a learned manner such things as are actually lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition."},{"o":[614,755],"en":"Otherwise one makes oneself ridiculous and contemptible, and neglects thereby the learned cognition of more important and more necessary things."}],"47":[{"o":[0,382],"en":"A thing is outside the horizon of learned human cognition (res extra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita) which indeed could be cognized by a human being in a learned manner, which is also not unworthy of a human learned cognition, but whose learned cognition would hinder a human being in his remaining occupations."},{"o":[383,770],"en":"No one must without sufficient ground pass off a thing as something that is found outside the horizon of his learned cognition; but also no one must strive after a learned cognition of such things as are located outside the horizon of his learned cognition, because otherwise he would meddle in foreign affairs, and neglect his own work thereby."}],"48":[{"o":[0,387],"en":"All those things which are neither elevated above the horizon of human learned cognition, nor lowered beneath it, nor found outside it, are located within the compass of the horizon of human learned cognition (res intra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita), and they constitute the learned horizon §.44."}],"49":[{"o":[0,174],"en":"The most extensive learned cognition of a human being consists in the learned cognition of all things that are located within the compass of his horizon §.48."},{"o":[175,416],"en":"25. Since now every scholar must attain the most extensive learned cognition that is possible for him; he must seek to measure out the limits and compass of his horizon as exactly as the weakness of human beings permits §.44."}],"50":[{"o":[0,204],"en":"And even if a human being should have attained the most extensive learned cognition, much necessary ignorance still remains, which redounds to him neither to honor nor to disgrace §.43."},{"o":[205,305],"en":"But as for voluntary ignorance, it is either praiseworthy or blameworthy."},{"o":[306,486],"en":"Praiseworthy ignorance (ignorantia laudabilis) is the ignorance of such things as are found beneath and outside the horizon of learned cognition §.46.47."}],"51":[{"o":[0,173],"en":"Blameworthy ignorance (ignorantia illaudabilis) is the ignorance of such things as are found within the horizon of learned cognition §.48."},{"o":[174,347],"en":"Whoever therefore wants to attain the most extensive learned cognition must seek to avoid all blameworthy ignorance, and no other ignorance besides §.49."}],"52":[{"o":[0,300],"en":"It is a ridiculous folly of many paltry minds among the learned when they credit their blameworthy ignorance to themselves as a merit, and, with Socrates, who possessed a very extensive learnedness, profess of themselves: that they know nothing, except that they know nothing."}],"53":[{"o":[0,105],"en":"The more things someone cognizes in a learned manner, the more extensive is his learned cognition."},{"o":[106,255],"en":"A higher or noticeable, and consequently rarer, degree of the extensiveness of learned cognition is called polymathy (polyhistoria)."},{"o":[256,563],"en":"It is undeniably a great perfection of learned cognition, provided one is not misled by greed for polyhistory 1) to transgress the limits of one's learned horizon, and 2) to neglect the remaining perfections of learned cognition all too noticeably."}],"54":[{"o":[0,118],"en":"Whoever wants to make his learned cognition extensive enough must 1) learn many principal parts of learnedness."},{"o":[119,565],"en":"E.g. philosophy, theology, history, philology etc. 2) From each principal part many parts, e.g. from philosophy the doctrine of reason, metaphysics, physics, natural right etc. 3) Each of these parts he must again learn extensively, e.g. the doctrine of reason, and 4) of each single truth he must again seek to attain an extensive learned cognition, e.g. of the omniscience of God."}],"55":[{"o":[0,239],"en":"An extensive learned cognition is thorough and complete (completa cognitio erudita) when it suffices for its aims, or when we cognize in a learned manner as much as the whole aim of our learned cognition requires."},{"o":[240,549],"en":"Extensiveness without thoroughness is not perfect enough, and one must accordingly learn to distinguish the principal truths from the side matters in every object of learned cognition, so that through the learned cognition of the former one may attain a complete learnedness."}],"56":[{"o":[0,480],"en":"Whoever studies merely in a cavalier manner, in that he merely runs cursorily through an extensive outline of all parts of learnedness; goes through a very few parts of learnedness somewhat more extensively, but still quite briefly; and perhaps here and there investigates some particular matters because for some reason they especially please him: his learned cognition is a poor skeleton of learnedness, and deserves very little regard §.55."}],"57":[{"o":[0,273],"en":"Because it is impossible for a human being to attain a thoroughly extensive learned cognition all at once; one must constantly seek to extend learned cognition: so that one does not go backward in it by failing to go further forward."}],"58":[{"o":[0,339],"en":"Because, through the extension of learned cognition, the powers of cognition are at the same time made more capable; one need not fear that one will overload one's head through the constant extension of learned cognition, provided one does not in this occupation transgress the limits of the learned horizon."}],"59":[{"o":[0,316],"en":"Although art is long and human life short, this consideration must rather incite us to learn as much as is possible with proper dispatch, than that it should instill in us a timidity and discouragement that hinders us from the extension of learned cognition."}],"60":[{"o":[0,233],"en":"So that one may not regard the extension of learned cognition as unnecessary and useless, one must not set before oneself too small, trifling and base a purpose, which one strives to reach through one's learned cognition."}],"61":[{"o":[0,206],"en":"Since it is naturally necessary that we human beings forget much; for precisely this reason one must extend learned cognition very much, so that one may forget much and nonetheless still retain much."},{"o":[207,341],"en":"The truths that we forget have in any case not been wholly useless, because they have increased the degree of our powers of cognition."}],"62":[{"o":[0,473],"en":"The extensiveness of learned cognition arises from a twofold source, both of which must be together: 1) from the extensiveness and rich content of the object, when it contains much in itself that can be cognized by a human being in a learned manner (vastitas obiectiva); and 2) from the extension of the powers of cognition (vastitas subiectiva), by virtue of which one is in a position to cognize much of a thing in a learned manner."}],"63":[{"o":[0,295],"en":"Because it is impossible for a human being to learn all parts of learnedness in an equal degree of perfection; each must pick out one of them, with which he occupies himself most, and of which he strives to attain the most perfect learned cognition."},{"o":[296,364],"en":"This is his principal science (scientia eruditi principalis)."},{"o":[365,658],"en":"Each must choose as his principal science that part of learnedness 1) which can be cognized by human beings in a very high degree of perfection, and 2) for which he possesses the most skill, rational inclination, and other means of furtherance."}],"64":[{"o":[0,186],"en":"Each must 1) learn his principal science most extensively and thoroughly, and 2) investigate all other parts of learnedness in relation to his principal science."},{"o":[187,361],"en":"The more closely another part of learnedness is connected with the principal science, the more perfectly and extensively must one seek to cognize it in a learned manner §.63."}],"65":[{"o":[0,608],"en":"The paltriness of learned cognition causes, among others, a threefold harm: 1) Learned pedantry and charlatanry (pedantismus et charlataneria eruditorum), by virtue of which one values far too highly the little that one understands, and despises everything else entirely; 2) a ridiculous conceit and a vain arrogance; and 3) little learnedness can make a human being unhappy for his whole life, in that he can fall into just such circumstances in which that which he has learned is not demanded of him, and that which he has not learned is expected of him."},{"o":[608,649],"en":"Of the magnitude of learned cognition."}],"66":[{"o":[0,158],"en":"The magnitude of learned cognition requires not only a great object, but the cognition must also be great enough for the object §.26."},{"o":[159,273],"en":"The greater the object, and the more proportioned the cognition, the greater is the learned cognition."}],"67":[{"o":[0,219],"en":"The object of learned cognition is great considered in itself (magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva absoluta) when it contains much manifold in itself that can be cognized in a learned manner."},{"o":[220,276],"en":"For example: God, philosophy, history etc."}],"68":[{"o":[0,394],"en":"The object of learned cognition is great with respect to its consequences (magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva respectiva), 1) when it is important (res digna, gravis), that is, when it has great consequences, e.g. when the happiness of human beings, the welfare of the fatherland etc. rests on it; 2) when it is fruitful (res foecunda), that is, when many consequences flow from it."},{"o":[395,454],"en":"E.g. godliness, for it is profitable for all things."}],"69":[{"o":[0,146],"en":"Whoever wants to make his learned cognition truly great must seek to cognize nothing but absolutely great, important and fruitful things."},{"o":[147,449],"en":"The more the object contains in itself, the greater its consequences are, and the more consequences it has, the greater, more important and more fruitful it is; and the greater, more important and more fruitful is the learned cognition of it, insofar as one considers it, namely, with respect to its object."}],"70":[{"o":[0,186],"en":"The magnitude of learned cognition that belongs to it itself (magnitudo cognitionis eruditae subiectiva) consists in this, when it is proportioned to the magnitude of its object §.66."},{"o":[187,413],"en":"The greater the object is, the more extensive, correct, distinct, certain and practical the learned cognition of it must be, and the more time and diligence must be expended on the attainment of it."},{"o":[414,595],"en":"The less great the object is, the less perfect the learned cognition must be, and the less time and diligence must be expended on the attainment of it."}],"71":[{"o":[0,322],"en":"The imperfection of learned cognition that is opposed to its magnitude is called the smallness of it (parvitas, vilitas cognitionis eruditae); and it arises either from the smallness of the object, or from the learned cognition's not being proportioned to the object §.66."}],"72":[{"o":[0,206],"en":"The object of learned cognition is small, considered in itself (parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva absoluta), when it contains little in itself that can be cognized in a learned manner."},{"o":[207,255],"en":"E.g. the hairpins of Roman womenfolk."}],"73":[{"o":[0,433],"en":"The object of learned cognition is small with respect to its consequences (parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva respectiva), 1) when it is not important (res leviores), that is, when it has no great consequences, e.g. the doctrine of the composition of bodies from monads; 2) when it is unfruitful (res infoecunda, sterilis), that is, when it does not have many consequences, e.g. the doctrine of the incorporeality of the soul."}],"74":[{"o":[0,50],"en":"When a thing is small in all respects §.72."},{"o":[51,161],"en":"73, it is worth no learned cognition: for it is lowered beneath the horizon of it §.46."},{"o":[162,264],"en":"The less a thing contains in itself, the fewer and smaller consequences it has, the smaller it is."},{"o":[265,500],"en":"Among these trifles one must also count the vulgar and base things (res plebeiae, abiectae), the learned investigation of which would even be contrary to honorable morals, e.g. the outbreaks of vices among the rabble."}],"75":[{"o":[0,183],"en":"The smallness of learned cognition that belongs to it itself (parvitas cognitionis eruditae subiectiva) consists in this, when it is not proportioned to the objects §.71."},{"o":[184,481],"en":"Consequently 1) when one seeks a more perfect learned cognition with more effort, and expends more time on it, than the object deserves; and 2) when one does not cognize an object as perfectly, does not investigate it with as much effort, and does not expend as much time on it as it deserves."},{"o":[482,615],"en":"E.g. whoever assails errors more learnedly and diligently than vices, his learned cognition is not proportioned enough."}],"76":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"Whoever wants to make his learned cognition truly perfect must 1) if it is possible for him, choose for his principal science the greatest §.67."},{"o":[145,519],"en":"68 part of learnedness §.63; 2) the greater a part of learnedness is, the more effort and diligence he must expend on it, and the more perfect the learned cognition of it must be; 3) the greater the truths are, the more effort and time he must expend on them, and the more perfect his learned cognition of them must be."}],"77":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"Whoever possesses no mature and manly judgment cannot possibly judge of the true worth of things, and it is therefore impossible for him to attain the magnitude of learned cognition."}],"78":[{"o":[0,149],"en":"Because all objects of learned cognition stand in a universal connection, they are all infinitely great, important and fruitful §.67."},{"o":[150,321],"en":"68. An object is therefore called a trifle only in relation to us, because it is impossible for us to cognize its magnitude, importance and fruitfulness in a learned manner."}],"79":[{"o":[0,301],"en":"Just as a learned cognition together with its object is not to be esteemed great because this or that small mind makes a great fuss about it; so one must also not hold it small because it is passed off by the same as a thing of little value."}],"80":[{"o":[0,220],"en":"A learned cognition is not a trifle of little value and unfruitful because this or that dry and unfruitful head is not able to derive many and important consequences from it."},{"o":[221,289],"en":"The unfruitfulness of the soil is not to be imputed to the plant."}],"81":[{"o":[0,278],"en":"The application of a great learned cognition to small, ridiculous, vulgar and contemptible cases can indeed accidentally make it ridiculous, contemptible and vulgar; but for that reason it must not be counted among the trifles of little value."}],"82":[{"o":[0,249],"en":"When the great objects of learned cognition are represented and presented by this or that person in a contemptible and ridiculous manner; for that reason they must not be counted among the contemptible trifles."}],"83":[{"o":[0,197],"en":"A scholar must not, through his own fault, through wretched application, and through ridiculous representations and expressions, make learned cognition ridiculous and contemptible §.81."},{"o":[198,201],"en":"82."}],"84":[{"o":[0,244],"en":"If a learned cognition should not be important and fruitful in our present circumstances, because we do not yet see its consequences; it can nevertheless become important and fruitful in the future, and it is therefore not a trifle on that account."},{"o":[245,333],"en":"We must also plant trees for our descendants, whose fruits we do not enjoy."}],"85":[{"o":[0,205],"en":"One disgraces oneself if one does not hold abstract cognition, subtleties and profound distinctions to be great and important things, because they require much laborious reflection."}],"86":[{"o":[0,271],"en":"The intermediate truths in an extensive doctrinal edifice indeed do not signify much in themselves; but because without them one cannot properly cognize the principal truths in a learned manner, they are for that reason great and worthy objects of our learned cognition."}],"87":[{"o":[0,209],"en":"A learned investigation is not important because it requires much effort, diligence and time, and because it cannot be undertaken without great learnedness: for there are also very difficult farces."}],"88":[{"o":[0,130],"en":"A part of learnedness is not to be counted among the trifles because it is filled with many trifles."}],"89":[{"o":[0,179],"en":"A learned cognition that is not great in one respect can be great in another respect, and it must therefore not be held to be a trifle §.67."},{"o":[180,251],"en":"68. E.g. a cognition can be not important, but nevertheless fruitful."}],"90":[{"o":[0,200],"en":"Because different scholars can have chosen different principal sciences §.63, a learned investigation can be great with respect to the one, and small with respect to the other."}],"91":[{"o":[0,88],"en":"A great mind possesses the skill to have only a great learned cognition."},{"o":[89,399],"en":"His inclination toward it and his aversion to all trifles compel him always first to investigate the magnitude of the thing before he troubles himself to cognize it in a learned manner, so that he may know whether it is worth a learned cognition, and of what degree of perfection of it."},{"o":[399,442],"en":"Of the truth of learned cognition."}],"92":[{"o":[0,153],"en":"A false or incorrect cognition (cognitio falsa) is a cognition that is no cognition and yet seems to be a cognition."},{"o":[154,353],"en":"A false learned cognition (cognitio erudita falsa) only seems to be a learned cognition, and it is either no cognition at all, or at least no learned cognition."},{"o":[354,522],"en":"E.g. those who assume the vegetative soul of plants, and derive the growth of plants from it, have a false learned cognition."}],"93":[{"o":[0,134],"en":"A true or correct cognition (cognitio vera) not only seems to be a cognition, but it is one in fact."},{"o":[135,296],"en":"A true learned cognition (cognitio erudita vera) not only seems to be a learned cognition, but it also deserves this name in fact."},{"o":[297,420],"en":"E.g. whoever demonstrates convincingly the actuality of God from the contingency of this world has a true learned cognition."}],"94":[{"o":[0,215],"en":"The marks of the correctness and incorrectness of cognition (criteria veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis) are the grounds from which it can be cognized that a cognition is true, or that it is false."},{"o":[216,288],"en":"And they are either present in the cognition itself, or not."},{"o":[289,455],"en":"The former are the internal, and the latter the external marks of correctness and incorrectness (criteria interna et externa veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis)."}],"95":[{"o":[0,282],"en":"The first internal mark of the truth of a cognition consists in its inner possibility (possibilitas cognitionis interna), insofar as it represents something possible, and contains nothing that is contrary to one another, even if one considers it quite alone."},{"o":[283,523],"en":"The internal impossibility of cognition (impossibilitas cognitionis interna), when it represents nothing, and when the manifold in it conflicts with one another, is therefore the first internal mark that it is false §.94.93."},{"o":[524,527],"en":"92."}],"96":[{"o":[0,163],"en":"The second internal mark of the truth of a cognition consists in this, when it is possible in a connection (possibilitas cognitionis hypothetica)."},{"o":[164,258],"en":"Consequently 1) when it is a consequence of correct grounds, and 2) a ground of correct consequences, §.94."},{"o":[259,386],"en":"93. A cognition is accordingly true when it is not impossible, and is in accordance with the principle of sufficient reason §.16."}],"97":[{"o":[0,179],"en":"A cognition is false if it is impossible in connection (impossibilitas in nexu); consequently if it has no grounds or false grounds, and no consequences or false consequences §.96."},{"o":[180,254],"en":"And this is the other inner mark of its incorrectness §.94."}],"98":[{"o":[0,410],"en":"We must not assume: 1) that a cognition is true because we do not become aware of any inner impossibility in it; 2) that it is false because we do not become aware of any inner possibility in it; 3) that a cognition is true whose groundlessness and false grounds and consequences we do not become aware of; 4) that a cognition is false of which we recognize no correct grounds and consequences."},{"o":[411,451],"en":"For we human beings are not omniscient."}],"99":[{"o":[0,115],"en":"If we represent a thing to ourselves otherwise than it is, we believe that we cognize it, and yet do not cognize it."},{"o":[116,161],"en":"Our cognition is therefore false §.92."},{"o":[162,252],"en":"Moreover, all possible things have an inner possibility, grounds and consequences §.15.16."},{"o":[253,530],"en":"Logical truth of cognition (veritas cognitionis logica) therefore consists in its agreement with its object, and its logical incorrectness (falsitas cognitionis logica) in its not agreeing with its object §.95."},{"o":[531,538],"en":"96. 97."}],"100":[{"o":[0,165],"en":"If a cognition contains nothing true, it is wholly false (falsitas totalis), and if it contains nothing false, it is wholly true (veritas totalis)."},{"o":[166,275],"en":"But it can be true and false at once, though in different respects (veritas et falsitas partialis)."},{"o":[276,519],"en":"One must therefore not accept an extensive cognition wholly, because perhaps the greatest and most important part of it is true; and not reject it wholly, because perhaps the greatest and most important part of it is false."},{"o":[520,574],"en":"For example, the doctrinal systems of various religions."}],"101":[{"o":[0,235],"en":"The more numerous and manifold the parts a cognition contains in itself that are possible together, the greater these parts are, and the more numerous and greater the correct grounds and consequences it has, the more correct the cognition is §.95."},{"o":[236,379],"en":"96. Whoever, then, wants to improve his learned cognition as far as possible must seek to attain in it the greatest possible degree of truth."}],"102":[{"o":[0,210],"en":"A cognition that is correct to a higher degree is called an exact cognition (cognitio exacta, exasciata); but one that is true to a lesser degree is called a coarse one (cognitio crassa)."},{"o":[211,356],"en":"All coarse cognition must be avoided, and indeed all the more, the coarser it is, or the more and the more important falsehood it contains."},{"o":[357,534],"en":"Conversely, perfect learned cognition must be as exact as possible §.101, and the greater the objects are, the more exactly one must seek to cognize them §.70."},{"o":[535,615],"en":"The smaller they are, however, the less correct their cognition need be §.70."}],"103":[{"o":[0,354],"en":"Learned cognition can be false in a threefold way: 1) when the cognition of the things is false, although the cognition of the grounds is correct; 2) when the cognition of the grounds is false, although the cognition of the things is correct; 3) when the representation of the connection between true grounds and consequences is incorrect §.100."},{"o":[355,499],"en":"A true learned cognition must therefore be a correct cognition of the things, of the grounds, and of their connection at the same time §.21."}],"104":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"By truths (veritates) one also understands true cognition itself, and then all truths are either dogmatic (veritates dogmaticae) or historical (veritates historicae)."},{"o":[195,315],"en":"The former can and must be cognized from the inner marks of truth, the latter only from the outer ones."},{"o":[316,445],"en":"E.g., that there is a GOD is a dogmatic truth; but that David was the second king of the Israelites is a historical one."},{"o":[446,657],"en":"A doctrinal system (systema) is a multitude of dogmatic truths that are connected with one another in such a way that, taken together, they make up one cognition that one can consider as a whole."}],"105":[{"o":[0,130],"en":"The more truths occur in a doctrinal system, and the greater and more correct they are, the more perfect it is §.104."},{"o":[131,435],"en":"For the most exact truth of a doctrinal system it is required: 1) that all its parts are most exactly correct; 2) that none contradicts another; and 3) that they are all connected, in that each is either a ground of the rest, or a consequence, or both at the same time §.95."},{"o":[436,498],"en":"96. Since there is no truth outside doctrinal systems §.96."},{"o":[499,601],"en":"104, learned cognition must be systematic if it is to be perfectly correct at all."}],"106":[{"o":[0,564],"en":"All truths are either merely aesthetic truths (veritates mere aestheticae), which must be cognized merely beautifully, and with these learned cognition must never occupy itself; or merely learned (veritates mere eruditae), which can only be cognized in a learned way, and with these alone learned cognition must not occupy itself, for it would otherwise be merely learned §.32; or both at once (veritates aesthetico-eruditae), and these are the foremost object of a truly perfect learned cognition."}],"107":[{"o":[0,227],"en":"Since truth is not the only perfection of learned cognition, a scholar can never be excused if he occupies himself with a learned investigation merely because it is true."},{"o":[228,285],"en":"It is not good to think and to say all truths."}],"108":[{"o":[0,128],"en":"Everyone must seek to cognize his principal science most exactly §.102, and in the highest possible degree systematically §.105 §.63."}],"109":[{"o":[0,128],"en":"Error (cognitio erronea, error) consists in our holding false cognition to be true, and true cognition to be false."},{"o":[129,343],"en":"Consequently 1) every erroneous cognition is false §.99; 2) not every false cognition is erroneous, namely when we recognize that it is false §.99; 3) error arises from false cognition."},{"o":[344,428],"en":"If we had no false cognition at all, we could also have no errors."},{"o":[429,524],"en":"Error is worse than merely false cognition, for it is a hidden poison."},{"o":[525,604],"en":"Learned cognition can therefore be erroneous in a threefold way §.103."}],"110":[{"o":[0,82],"en":"If we transgress the rules of the 98th paragraph, error arises §.109."},{"o":[83,268],"en":"The first source of all errors is therefore ignorance §.41, when it is joined by precipitancy, by virtue of which we deny that of which we have no cognition."}],"111":[{"o":[0,109],"en":"Error is either an avoidable one (error vincibilis) or an unavoidable one (error invincibilis)."},{"o":[110,205],"en":"The former arises from an avoidable, the latter from an unavoidable ignorance §.43."},{"o":[206,328],"en":"The former is only a blameworthy stain on learned cognition, but the latter can and may not be avoided."}],"112":[{"o":[0,131],"en":"The more extensive, important and fruitful an error is, and the more easily it could have been prevented, the greater it is."},{"o":[132,258],"en":"The greater an error is, the more it disgraces learned cognition, and the more carefully it must be prevented."},{"o":[259,361],"en":"Consequently everyone must seek especially to prevent the errors in his principal science §.108."}],"113":[{"o":[0,169],"en":"A cognition is manifestly false (cognitio aperte falsa) if its incorrectness is discovered merely by considering it, e.g., a square triangle."},{"o":[170,363],"en":"But if one must undertake a more extensive investigation in order to discover its incorrectness, it is falsely concealed (cognitio cuius falsitas latet), e.g., matter can think."},{"o":[364,533],"en":"An error by which a manifestly false cognition is accepted as true is an absurd, nonsensical and stupid error (cognitio absurda, absona)."},{"o":[534,576],"en":"Not all errors are absurdities."}],"114":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"All truths are either absolutely necessary (veritates absolute necessariae) or contingent truths (veritates contingentes)."},{"o":[145,229],"en":"In the former it is wholly impossible that they should be false, e.g., there is a GOD."},{"o":[230,296],"en":"The latter, however, could also be false, e.g., this world is actual."},{"o":[297,420],"en":"Since error is easier to avoid in the former, it is greater than error in contingent truths §.112."},{"o":[420,463],"en":"On the clarity of learned cognition."}],"115":[{"o":[0,308],"en":"A mark, a characteristic of cognition and of things (nota, character cognitionis et rei) is that in the cognition or in the things which, when it is cognized, is the ground why we are conscious of them; or they are the distinguishing parts of cognition and of its objects."},{"o":[309,370],"en":"Where there is consciousness, therefore, marks are cognized §.13."},{"o":[371,468],"en":"E.g., reason is a mark of the human being, and of the cognition we have of him."}],"116":[{"o":[0,48],"en":"Marks in turn have their marks §.115."},{"o":[49,192],"en":"Consequently all marks of a thing are either immediate marks (notae immediatae, proximae) or mediate ones (notae mediatae, remotae)."},{"o":[193,312],"en":"The latter are marks of marks, the former only marks of the thing, though they are not marks of its marks."},{"o":[313,521],"en":"E.g., reason is an immediate mark of the human being, but because reason is a faculty of distinctly understanding the connection of things, the faculty is a mediate mark of the human being."},{"o":[522,583],"en":"Consciousness rests on the immediate marks §.13."}],"117":[{"o":[0,103],"en":"Marks are either negative (notae negativae) or affirmative (notae affirmativae, positivae)."},{"o":[104,263],"en":"Through the former we represent something to ourselves as absent in the thing, and thereby we cognize only what it is not; e.g., the unreason of unreasoning animals."},{"o":[264,409],"en":"Through the latter we represent something to ourselves as present in the thing, and through them we cognize what the thing is, e.g., the reason of human beings."},{"o":[410,533],"en":"Although both kinds can cause consciousness, the affirmative are nevertheless better marks than the negative."}],"118":[{"o":[0,98],"en":"Marks are either more weighty (notae graviores) or lesser marks (notae leviores)."},{"o":[99,179],"en":"The former reveal a greater, the latter a lesser difference of the thing."},{"o":[180,266],"en":"Either fruitful (notae foecundae) or unfruitful marks (notae infoecundae)."},{"o":[267,349],"en":"The former reveal a manifold difference from many things, the latter do not."},{"o":[350,483],"en":"Reason is a weighty and fruitful mark of a human being; but that he is a thing is a lesser and more unfruitful one."},{"o":[484,581],"en":"The more weighty and fruitful the marks are, therefore, the more they promote consciousness."}],"119":[{"o":[0,124],"en":"Since marks are grounds §.115, they are either sufficient grounds of consciousness or insufficient grounds §.15."},{"o":[125,225],"en":"The former are sufficient (notae sufficientes), and the latter insufficient marks (notae insufficientes)."},{"o":[226,263],"en":"The former are better marks than the latter."},{"o":[264,377],"en":"The faculty of thinking is an insufficient mark of a spirit; but that it has understanding, a sufficient one."}],"120":[{"o":[0,198],"en":"Marks are either absolutely necessary and unchangeable (notae absolute necessariae et invariabiles) or contingent and changeable marks (notae contingentes et variabiles)."},{"o":[199,444],"en":"The former are constituted so that without them the thing cannot be represented, e.g., the reason of the human being; the latter, however, are constituted so that without them the thing can nevertheless be represented, e.g., the actual thinking of the human being."},{"o":[445,482],"en":"The former are better marks than the latter."}],"121":[{"o":[0,145],"en":"Marks can be represented in a thing either without considering it in connection with other things outside it, or not."},{"o":[146,261],"en":"The latter are outer marks or relations (notae externae, relationes), e.g., the dominion of a human being."},{"o":[262,309],"en":"The former are inner marks (notae internae)."},{"o":[310,368],"en":"The latter are either necessary or contingent §.120."},{"o":[369,456],"en":"The contingent ones are called contingent constitutions (modi), e.g., the learning of a human being."},{"o":[457,526],"en":"The necessary ones are either the grounds of all the remaining determinations, or not."},{"o":[527,616],"en":"The latter are the attributes (attributa), e.g., the faculty of thinking in a human being."},{"o":[617,700],"en":"The former are called the essential parts (essentialia), e.g., the reason of the human being."},{"o":[701,766],"en":"The sum total of all essential parts is the essence (essentia)."}],"122":[{"o":[0,176],"en":"The more marks we cognize of a thing, the greater these marks are, and the more perfectly we cognize the marks, the greater and better the consciousness is §.13."},{"o":[177,370],"en":"Consequently the affirmative, weighty, fruitful, inner, necessary and sufficient marks cause a greater and better consciousness than those opposed to them §.117-121."}],"123":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"A representation, insofar as we are conscious of it, is called a thought (cogitatio)."},{"o":[101,170],"en":"Consequently not every representation and cognition is a thought."},{"o":[171,247],"en":"And what has been proved of consciousness also holds of thinking §.115.116."},{"o":[248,252],"en":"122."}],"124":[{"o":[0,101],"en":"A cognition contains either as many marks as are required for consciousness, or not."},{"o":[102,247],"en":"The former is a clear cognition (cognitio clara), which is one and the same with the thought, and with the cognition of which we are conscious §.123."},{"o":[248,381],"en":"The latter is an obscure cognition (cognitio obscura), which is therefore neither a thought nor connected with consciousness §.123."},{"o":[382,414],"en":"The former is more perfect than the latter."},{"o":[415,585],"en":"When we cannot recall a word, and yet it seems as if it were running around on the tip of our tongue, then we have an obscure representation of it."}],"125":[{"o":[0,138],"en":"Obscure cognition is either absolutely obscure (cognitio absolute obscura) or relatively so (cognitio relative obscura)."},{"o":[139,226],"en":"The former would have to be so obscure that it would be absolutely impossible to make it clear."},{"o":[227,341],"en":"No true cognition is absolutely obscure, and one must therefore hold nothing to be absolutely obscure."},{"o":[342,507],"en":"All true obscure cognition is only relatively obscure, that is, the powers of this or that thinking being are not sufficient to make it clear."},{"o":[508,637],"en":"And then either the object is chiefly to blame for this obscurity, or the human being to whom the cognition is obscure."},{"o":[638,817],"en":"The former is the obscurity of the things (cognitio obiective obscura), and the latter the obscurity in the head of him to whom the cognition is so obscure (cognitio subiective obscura)."},{"o":[818,933],"en":"E.g., things that are too far removed from us, or too small, are to blame that we sense them obscurely."},{"o":[934,1001],"en":"But whoever finds logic obscure is himself to blame for it."},{"o":[1002,1259],"en":"Finally, an obscure cognition is either wholly obscure (cognitio totaliter obscura), when we are not at all conscious of it, or obscure only in part (cognitio partialiter obscura), when we are conscious and also not conscious of it at the same time."},{"o":[1260,1393],"en":"All our clear cognition is always partly obscure to us, because we are not capable of thoroughly thinking through a single thing completely."}],"126":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"One human being can cognize what another does not cognize, and one can therefore cognize marks that are unknown to the other §.115."},{"o":[141,221],"en":"Consequently one can cognize clearly what the other cognizes only obscurely §.124."},{"o":[222,489],"en":"One must therefore not infer: 1) what is clear to me is also clear to others; 2) what is obscure to me is also obscure to others; 3) what is clear to me now will also be clear to me in the future; 4) what is obscure to me now will also remain obscure to me in the future."}],"127":[{"o":[0,224],"en":"The more marks that are required for consciousness are unknown to us, and the greater the marks that are unknown to us, and the more force is required to make a cognition clear, the greater its obscurity is."},{"o":[225,343],"en":"Before a cognition can become clear, therefore, one must often expend much time and effort to diminish its obscurity."}],"128":[{"o":[0,269],"en":"Because we have much clear cognition whose marks we do cognize §.115, but not clearly, there are actually obscure representations present in our soul, which make up the materials from which the soul gradually composes its clear cognition."}],"129":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"The obscurity of cognition arises from a threefold source: 1) when a human being does not possess sufficient powers to make it clear."},{"o":[145,745],"en":"This lack of powers is either necessary or not, and in the latter case the human being should either have acquired the powers or not; 2) from the lack of attention, which arises either from a necessary lack of powers, or because we may not pay attention to a certain cognition, or because we do not pay attention to it although we should; 3) from ignorance of such things without which a certain cognition cannot become clear, whether this ignorance be necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy §.43.50."},{"o":[746,861],"en":"51. All obscurity of cognition is therefore either necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy."},{"o":[862,1114],"en":"Consequently, in the improvement of cognition one must seek to avoid only the contingent and blameworthy obscurity; and the obscurity that is necessary and praiseworthy in one person can be contingent and blameworthy in another."}],"130":[{"o":[0,82],"en":"Obscure cognition 1) can be true, but it can also be false §.92.93."},{"o":[83,203],"en":"Not every obscure cognition is therefore false; 2) is no learned cognition, insofar as it is obscure."},{"o":[204,279],"en":"It is an unavoidable evil in the learned cognition of human beings."}],"131":[{"o":[0,337],"en":"The action by which the obscurity of cognition is diminished, and the clarity of cognition is produced and increased, is called the unfolding or development of cognition (evolutio, explanatio cognitionis), just as the opposed action is called its enfolding (cognitionis involutio)."},{"o":[338,547],"en":"For the former, a) three things are required beforehand: 1) one investigates as far as possible whether the obscurity of the cognition one wants to develop is necessary or contingent, unculpable or culpable."},{"o":[548,607],"en":"In the latter case we may only venture the development."},{"o":[608,719],"en":"2) One investigates whether the development of it belongs to the horizon of our clear cognition or not."},{"o":[720,773],"en":"In the former case this work is alone permitted to us."},{"o":[774,907],"en":"3) One investigates whether this development does not presuppose some other clear cognition, without which it cannot take place."},{"o":[908,1428],"en":"And if this matter is in fact so, one must not undertake this work until we have obtained this other clear cognition §.129. b) For the unfolding itself, three things are required: 1) one directs one's attention to the thing one wants to cognize clearly; 2) one compares it with other things different from it, so that one may cognize its marks §.115; 3) one abstracts from all other things, or one obscures them, in that one pays attention to the object."},{"o":[1429,1727],"en":"The more often, more strongly and longer one pays attention to a thing, the more things one compares it with, the more strongly one abstracts from other things, the better and sooner the cognition of it develops, even if one should at first perform this work in vain several times."}],"132":[{"o":[0,171],"en":"Through a clear cognition we are either capable of distinguishing the object constantly and in all circumstances from all possible other things, or not."},{"o":[172,488],"en":"In the first case our cognition is completely clear (cognitio complete clara), e.g., the representation of the color red; in the second, however, incompletely clear (cognitio incomplete clara), e.g., when we do indeed taste that a wine is Rhine wine, but cannot taste of what sort it is."},{"o":[489,683],"en":"The former is more perfect than the latter, because the latter contains more obscurity than the former §.124, and we attain it through the cognition of the necessary, unchangeable and sufficient marks §.119."},{"o":[684,693],"en":"120. 121."}],"133":[{"o":[0,138],"en":"If we have a clear cognition, we are either conscious of everything that is found in the object, or not."},{"o":[139,214],"en":"If the first, the cognition is wholly clear (cognitio totaliter clara)."},{"o":[215,293],"en":"If the latter, it is clear only in part (cognitio partialiter clara)."},{"o":[294,440],"en":"No human clear cognition is wholly clear §.125, and a cognition can be completely clear although it is not wholly clear §.132."},{"o":[441,562],"en":"Meanwhile a cognition is all the more perfect, the more its clarity approaches total clarity §.124."}],"134":[{"o":[0,348],"en":"The more marks we cognize, consequently from the more numerous and more similar things we can distinguish a thing; the greater the marks, and the better we cognize all the marks; the more easily we can be conscious of a cognition and of its object: the clearer our cognition is §.124, and thus also the more perfect."},{"o":[349,460],"en":"In the improvement of a cognition, therefore, one must seek to attain the highest possible degree of its clarity."}],"135":[{"o":[0,218],"en":"A cognition that is clearer through the multitude of marks is called a lively cognition (cognitio extensive clarior, vivida); e.g., the melodious song of the nightingale runs resounding through the valleys."},{"o":[219,362],"en":"But one that is clearer through the greatness of the marks and their clearer representation is clearer in intensity (cognitio intensive clarior)."},{"o":[363,443],"en":"Only logic occupies itself with the latter degree of clarity §.1."},{"o":[444,451],"en":"17. 21."}],"136":[{"o":[0,177],"en":"If we forget marks of a clear cognition, a clearer cognition can be changed into a less clear one, and finally into a wholly obscure cognition §.124."},{"o":[178,286],"en":"Whoever wants to prevent the enfolding of clear cognition §.131 must therefore prevent forgetting."}],"137":[{"o":[0,124],"en":"A cognition considered with regard to the intensity of clarity is either distinct or confused §.14."},{"o":[125,195],"en":"135. In the former the marks are also clear, in the latter, however, obscure §.14."},{"o":[196,333],"en":"115. 124. Since distinctness is therefore a manifold clarity, distinct cognition is more perfect than confused §.124."}],"138":[{"o":[0,49],"en":"Since all learned cognition is distinct §.21."},{"o":[50,139],"en":"17, confused cognition, insofar as it is confused, is not learned §.137."},{"o":[140,364],"en":"Just as the beautiful sciences therefore occupy themselves with improving the confused half of human cognition, so a scholar must improve its distinct half through logic."},{"o":[365,517],"en":"Obscurity and confusion are a necessary evil in learned cognition, which one either also seeks to improve incidentally or not."},{"o":[518,655],"en":"In the latter case a merely learned cognition arises, and in the former a learned cognition that is at the same time beautiful §.24."}],"139":[{"o":[0,186],"en":"The action by which a certain degree of distinctness is produced in our cognition is called the resolution of cognition (resolutio, analysis, anatomia cognitionis)."},{"o":[187,324],"en":"A cognition can be resolved if it can be made distinct by some thinking being (cognitio resolubilis)."},{"o":[325,471],"en":"Insofar as it cannot become distinct, however, it is a cognition that cannot be resolved (cognitio irresolubilis)."},{"o":[472,641],"en":"And then it is either wholly and utterly impossible that it become distinct, or it cannot become distinct only through the powers of this or that thinking being."},{"o":[642,744],"en":"If the first, it cannot be resolved absolutely (cognitio absolute irresolubilis)."},{"o":[745,854],"en":"If the latter, it cannot be resolved only relatively (cognitio respective irresolubilis)."},{"o":[855,990],"en":"If a true cognition could not be resolved absolutely, its marks would have to be absolutely obscure §.137."},{"o":[991,1129],"en":"Since this is impossible §.120, all true cognition that cannot be resolved is so constituted only relatively."}],"140":[{"o":[0,108],"en":"Insofar as we have a distinct cognition of a thing, we comprehend it (concipere)."},{"o":[109,174],"en":"What can be cognized distinctly is comprehensible (conceptibile)."},{"o":[175,464],"en":"What cannot be cognized distinctly is incomprehensible (inconceptibile), either absolutely (absolute inconceptibile), if it cannot be resolved absolutely; or only relatively (relative inconceptibile), if it cannot be resolved relatively."},{"o":[465,607],"en":"All possible things are incomprehensible only relatively §.139, and what we cognize in a learned way, we comprehend always insofar §.21.17."}],"141":[{"o":[0,67],"en":"What we do not comprehend is not on that account an absurd thing §.140."},{"o":[68,449],"en":"And since a cognition can be distinct to one human being that is confused or even obscure to another §.126, one must not infer: what we do not comprehend, others do not comprehend either; what we comprehend, others comprehend; what I now comprehend, I shall also comprehend in the future; what I do not now comprehend, I shall also not comprehend in the future."},{"o":[450,543],"en":"These inferences hold also when the talk is of mere possibility or impossibility."}],"142":[{"o":[0,133],"en":"If one wants to resolve a cognition, one must 1) develop it §.131, if it should not already be clear in us beforehand."},{"o":[134,237],"en":"2) One must think through the clear cognition, or develop its marks gradually, according to §.131."},{"o":[238,326],"en":"The more marks, and the better marks §.116-121 one develops, the better it is."},{"o":[327,557],"en":"3) One reflects on the developed marks, or one represents them together as One cognition; otherwise we would think the object only piecemeal, and that is not yet a distinct cognition of the whole object."},{"o":[558,699],"en":"4) One represents the developed marks to oneself in the same connection and in the same order as they are found in the object."},{"o":[700,817],"en":"Otherwise the distinct cognition does not agree with the object, and all disorder puts us into confusion."},{"o":[818,1011],"en":"5) One must obscure, or abstract from, all other things and representations that have not been drawn into reflection, so that the cognition does not at once become confused again."}],"143":[{"o":[0,244],"en":"The more and greater clear marks we cognize of a thing, the better we cognize the marks, consequently the more extensive, proportioned, correct, clearer, certain and practical: the more distinct the cognition is §.137.25-30."}],"144":[{"o":[0,141],"en":"A distinct cognition is either wholly distinct (cognitio totaliter distincta) or distinct only in part (cognitio partialiter distincta)."},{"o":[142,223],"en":"The former contains no obscurity and confusion at all, the latter, however, contains such."},{"o":[224,288],"en":"No human distinct cognition is wholly distinct §.133."},{"o":[289,392],"en":"But it is all the more distinct, the nearer its distinctness comes to total distinctness § 142."}],"145":[{"o":[0,130],"en":"The confusion of cognition arises, 1) from the sources of obscurity, for it consists in the obscurity of the marks §.137."},{"o":[131,421],"en":"129. 2) From the necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy lack of reflection; likewise 3) of deliberation; 4) from the lack of order in thinking, and 5) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy lack of abstraction §.142."}],"146":[{"o":[0,274],"en":"There is therefore not only a confusion in all our cognition that we absolutely cannot prevent; but there is also a confusion that we could prevent, but we may not, because it is found beneath or outside our horizon §.146."},{"o":[275,426],"en":"Before we venture upon the resolution of a cognition, therefore, we must investigate beforehand as far as possible whether we can or may perform it."}],"147":[{"o":[0,58],"en":"The marks of a distinct cognition are clear §.137."},{"o":[59,112],"en":"They are therefore either distinct or confused §.137."},{"o":[113,315],"en":"In the first case we have an adequate cognition (cognitio adaequata), in the other, however, an inadequate one (cognitio inadaequata), e.g., vice is an acquired skill in sinning."},{"o":[316,473],"en":"An acquired skill is a facility in acting that we have obtained through practice, and we sin when we undertake evil free actions."},{"o":[474,639],"en":"This is an adequate cognition of vice, but it would be inadequate if I had no distinct cognition of skill and of sin."},{"o":[640,744],"en":"Adequacy is a manifold distinctness, and thus a perfection of cognition §.137."},{"o":[745,879],"en":"Inadequacy consists in the confusion of the cognition of the marks, and thus arises from the sources of confusion in general §.145."},{"o":[880,1001],"en":"The more distinct marks a cognition contains, and the more distinct they are, the more adequate the cognition is."},{"o":[1002,1087],"en":"And the most perfect learned cognition must be as adequate as possible §.21."}],"148":[{"o":[0,70],"en":"Adequacy arises from the resolution of the marks §.147."},{"o":[71,184],"en":"142. The more remote the marks are that we resolve in turn, the more adequate the cognition becomes §.116."},{"o":[185,288],"en":"147. A higher degree of adequacy is a profound cognition (cognitio profunda, purior)."},{"o":[289,447],"en":"And in resolving a cognition, we bring this business to an end when we discover the marks that we cannot resolve further."},{"o":[448,571],"en":"But we stop in the resolution when we do not resolve further marks that we nevertheless could resolve."},{"o":[572,901],"en":"Since it would be a loathsome labor if we wanted always to bring the resolution of all our distinct cognition to an end; we must always have a reasonable purpose for which we resolve a cognition, and as soon as we have attained it, we must interrupt this work for the time being."},{"o":[902,1005],"en":"It is in any case impossible for a human being to avoid all inadequacy in his cognition §.144."}],"149":[{"o":[0,147],"en":"Distinct cognition is either completely distinct (cognitio complete distincta) or incompletely so (cognitio incomplete distincta) §.132."},{"o":[148,186],"en":"The former is more perfect than the latter §.132."},{"o":[187,337],"en":"E.g., if I represent to myself a spirit as a being that can think, I have a distinct but incomplete cognition of it."},{"o":[338,481],"en":"But if I represent it to myself as a thing that has understanding, my distinct cognition of a spirit is at the same time complete."}],"150":[{"o":[0,287],"en":"If one wants to make a distinct cognition of a thing complete, one must 1) seek to discover those marks that are found outside it in no other thing; or 2) one must discover so many marks as, taken together, belong to no other thing."},{"o":[288,356],"en":"E.g., virtue is a skill of free lawful actions."},{"o":[357,519],"en":"Although each of these marks is also found in things that are not virtues, they are nevertheless, taken together, found in no other thing."},{"o":[520,659],"en":"And the sum total of these four marks therefore makes the distinct representation of virtue a completely distinct representation."}],"151":[{"o":[0,138],"en":"A distinct cognition is either a determinate cognition (cognitio determinata) or an indeterminate cognition (cognitio indeterminata)."},{"o":[139,266],"en":"The former is completely distinct, and contains not a single mark more than is absolutely necessary for completeness."},{"o":[267,318],"en":"E.g., a spirit is a being that has understanding."},{"o":[319,569],"en":"The latter contains either too few marks, and that is the incompletely distinct cognition §.149; or it contains more clear marks than are necessary for completeness, and that is an all too prolix cognition (cognitio nimis prolixa)."},{"o":[570,653],"en":"E.g., a spirit is a thinking being that possesses understanding and free will."},{"o":[654,877],"en":"If one therefore separates from a completely distinct cognition those marks that are either not necessary for completeness or that follow from other marks of the same cognition, it becomes determinate."},{"o":[878,1061],"en":"Determination is only a perfection of merely learned cognition §.24, when one wants to make the comprehension of a thing easier for the weak, profound understanding of the human being."}],"152":[{"o":[0,207],"en":"The most adequate cognition can gradually become less adequate, inadequate, confused, and finally wholly obscure, if, on account of forgetting, the clear cognition becomes obscure §.136."},{"o":[208,316],"en":"Whoever wants to prevent this loss of perfections must therefore seek to prevent forgetting."},{"o":[317,456],"en":"We human beings are never wholly secure in the possession of our perfections, and we must therefore be diligently on our guard everywhere."}],"153":[{"o":[0,168],"en":"Whoever wants to make learned cognition truly perfect with regard to its clarity must proportion the degree of clarity to the greatness of the objects §.70."},{"o":[169,353],"en":"Consequently, the more extensive and greater the object is, the more numerous, better and greater the kinds and degrees of clarity by which the learned cognition of it must be illuminated."},{"o":[354,440],"en":"In the opposite case one must also behave oppositely in this matter."}],"154":[{"o":[0,292],"en":"Every scholar must, if it is otherwise possible for him, choose for his principal science that part of learning which is capable of the most manifold and greatest clarity; and everyone must seek to attain in his principal science the very clearest cognition §.63."},{"o":[293,421],"en":"64. He must therefore be a bright head, or prefer the clearest truths, and constantly think as clearly as possible."},{"o":[422,481],"en":"A dark head scarcely deserves the name of a scholar."},{"o":[481,526],"en":"On the certainty of learned cognition."}],"155":[{"o":[0,126],"en":"Certainty (certitudo subiective spectata) is the consciousness of truth, or the clear cognition of truth §.29."},{"o":[127,317],"en":"If one wants to possess a certain learned cognition, therefore, it must be true according to the rules of the fourth section, and clear in the proper way according to the rules of the fifth section."}],"156":[{"o":[0,135],"en":"If we cognize clearly neither that something is true nor that it is false, our cognition of it is uncertain (incertitudo)."},{"o":[136,227],"en":"Uncertainty is therefore to be found only as an imperfection in our cognition."},{"o":[228,459],"en":"A cognition that is not uncertain to us is either certainly true (certo vera cognitio), when we are conscious of its truth, or certainly false (certo falsa cognitio), when we are conscious of its incorrectness §.155."}],"157":[{"o":[0,96],"en":"All certainty is either a distinct or a confused cognition of truth §.155."},{"o":[97,250],"en":"137. The latter is sensitive certainty (certitudo sensitiva), and when it is perfect to a higher degree, aesthetic (certitudo aesthetica)."},{"o":[251,428],"en":"The former is rational certainty (certitudo rationalis), and when it is perfect to a higher degree, it is called logical or learned (certitudo logica, erudita)."},{"o":[429,512],"en":"No human certain cognition can be merely rational and learned §.144."}],"158":[{"o":[0,290],"en":"Rational certainty is either an adequate certainty (certitudo adaequata), when we are in turn rationally certain of the marks of truth; or an inadequate one (certitudo inadaequata), when we are only sensitively certain of the marks of truth §.157."},{"o":[291,359],"en":"147. E.g., what thinks, is actual; I think, therefore I am actual."},{"o":[360,486],"en":"Because I have a distinct certainty of the first proposition, I am insofar adequately certain of the last proposition."},{"o":[487,606],"en":"But because the second proposition is only sensitively certain to me, the last proposition is insofar only inadequately certain to me."}],"159":[{"o":[0,138],"en":"Certainty is either a complete one (completa certitudo) or an incomplete certainty (incompleta certitudo) §.132.149."},{"o":[139,329],"en":"Determinate certainty (certitudo determinata) is a complete certainty that contains no more marks of truth than are indispensable for complete certainty §.151."}],"160":[{"o":[0,240],"en":"Through complete certainty the mind is always so calmed that it is raised above all reasonable fear of the opposite; incomplete certainty, however, cannot wholly drive out this reasonable fear §.159."},{"o":[241,372],"en":"Meanwhile a human being can often be freed from all fear of the opposite, and nevertheless not even be certain in general."}],"161":[{"o":[0,134],"en":"Determinate certainty, if it is moreover as adequate as possible, is mathematical certainty (certitudo mathematica)."}],"162":[{"o":[0,239],"en":"We have a total certainty of a thing (certitudo totalis) if everything we cognize of it is certain to us; but if not all of this is certain to us, the thing is certain to us only in part (certitudo partialis)."}],"163":[{"o":[0,501],"en":"A certain cognition is called, 1) convincing (cognitio convincens), insofar as it is completely certain, and the production of such a certain cognition is called conviction (convictio); 2) undeniable (cognitio evidens, indubitata), insofar as we cognize clearly that that of which we are convinced could not possibly be false; 3) thorough (cognitio solida), insofar as the certainty is adequate; or also when it is as great and of the kind as is required."}],"164":[{"o":[0,233],"en":"The more things we cognize with certainty, and the more we cognize with certainty of each object; the greater the things are that we cognize with certainty, and the more clearly we cognize the truth: the greater the certainty is §.155."},{"o":[234,554],"en":"Whoever wants to improve learned cognition properly must therefore make it as certain as is possible, in that he produces certainty of all kinds, and in each kind the greatest degree of certainty, as far as, namely, the remaining rules of the perfection of learned cognition permit."}],"165":[{"o":[0,107],"en":"We must not infer: 1) What is certain to me is also certain to other people, and conversely §.126."},{"o":[108,199],"en":"2) What is uncertain to me is, considered in itself, uncertain, or even false §.156."},{"o":[200,282],"en":"3) What is uncertain to me is also uncertain to other people, and conversely §.126."},{"o":[283,367],"en":"4) What is certain to me now will always remain certain to me in the future §.126."},{"o":[368,459],"en":"5) What is uncertain to me now will always remain uncertain to me in the future §.126."},{"o":[460,556],"en":"6) If not everything in an object is certain, it has no certainty at all §.162."}],"166":[{"o":[0,359],"en":"Because we attain some kinds of certainty, especially of logical certainty, only gradually, one must on the one hand not become vexed if certainty will not follow as quickly as we sometimes wish; on the other hand one must be distrustful if we have in some case become certain all too quickly, whether one may not have overlooked something."},{"o":[360,417],"en":"In the attainment of certainty one must hasten slowly."}],"167":[{"o":[0,171],"en":"If a cognition is not certain in the kind and to the degree that it could and should be, it is called a shallow cognition (cognitio superficiaria)."}],"168":[{"o":[0,286],"en":"We give a cognition our assent, or we accept it (assentiri, ponere aliquid), when we hold it to be true; we reject it (tollere aliquid), when we hold it to be false; and we withhold our assent (suspendere iudicium), when we do neither of the two."},{"o":[287,498],"en":"When we accept or reject an uncertain cognition, we do this either because we recognize some marks of correctness or incorrectness, or we recognize none of these marks at all."},{"o":[499,765],"en":"In the latter case we are precipitate (praecipitantia), and the uncertain cognition that we accept or reject out of precipitancy is a begged cognition, a prejudice, a preconceived opinion (praecaria cognitio, praeiudicium, praeconcepta opinio)."},{"o":[766,833],"en":"Begged cognition is no learned cognition at all §.21."},{"o":[834,979],"en":"17, and all prejudices can be avoided in learned cognition, and they are therefore an unjustifiable stain upon it."}],"169":[{"o":[0,212],"en":"Because the correctness and incorrectness of things does not depend on our insights, 1) that which we accept through a prejudice can be false, and that which we reject through a prejudice can be true."},{"o":[213,359],"en":"2) That which we accept through a prejudice can in fact be true, and that which we reject through a prejudice can actually be false."},{"o":[360,503],"en":"3) In every prejudice there is always something erroneous and false, because we believe ourselves to be certain when we are nevertheless not certain §.168."}],"170":[{"o":[0,174],"en":"A logical prejudice (praeiudicium logicum) is a prejudice by which the perfection of learned cognition, especially its thoroughness, is hindered."},{"o":[175,337],"en":"For example: a) the prejudice of excessively great confidence (praeiudicium nimiae confidentiae), when one hastily holds something to be logically perfect."},{"o":[338,558],"en":"1) The prejudice of excessively great authority (praeiudicium autoritatis), when we accept or reject something because a person accepts and rejects it whom we honor so highly that we hold him worthy of imitation."},{"o":[559,699],"en":"2) Logical egoism (egoismus logicus), when someone holds something to be logically perfect because he himself is its author."},{"o":[700,825],"en":"3) The prejudice of antiquity (praeiudicium antiquitatis), when we hold something to be true because it is an old opinion."},{"o":[826,946],"en":"4) The prejudice of novelty (praeiudicium novitatis), when we hold something to be true because it is a new opinion."},{"o":[947,1131],"en":"5) The prejudice of the accepted system of doctrine (praeiudicium systematis), when one accepts something merely because it conforms to our system of doctrine, and rejects it because it is contrary to it."},{"o":[1132,1321],"en":"6) The prejudice of lazy trust (praeiudicium pigritiae), when one believes that with less diligence one can get just as far in learned cognition as with greater."},{"o":[1322,1645],"en":"7) The prejudice of shallowness (praeiudicium corticis), when one believes that with a shallow cognition one can get just as far as with a thorough one. b) The prejudice of excessively great distrust (praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae), when one hastily holds something to be logically imperfect."},{"o":[1646,1749],"en":"1) The prejudice of antiquity (praeiudicium antiquitatis), when one rejects something because it is old."},{"o":[1750,1848],"en":"2) The prejudice of novelty (praeiudicium novitatis), when one rejects something because it is new."},{"o":[1849,1969],"en":"3) The prejudice of nationality (nazarethismus), when one rejects something because it stems from a certain people."},{"o":[1970,2141],"en":"4) The prejudice of the distrust which one places in oneself (praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae in se ipsum positae), when one trusts oneself too little, etc."}],"171":[{"o":[0,591],"en":"When we accept or reject an uncertain cognition on account of some marks of correctness and incorrectness, §.168, we either recognize more and stronger grounds for accepting it than for rejecting it, and then our cognition is probable (cognitio probabilis, verosimilis); or we recognize more and stronger grounds for rejecting it than for accepting it, and so we have an improbable cognition (cognitio improbabilis); or the grounds on both sides are equal to one another, and then it is a doubtful cognition (cognitio dubia)."},{"o":[592,706],"en":"E.g. It is probable that the planets are inhabited, but it is improbable that the sun is."},{"o":[707,804],"en":"As often as we cannot arrive at any resolution, so often we have a doubtful cognition."}],"172":[{"o":[0,330],"en":"Because human beings differ so infinitely from one another in their cognition, one can accept or reject through a prejudice a thing which another holds to be quite certainly true, a third quite certainly false, a fourth probable, a fifth improbable, and a sixth doubtful."},{"o":[331,365],"en":"The first always acts wrongly."},{"o":[366,435],"en":"The two following cannot possibly both be right or both be wrong."},{"o":[436,497],"en":"The last three, however, can all be right together §.159."},{"o":[498,507],"en":"168. 171."}],"173":[{"o":[0,166],"en":"Probability, improbability and doubtfulness are uncertainties §.171, and thus are not to be found in the objects of our cognition §.156."},{"o":[167,255],"en":"The most probable things can be false, and the most improbable true."}],"174":[{"o":[0,117],"en":"Prejudices one must hold neither true nor false §.168, just as with doubtful cognition §.171."},{"o":[118,279],"en":"Improbable cognition one must hold false, and probable cognition true, yet both under a constant fear of the opposite §.171."},{"o":[280,418],"en":"To exhaustively certain cognition we must give our assent with the greatest confidence, without fearing the opposite §.159."}],"175":[{"o":[0,290],"en":"The more and greater grounds we recognize for accepting something, the fewer and smaller grounds we recognize for rejecting it, the better we recognize the former and the worse the latter: the greater is the probability, and the greater the improbability of the opposite §.171."},{"o":[291,484],"en":"A degree of probability so great that it is as good in our regular conduct as an exhaustive certainty is called moral certainty (certitudo moralis)."}],"176":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"When we hold something to be true or false, any cognition of a ground for the opposite is called a doubt (dubium)."},{"o":[141,266],"en":"Doubts are cognized either in a merely obscure and indistinct way, or in a rational and learned manner."},{"o":[267,342],"en":"The former are called scruples (scrupulus), and the latter objections (obiectio)."}],"177":[{"o":[0,92],"en":"All doubts are either true doubts (dubium verum), or false ones (dubium falsum) §.176."},{"o":[93,168],"en":"92. 93, which, if they seem to be true, have a great semblance."},{"o":[169,328],"en":"When one clearly recognizes what is incorrect in a false doubt, the doubt is resolved or answered (dubium resolvitur, seu ad dubium respondetur)."},{"o":[329,509],"en":"All doubts are either answerable or unanswerable, and both either absolutely or relatively (dubium absolute et respective resolubile aut irresolubile)."},{"o":[510,837],"en":"Consequently 1) doubts can be raised against all truths, and nothing is false because doubts are raised against it; 2) against a truth doubts can be raised that are unanswerable to this or that person; 3) against no truth can absolutely unanswerable doubts be raised."},{"o":[837,1104],"en":"Established truths (veritates indubitatae, extra omnem dubitationis aleam positae) are either such truths as are exhaustively certain; or against which no other doubts can be raised than those that are answerable and have already been answered."},{"o":[1105,1202],"en":"Truths that are not established are unestablished truths (veritates non indubitatae)."}],"178":[{"o":[0,644],"en":"For full conviction of a truth 1) it is not required that the unknown doubts against it be answered; nor 2) that the scruples be answered, for they deserve no answer at all; 3) nor that all known doubts be answered; there can even be among them some which are unanswerable to a person, and who nevertheless can be fully convinced; 4) it is required that all doubts which make the mind waver be answered; 5) the answering of doubts always contributes to the increase of certainty, and to the strengthening of conviction §.176."}],"179":[{"o":[0,246],"en":"The uncertainty of cognition arises 1) from the limitation of our power of cognition, for which we are either to blame or not to blame; 2) from the lack of proper attention, reflection, diligence and patience §.129."},{"o":[247,602],"en":"145, which can be either necessary or contingent, blameworthy or praiseworthy; 3) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy ignorance of such truths without whose cognition another truth cannot become certain to us § 43. 50. 51; 4) from excessively strong attention to the doubts §.176."}],"180":[{"o":[0,451],"en":"In human cognition there is an uncertainty 1) which is wholly unavoidable, and which redounds neither to our shame nor to our honor; 2) which we need not avoid, even if we could, because its object is found outside our horizon, or beneath it, and which redounds to a person's honor; 3) which a person can and should avoid, because the opposite certainty belongs within his horizon §.178."},{"o":[452,634],"en":"This last always redounds to our shame, and whoever wants to improve his learned cognition as far as possible must seek to avoid all uncertainty of the third kind."}],"181":[{"o":[0,141],"en":"An opinion (opinio) is any uncertain cognition, insofar as we accept it and at the same time recognize that it is not certain."},{"o":[142,262],"en":"An opinion is either accepted as a ground from which we explain the appearances in the world, or not."},{"o":[263,317],"en":"The latter is a common opinion (opinio vulgaris)."},{"o":[318,534],"en":"The former is a philosophical or learned opinion (hypothesis philosophica, erudita), e.g. when one assumes the efflux of a magnetic matter in order to explain thereby how the magnet attracts iron."}],"182":[{"o":[0,530],"en":"All opinions 1) have the nature of all uncertain cognition; 2) common opinions are, at least in learned cognition, wholly to be despised; 3) what is an opinion to some is not an opinion to all; 4) one must not make too much of learned opinions, for they belong to the more imperfect half of learned cognition; 5) one must not wholly despise them, because they are the transition from historical cognition to fully certain learned cognition §.181."}],"183":[{"o":[0,198],"en":"In the case of learned opinions one must observe the following rules: 1) one must hold no learned opinion to be certainly true, and must not show as much zeal for it as for certain truths."},{"o":[199,304],"en":"2) One must accept them only in case of necessity, when we cannot yet have any better learned cognition."},{"o":[305,488],"en":"3) One must accept no manifestly false opinion, which is either internally impossible, or contradicts another undeniable truth, or contradicts the appearances."},{"o":[489,592],"en":"An appearance does not contradict an opinion merely because we cannot derive it from it."},{"o":[593,718],"en":"4) One must be ready to let go of the most elegant and most learned opinion as soon as one discovers its incorrectness."},{"o":[719,836],"en":"5) One must not accept a learned opinion before one has brought it to some probability."},{"o":[837,916],"en":"One must therefore, among other things, be able to explain most of the appearances from it."},{"o":[917,1008],"en":"6) One must constantly endeavor to transform an opinion into a certain cognition."},{"o":[1009,1107],"en":"7) One must have no excessively great inclination toward opinions, and not invent too many of them."},{"o":[1108,1319],"en":"8) One must not hold an opinion to be true because it is new, pleasing, marvelous, harmless, edifying, and required much effort, learning, wit and acuteness before it was invented."}],"184":[{"o":[0,72],"en":"Certainty and conviction are either true or merely apparent."},{"o":[73,247],"en":"The error through which we imagine ourselves to be convinced, when we are nevertheless not convinced, is called persuasion in the bad sense (persuasio malo significatu)."},{"o":[248,374],"en":"Since through it the certainty of learned cognition is hindered, one must seek to prevent it as far as possible."},{"o":[375,609],"en":"It arises, however: 1) from ignorance of the rules of logic; 2) from the lack of a certain cognition, for then one does not yet know how it feels with a true conviction; 3) from prejudices §.169."},{"o":[610,681],"en":"170. 171; 4) from excessive negligence and haste."},{"o":[682,862],"en":"Insofar as it lies within a person's power to remove these causes of persuasion, so far is he also able to avoid this fault himself."}],"185":[{"o":[0,225],"en":"When one now prevents persuasion and attains a thorough conviction, one acquires a science (scientia subiective spectata), that is, a learned cognition insofar as it is exhaustively certain."}],"186":[{"o":[0,299],"en":"The greater, more important and more fruitful the object of our learned cognition is, the more certain our cognition must be §.70; that is, we must seek to make the cognition of it thorough according to the most and best kinds of certainty, and in the highest degrees of it."},{"o":[300,363],"en":"With smaller objects the matter stands exactly the reverse."}],"187":[{"o":[0,301],"en":"Each person must not only choose for his principal science that part of learning which is capable of the greatest certainty, so far as is otherwise possible for him; but he must also seek to raise his chosen principal science to the highest possible degree of certainty §.63.64."}],"188":[{"o":[0,184],"en":"Exhaustive certainty consists in the consciousness of truth §.155, consequently that it is possible and grounded §.94.96, so that no fear of the opposite remains §.160."},{"o":[185,287],"en":"Consequently we are then conscious that the opposite is false, impossible and groundless §.95."},{"o":[288,396],"en":"97. Consequently exhaustive certainty arises from the consciousness of the necessity of truth §.114."}],"189":[{"o":[0,176],"en":"Exhaustive certainty arises either from our clearly recognizing that the truth is absolutely necessary, or that it is necessary only in this world §.113."},{"o":[177,322],"en":"If we recognize the first with mathematical certainty, then mathematical certainty of the first rank consists therein (certitudo mathematica primi ordinis)."},{"o":[323,469],"en":"In the other case we have mathematical certainty of the second rank (mathematica certitudo secundi ordinis), e.g. in our experiences."}],"190":[{"o":[0,134],"en":"The consciousness of the contingency of truth causes uncertainty, and at most only an inexhaustive certainty §.189.113."},{"o":[135,315],"en":"Arbitrary truths (hypothesis) are those whose truth depends on the arbitrary will of human beings, and their arbitrariness does not hinder complete certainty about them."}],"191":[{"o":[0,101],"en":"The proof (probatio) is that which is added to a truth so that it may become certain."},{"o":[102,275],"en":"The ground of proof (probatio materialiter sumta, ratio probans) is the ground from which the truth can be clearly recognized, and these are the marks of truth §.94."},{"o":[275,398],"en":"The consequence of the proof (probatio formaliter sumta, consequentia) is the connection of the truth with the ground of proof §.15."},{"o":[399,559],"en":"Every proof accordingly consists of the ground of proof and the consequence, and it can cause either an exhaustive or an inexhaustive certainty §.159."},{"o":[560,749],"en":"If the first, it is called a sufficient proof or a demonstration (probatio sufficiens, demonstratio); if the other, it is an insufficient proof (probatio insufficiens)."},{"o":[750,848],"en":"The latter is treated at length in the logic of probable cognition."}],"192":[{"o":[0,152],"en":"The cognition of a truth is either a demonstrable (cognitio demonstrabilis) or an indemonstrable cognition (cognitio indemonstrabilis)."},{"o":[153,226],"en":"The latter becomes certain to us as soon as we cognize it distinctly, the former not."},{"o":[227,280],"en":"The latter is fully certain without proof, the former not."},{"o":[281,388],"en":"One must take care that one hold neither begged cognition nor demonstrable cognition to be indemonstrable §.168."}],"193":[{"o":[0,261],"en":"If we wish to attain exhaustive certainty of a truth by means of proof and from it, or if a proof is to be a demonstration, §.191; then 1) every ground of proof contained in the proof must be exhaustively certain."},{"o":[262,440],"en":"All demonstrable grounds of proof must accordingly be proved again until one arrives at nothing but indemonstrable grounds of proof; 2) the consequence must be exhaustively certain §.191."},{"o":[441,558],"en":"Beyond these two items nothing further is required for a proof if it is to be a demonstration §.15."}],"194":[{"o":[0,279],"en":"If 1) even a single ground of proof in a proof is false, or 2) uncertain, 3) a single consequence in the proof is false, or 4) uncertain, or 5) if several of these faults are found together in a proof, it cannot be a demonstration §.193."}],"195":[{"o":[0,209],"en":"Just as someone can hold an insufficient proof to be a demonstration, so someone can also hold a demonstration to be an insufficient proof, or even a false proof §.165."},{"o":[210,341],"en":"Consequently one must not reject a demonstration at all merely because it does not have the proper effect on our mind."}],"196":[{"o":[0,113],"en":"Through a demonstration we seek to become certain either that something is true, or that something is false §.191."},{"o":[114,486],"en":"156. In the first case one can demonstrate a truth in two ways: 1) in an immediate way (demonstratio directa, ostensiva), when we derive the truth from its marks; 2) in a mediate way (demonstratio indirecta, apogogica, deductio ad absurdum), when we demonstrate the incorrectness of its opposite and infer its truth from that."},{"o":[487,785],"en":"In the same way one can demonstrate that something is false, or refute it (refutatio), a) in a mediate way (refutatio mediata), when we demonstrate the truth of its opposite; b) in an immediate way (refutatio immediata), when we derive its incorrectness from its marks."},{"o":[786,983],"en":"But one can prove that something is false: α) when we prove that it is impossible and groundless, β) that it is contrary to other undeniable truths, and γ) that something false follows from it."}],"197":[{"o":[0,99],"en":"A demonstration causes either a distinctly or an indistinctly exhaustive certainty §.191."},{"o":[100,277],"en":"188. The latter are the aesthetic demonstrations (demonstratio aesthetica), the former the logical, philosophical and learned ones (demonstratio logica, philosophica, erudita)."},{"o":[278,426],"en":"A logical demonstration which causes a mathematical certainty is a mathematical demonstration (demonstratio mathematica) §.161."}],"198":[{"o":[0,224],"en":"We can always infer: 1) if the whole proof is correct and certain, then the thing proved is also correct and certain; 2) if the thing proved is false and uncertain, then there is a fault in the proof §.194."},{"o":[225,543],"en":"But we cannot infer: 1) if the proof is false, then the thing proved is also false; 2) if the proof is uncertain, then the thing proved is also only uncertain; 3) what we cannot prove is false; 4) if the thing proved is true and certain, then the proof is also true and certain."}],"199":[{"o":[0,230],"en":"The spirit of demonstration (spiritus demonstrationis) consists in a person's sufficient skill in demonstrating, and the natural drive for such a certain cognition as one attains by demonstrating."},{"o":[231,340],"en":"Without this spirit no one can demonstrate, and no one should venture upon this matter without it."}],"200":[{"o":[0,89],"en":"Excessive love of demonstrating is the mania for demonstration (pruritus demonstrandi)."},{"o":[90,838],"en":"This learned sickness shows itself in the following items: 1) when one seeks to demonstrate what one cannot and may not demonstrate; 2) when one even demonstrates in a spurious way, merely to arouse the semblance of demonstrating among the dull-witted; 3) when one stops at mere demonstrating, and does not properly use and apply the demonstrated truths; 4) when one is a pedant in demonstrating, or wholly despises whatever is not demonstrated, whether it is actually not demonstrated, or we merely do not hold it to be so; 5) when one, like a charlatan, exalts demonstrating too highly, and makes too much boasting of it, even when one demonstrates useless trifles."}],"201":[{"o":[0,136],"en":"A sensation (sensatio) is a representation of a present thing, and in that we sense something clearly, we experience it."},{"o":[137,238],"en":"Experience (experientia) therefore consists in that cognition which is clear through sensing."},{"o":[239,492],"en":"Clear sensations are immediate experience (experientia immediata), and the remaining clear cognition, which is derived from immediate experience by a shorter proof, is called mediate experience (experientia mediata)."},{"o":[493,635],"en":"E.g. that I think is a sensation and immediate experience, but that I have a faculty of thinking is a mediate experience."}],"202":[{"o":[0,202],"en":"When something is certain to us, it is certain to us either from experience, or from other grounds, and in the first case either from our own experience, or from the experience of other people."},{"o":[203,373],"en":"Consequently we have a threefold source of all proofs §.191, namely our own experience, the experience of other people, and other grounds which are not experiences."}],"203":[{"o":[0,178],"en":"When we conduct a proof 1) from our own immediate experience, we need only represent clearly a single case in which we have sensed something §.201."},{"o":[179,380],"en":"2) In proofs from mediate experience, not only must at least one immediate experience be adduced, but the remaining grounds of proof together with the consequence must also be certain §.201."},{"o":[381,498],"en":"193. 3) That of which we are certain even through immediate experience is not an indemonstrable cognition §.192."},{"o":[499,632],"en":"201. 4) Experience gives us an exhaustive certainty, in that it contains all the marks of the truth of the object §.201."},{"o":[633,807],"en":"94. 96. 5) Experience gives us only an exhaustive certainty of the second rank §.189, and 6) it represents the truth to us only from the external marks §.94."}],"204":[{"o":[0,154],"en":"When we prove a truth from other grounds of proof which are not experience, we conduct a proof from reason (probatio ex ratione)."},{"o":[155,368],"en":"In such a proof 1) no ground of proof may occur which is an experience, and 2) all its grounds of proof must be exhaustively certain without experience, if it is to be a demonstration §.193."}],"205":[{"o":[0,217],"en":"All experience, and what we prove from it, is cognition from behind (cognitio a posteriore), but the remaining rational cognition is called cognition from before (cognitio a priore)."},{"o":[218,423],"en":"When we are certain of a truth both from our own experience and from reason, this is called the union of reason and experience (connubium rationis et experientiae)."}],"206":[{"o":[0,73],"en":"From the experience of other people we become certain by means of belief."},{"o":[74,244],"en":"Whoever gives out an actual thing as true, so that another may also hold it to be true, is called a witness (testis), and his action a testimony (testimonium, testari)."},{"o":[245,313],"en":"To believe (credere) means to accept something on account of a testimony."},{"o":[314,420],"en":"Belief (fides, fides historica) is the assent which we give to a thing on account of a testimony."},{"o":[421,543],"en":"The object of belief consists in past, present and future things, but not in other truths."}],"207":[{"o":[0,165],"en":"The authority of a witness (autoritas testis) consists in that degree of his honor by means of which he is held worthy of imitation in his cognition."},{"o":[166,236],"en":"We cannot believe a witness who has no authority with us."},{"o":[237,616],"en":"And this authority consists 1) in the competence of the witness (dexteritas testis), when he possesses sufficient powers not only to obtain a correct experience, but also to designate it in a correct manner; 2) in the sincerity of the witness (sinceritas testis), or in the inclination of his will to designate his experiences as he holds them to be true."},{"o":[617,703],"en":"Neither of the two can, without the other, procure for a witness the proper authority."}],"208":[{"o":[0,103],"en":"An eyewitness (testis oculatus) is a witness who has himself experienced the thing to which he testifies."},{"o":[104,220],"en":"A hearsay witness (testis auritus) is no eyewitness, but has only learned of the thing from the testimony of others."}],"209":[{"o":[0,386],"en":"For the competence of an eyewitness the following is required: 1) he must have been present at the thing to which he testifies; 2) he must be in a position to obtain a correct experience; 3) he must have a good and faithful memory, or write down his experiences at once; 4) he must possess the gift of designating his own cognition in a correct and sufficient manner."}],"210":[{"o":[0,152],"en":"A hearsay witness is, with regard to the testimony which he bears, an eyewitness, and he must accordingly possess all the competence required for that §.209."},{"o":[153,409],"en":"Insofar as he is a hearsay witness, however, the following items are required for his competence: 1) he must repeat only the testimonies of competent eyewitnesses, and must therefore always be able to name a competent eyewitness who is his authority."},{"o":[410,519],"en":"A common report (fama) is a testimony of many hearsay witnesses about a thing whose eyewitness is unknown."},{"o":[520,738],"en":"These hearsay witnesses live either at one time, and then their report is a public rumor (rumor sine capite), or at different times, and then it is an oral tradition (oralis traditio)."},{"o":[739,989],"en":"The common report lacks the necessary authority; 2) he must possess understanding enough to grasp rightly what he hears or reads; 3) he must possess a faithful memory, in order to communicate the information to others just as he received it."}],"211":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"In the sincerity of a witness it depends merely on his good will not to lie §.207."},{"o":[101,325],"en":"That witness who promises himself from a true testimony either nothing but good, or more good than evil, and who promises himself from lying nothing but evil, or more evil than good, speaks the truth."},{"o":[326,370],"en":"And in the opposite case he lies."},{"o":[371,657],"en":"This good or evil can concern his soul or his body, or his external condition; he may hope or fear it from GOD, from those people to whom he gives the testimony, or of whom he testifies, etc., whether he deceives himself in this matter or not."}],"212":[{"o":[0,96],"en":"The certainty of a testimony rests 1) on the internal marks of its truth §.95."},{"o":[97,343],"en":"96; 2) on the external marks of its truth, namely on the sufficient authority of the witness, which must at least be very probable §.207, 94. The testimonies of a competent and sincere witness cannot be false."}],"213":[{"o":[0,43],"en":"What we believe, we do not beg §.212."},{"o":[44,160],"en":"168. Unbelief (incredulitas) is the habit of not believing a certain or probable testimony."},{"o":[161,262],"en":"Credulity (credulitas) is the habit of believing an improbable testimony."},{"o":[263,300],"en":"Both faults are unreasonable §.174."}],"214":[{"o":[0,244],"en":"A witness is credible (testis fide dignus) if one can recognize in a learned manner, at least probably, that he has sufficient authority; the testimony of such a witness is a credible testimony (testimonium fide dignum)."},{"o":[245,366],"en":"Reasonable or seeing belief (fides oculata, rationalis) is the habit of believing only credible witnesses."}],"215":[{"o":[0,540],"en":"There is 1) a union of experience and belief (connubium experientiae et fidei), when we cognize something both from our own experience and from credible testimonies; 2) a union of belief and reason (connubium fidei et rationis), when we are certain of a thing both from reason and through belief; and 3) a union of reason, belief and experience (connubium rationis experientiae et fidei), when we cognize a truth convincingly by all three ways."},{"o":[541,770],"en":"The union of belief with the remaining sources of cognition is also called mixed belief (fides mixta); but when we accept something solely for the sake of belief, it is called pure belief (fides pura)."},{"o":[771,963],"en":"Because we can demonstrate neither the competence nor the sincerity of human witnesses, belief gives only a probability, and at most only a moral certainty §.175."},{"o":[963,1005],"en":"Of practical learned cognition."}],"216":[{"o":[0,146],"en":"A cognition is practical (cognitio practica) insofar as it can move us in a noticeable way to do or to omit an action."},{"o":[147,286],"en":"All more perfect cognition which is not practical is called a speculative cognition (cognitio speculativa, speculatio)."},{"o":[287,364],"en":"All learned cognition is accordingly either practical or speculative."}],"217":[{"o":[0,311],"en":"A cognition in which we represent to ourselves that something should be done or omitted is also called practical, insofar as it is opposed to theoretical cognition (cognitio theoretica, theoria), the cognition which does not represent to us that something should be done or omitted."},{"o":[312,471],"en":"All learned cognition is either practical or theoretical, and both kinds belong either to practical or to speculative cognition §.216."}],"218":[{"o":[0,165],"en":"Practical cognition is better than speculative cognition §.216, because in it a greater agreement of the manifold is found §.22."},{"o":[166,311],"en":"Whoever, then, wants to improve his learned cognition as far as possible must avoid speculation, and seek nothing but practical cognition."}],"219":[{"o":[0,238],"en":"No true learned cognition is speculative by its nature, but only on account of the lack of insight of a scholar, who cannot see, or will not see, its connection with human conduct."},{"o":[239,296],"en":"In the latter case the scholar disgraces himself."}],"220":[{"o":[0,389],"en":"It is unreasonable to want to hold as speculation: 1) all profound, determinate, precise and difficult learned cognition; 2) all learning in general; 3) all theory; 4) that learned cognition which is not practical in this or that person; 5) all cognition which does not have an immediate influence on the moral conduct of a person."}],"221":[{"o":[0,181],"en":"A cognition is practical either on account of its object (cognitio obiective practica), or on account of its constitution (cognitio subiective practica), or both at once."},{"o":[182,318],"en":"In the first case it must have an object which can be cognized as is required for a practical cognition."},{"o":[319,406],"en":"In the other case it is so constituted that it can act upon the faculty of desire."}],"222":[{"o":[0,121],"en":"A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 1) when the truths cognized are good and useful."},{"o":[122,195],"en":"And in this respect all true learned cognition is practical §.39."},{"o":[196,200],"en":"221."}],"223":[{"o":[0,265],"en":"A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 2) insofar as it underlies the rational actions by which human perfection is preserved §.221, and whose lack one notices at once in a person's actions."}],"224":[{"o":[0,214],"en":"A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 3) when its object consists in the rules which we must observe if we want to preserve our entire welfare in the best manner §.221."}],"225":[{"o":[0,388],"en":"A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 4) when its object has, in a remote and mediate way, an influence on our entire good conduct §.221, e.g. the cognition of such truths without which the remaining practical truths §.222-224 could not be cognized by us human beings either at all, or at least not in a learned manner."}],"226":[{"o":[0,387],"en":"Because no human being is in a position to cognize all practical truths, that learned cognition is called preeminently and absolutely practical (cognitio obiective et comparative s. eminenter practica) which is practical in a higher degree, e.g. which is noticeably more useful to us than another, and which has an immediate or very near influence on our conduct."}],"227":[{"o":[0,161],"en":"All learned cognition is called comparatively speculative (cognitio comparative speculativa) if it is practical in a very small degree."},{"o":[162,378],"en":"E.g. 1) if it has a very small and imperceptible influence on our regular conduct; 2) if it has a very small utility; 3) if it hinders us from a more necessary and more useful cognition."}],"228":[{"o":[0,191],"en":"On account of the differing way of life and principal science, a learned cognition can rightly be held by one person to be practical, and by another to be speculative §.226."},{"o":[192,435],"en":"227. Indeed, someone may, on account of his meager powers of cognition, and from negligence, fail to see the connection of a learned cognition with our conduct, and will therefore hold it to be a speculative cognition."}],"229":[{"o":[0,87],"en":"Insofar as a learned cognition is practical, so far is it also useful."},{"o":[88,183],"en":"One must accordingly 1) hold no truth and no learned cognition to be wholly useless §.222."},{"o":[184,321],"en":"2) A learned cognition is not useless merely because it is of no use to this or that person, and is perhaps even harmful to him."},{"o":[322,439],"en":"3) A learned cognition which is not useful in one respect can be useful in another respect."},{"o":[440,550],"en":"4) One must not infer: what is not useful to us now will also not be useful to us in the future."}],"230":[{"o":[0,196],"en":"The more utilities a learned cognition procures, the greater they are, and the greater and more manifold its influence on our entire conduct, the more practical it is §.216."}],"231":[{"o":[0,180],"en":"In order that every scholar may improve his learned cognition as far as possible, he must 1) cognize most perfectly those truths which are practical in the highest degree."},{"o":[181,329],"en":"The degree of diligence and of perfection of learned cognition must be proportioned to the degree of the practical in its object §.70."},{"o":[330,479],"en":"2) Each must, where possible, choose for his principal science that part of learning which is practical in the highest degree §.63."}],"232":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"A cognition which is practical according to its constitution is called a living and moving cognition (cognitio viva, movens)."},{"o":[141,491],"en":"A living rational cognition (cognitio rationalis viva) is a rational cognition which is so constituted that rational desires and aversions are awakened through it; or it contains motives (motiva), that is, such rational representations of the good and evil through which desires and aversions arise."},{"o":[492,622],"en":"All rational cognition which is not so constituted is called a dead rational cognition (cognitio rationalis mortua)."}],"233":[{"o":[0,355],"en":"That constitution of cognition by virtue of which it effects desires and aversions is called the life of cognition (cognitionis vita), which, when it consists in the motives §.232, is called the rational life of cognition, or then the cognition moves and stirs in a rational manner (vita rationalis cognitionis)."}],"234":[{"o":[0,358],"en":"A learned cognition can 1) with regard to its object be uncommonly practical, and nevertheless dead; 2) it can be false and uncommonly living; the life of cognition is therefore no mark of its truth; 3) a learned cognition can be very extensive, great, correct, certain and distinct, and nevertheless dead §.232."},{"o":[359,501],"en":"Life is accordingly yet another perfection of learned cognition, which is different from all its remaining perfections."}],"235":[{"o":[0,88],"en":"The practical in learned cognition becomes actually practical only through life."},{"o":[89,284],"en":"Living rational cognition is accordingly more perfect than dead, even if both should otherwise be adorned with all the remaining logical perfections in equal degree."}],"236":[{"o":[0,119],"en":"If rational and learned cognition is to move rationally, it must 1) be an intuitive cognition."},{"o":[120,354],"en":"A cognition is intuitive (cognitio intuitiva) when we represent the object more strongly than its signs; but if we represent these more strongly than the former, the cognition is symbolic (cognitio symbolica)."},{"o":[355,421],"en":"All learned cognition is either intuitive or symbolic."},{"o":[422,608],"en":"When one occupies attention, reflection and deliberation chiefly with the object of learned cognition, it becomes a distinct intuitive cognition."}],"237":[{"o":[0,162],"en":"Pleasure (voluptas) is the intuitive cognition of perfection, and the intuitive cognition of imperfection is displeasure (taedium)."},{"o":[163,301],"en":"A cognition causes us indifference (indifferentia) when it causes us neither pleasure nor displeasure."},{"o":[302,657],"en":"If, then, rational and learned cognition is to move rationally, it must 2) awaken pleasure and displeasure; a) about itself it must awaken in us nothing but pleasure: because by virtue of all the rules of logic it must be uncommonly perfect, and the feeling of its perfection must drive us to desire and to attain it."},{"o":[658,778],"en":"Whoever, then, is indifferent whether he cognizes a thing in a learned way or not, his learned cognition is not living."}],"238":[{"o":[0,286],"en":"Rational and learned cognition, if it is to move rationally, must b) awaken about the object either pleasure, or vexation, or both at once, in that it represents the object to us in an intuitive manner either as good, or as evil, or as both at once."},{"o":[287,464],"en":"Then the learned cognition contains motives §.232; not only to desire itself §.237, but also either to desire or to abhor its object."}],"239":[{"o":[0,268],"en":"If rational and learned cognition is to move us rationally, it must 3) represent to us in a learned manner that all the good and all the evil on account of which it pleases or displeases us will actually be in our subsequent states."}],"240":[{"o":[0,309],"en":"If rational and learned cognition is to move us rationally, it must 4) represent to us in a learned manner that it is within our power, or can be within our power, to make actual the good on account of which it pleases us, and to prevent the evil on account of which it causes us displeasure."}],"241":[{"o":[0,134],"en":"If learned cognition is to be properly living, it must 1) cause no equilibrium, or at least soon remove it."},{"o":[135,389],"en":"We stand, namely, in an equilibrium (aequilibrium) when the motives on both sides are equal and opposed to one another; 2) it must outweigh the sensible life of indistinct cognition, or the latter must not contradict it at all."}],"242":[{"o":[0,237],"en":"If rational and learned cognition is properly living, it causes a powerful and lasting resolution, which breaks out in deed, in that we actually act as accords with the learned cognition."}],"243":[{"o":[0,248],"en":"The genuine life of learned cognition (vita vera cognitionis eruditae) consists in its true use (usus verus cognitionis eruditae), when we effect all its true utilities, or make ourselves in fact more perfect through it."},{"o":[249,472],"en":"But if we make ourselves thereby more imperfect or only apparently more perfect, its misuse (abusus cognitionis eruditae) consists therein, and that is its spurious life (vita cognitionis eruditae spuria)."},{"o":[473,549],"en":"A perfect learned cognition must be living in a genuine way."}],"244":[{"o":[0,235],"en":"The more and greater motives a learned cognition contains, the better they are cognized, and the more and greater desires and aversions are awakened through it, the more living is the learned cognition."}],"245":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"One must not infer: 1) the learned cognition which does not move me does not move others either; 2) that which moves me moves others also."}],"246":[{"o":[0,84],"en":"The lack of life of learned cognition arises from ignorance §.236-240."},{"o":[85,190],"en":"41. According as the latter is either necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy §.43.50."},{"o":[191,249],"en":"51, so also is the lack of life constituted."},{"o":[250,350],"en":"It cannot be demanded that all learned cognition move constantly and in equal degree."}],"247":[{"o":[0,207],"en":"The greater and more practical the objects are, the more strongly their learned cognition must move; the smaller and less practical they are, the less their learned cognition must move §.70."},{"o":[208,283],"en":"All our learned cognition must move us in some respect §.237."}],"248":[{"o":[0,257],"en":"Each person must choose for his principal science that part of learning which by its nature is capable of the most living learned cognition; and each must seek to cognize his principal science most livingly §.63."},{"o":[258,290],"en":"64. Of learned concepts."}],"249":[{"o":[0,112],"en":"A concept (conceptus) is a representation of a thing in a being which possesses the faculty of thinking."},{"o":[113,164],"en":"All our representations are accordingly concepts."}],"250":[{"o":[0,115],"en":"As many different kinds of cognition as there are, so many different kinds of concepts there are also §.249."},{"o":[116,217],"en":"Consequently concepts are either learned concepts (conceptus eruditus, logicus), or not §.21."},{"o":[218,309],"en":"The former is a distinct concept that is logically perfect to a noticeable degree."},{"o":[310,437],"en":"An unlearned concept (conceptus ineruditus) is a concept that possesses no noticeable degree of logical perfection."},{"o":[438,503],"en":"And then it is either a beautiful concept, or not §.19."},{"o":[504,619],"en":"The former is an aesthetic concept (conceptus pulcher, aestheticus), the latter however a common one (conceptus vulgaris)."},{"o":[620,682],"en":"A learned concept is either at the same time beautiful, or not."},{"o":[683,752],"en":"The latter is a merely learned concept (conceptus mere eruditus)."},{"o":[753,838],"en":"The former, however, is a concept that is not merely learned (conceptus non mere eruditus)."},{"o":[839,885],"en":"And concepts of this kind are the best concepts §.32."}],"251":[{"o":[0,213],"en":"A learned concept must conform as far as possible to the rules of extensiveness, of magnitude, of truth, of clarity, of certainty, and of the practical character of learned cognition §.41-248."}],"252":[{"o":[0,83],"en":"The object of learned concepts is not determined by their nature."},{"o":[84,159],"en":"Of any possible thing whatever a learned concept is possible §.250."}],"253":[{"o":[0,199],"en":"Everyone must improve according to the doctrine of reason chiefly those concepts that belong to his principal science, §.64, and in any system whatever chiefly the principal concepts."},{"o":[200,379],"en":"It is neither possible nor necessary to improve all our concepts according to the doctrine of reason, and just as little to improve all our learned concepts to an equal degree."},{"o":[380,448],"en":"The greater a concept is, the more one must improve it §.70."}],"254":[{"o":[0,114],"en":"We have only three ways of arriving at concepts: experience, abstraction, and arbitrary combination."}],"255":[{"o":[0,45],"en":"All our sensations are concepts §.249."},{"o":[46,165],"en":"201. A concept of experience (conceptus per experientiam formatus) is a concept that we obtain through experience."},{"o":[166,266],"en":"E.g. the concepts of the alterations of our soul, of our body, and of other things outside us."},{"o":[267,373],"en":"We obtain a concept of experience either through immediate or through mediate experience."},{"o":[374,380],"en":"§.201."}],"256":[{"o":[0,203],"en":"Through immediate experience we can obtain only concepts of actual things, insofar as they are present to us, and indeed these concepts contain only affirmative and changeable marks."},{"o":[204,314],"en":"The mediate concepts of experience can also represent to us other objects, and other things about them."},{"o":[315,321],"en":"§.201."}],"257":[{"o":[0,135],"en":"Because concepts of experience can be learned only insofar as they are distinct §.250, one must analyze them carefully §.142."},{"o":[136,247],"en":"And this work is often furthered 1) by the use of the weapons of the senses, e.g. magnifying glasses."},{"o":[248,360],"en":"2) By the anatomy of corporeal things, whereby one cognizes the parts and the manner of their composition."},{"o":[361,437],"en":"And thereby one obtains the concept of the essence of a corporeal thing."},{"o":[438,632],"en":"3) By attention to that from which a thing arises and how it arises, when one is present at its arising, and from this one also obtains concepts of the essence."},{"o":[633,814],"en":"4) When one seeks to experience the object often in different circumstances: for then one discovers the best marks more easily, whereby the concept of experience becomes complete."}],"258":[{"o":[0,176],"en":"All concepts of experience are true and certain §.202, and they represent to us the objects as they are constituted, because otherwise we would be inhabitants of another world."},{"o":[177,451],"en":"But if we hold a concept to be a concept of experience that is not one, or something to be an object of the concept of experience that is not one: then it does indeed seem as if the concept of experience were false, but the error lies in another concept."}],"259":[{"o":[0,271],"en":"We make a concept through logical abstraction (conceptus per abstractionem logicam formatus) when we hold up against one another concordant concepts of different things, and represent distinctly to ourselves alone the marks that they have in common with one another."},{"o":[272,365],"en":"To this end 1) let one take some concepts that are different and at the same time similar."},{"o":[366,628],"en":"E.g. a rational animal and an irrational animal; 2) let one analyze each of them §.142; 3) let one abstract the marks that are different in them, or obscure them; 4) let one gather the remaining marks together into one concept, e.g. an animal."}],"260":[{"o":[0,141],"en":"All concepts that are made through logical abstraction are abstracted or abstract concepts (conceptus abstractus, notio)."},{"o":[142,235],"en":"Concepts that are not abstracted are called singular concepts (conceptus singularis, idea)."},{"o":[236,249],"en":"E.g. Leibniz."},{"o":[250,320],"en":"All immediate concepts of experience are singular concepts §.255.201."},{"o":[321,446],"en":"What is represented as a mark of another is contained in it and belongs to it (in altero contineri, ipsi convenire)."},{"o":[447,561],"en":"The abstracted concept is therefore contained in all those concepts from which it can be abstracted §.259."},{"o":[562,779],"en":"The abstracted concept contains under itself those from which it was abstracted, and these are contained under it (conceptus alios sub se continet, et conceptus sub alio continentur seu ad eum referuntur)."}],"261":[{"o":[0,257],"en":"An abstracted concept is called a higher concept (conceptus superior) insofar as it contains others under itself; but insofar as a concept is contained under another, it is called a lower concept (conceptus inferior)."},{"o":[258,454],"en":"The lower concept always contains marks that are not contained in the higher §.259, and these are called the difference of the lower concepts (differentia conceptus inferioris)."},{"o":[455,654],"en":"An abstracted concept that comprehends under itself only singular concepts is called a species (species), but one that also contains abstracted concepts under itself is called a genus (genus)."}],"262":[{"o":[0,129],"en":"The aggregate of all concepts that are contained under an abstracted concept is its extension (sphaera notionis)."},{"o":[130,273],"en":"The more abstract and higher a concept is, that is: the more often logical abstraction has been repeated in its case, the greater is its extension."},{"o":[274,431],"en":"An abstracted concept belongs either to more concepts than those that are contained under another, or to fewer, or to neither."},{"o":[432,674],"en":"In the first case it is a wider concept than the other (conceptus latior), in the second a narrower (conceptus angustior), and in the third they are reciprocal concepts (conceptus reciproci), of which neither is wider than the other."},{"o":[675,929],"en":"Insofar as an abstracted concept belongs to all that are contained under another, it is called a universal concept (conceptus universalis), but insofar as it does not belong to all of them, a particular one (conceptus particularis)."},{"o":[930,1088],"en":"An abstracted concept can be called a higher and lower, wider and narrower, universal and particular concept in different respects."},{"o":[1089,1155],"en":"Every abstracted concept is universal in a certain respect."},{"o":[1156,1277],"en":"Abstract learned cognition is therefore called universal learned cognition (cognitio erudita universalis)."}],"263":[{"o":[0,204],"en":"In universal cognition one can 1) infer: What belongs to or contradicts the higher concepts, that also belongs to or contradicts all lower concepts that are contained under them."},{"o":[205,211],"en":"§.260."},{"o":[212,414],"en":"2) One cannot infer: what belongs to or contradicts a lower concept, that also belongs to or contradicts other lower concepts that belong with it to a higher concept."},{"o":[415,421],"en":"§.261."},{"o":[422,581],"en":"3) One can infer: what belongs to or contradicts all and each of the lower concepts, that also belongs to or contradicts their higher concept §.259."},{"o":[582,586],"en":"261."}],"264":[{"o":[0,98],"en":"Abstract cognition furthers 1) the distinctness and completeness of cognition §.143."},{"o":[99,287],"en":"For the more abstract it is, the less it contains in itself §.259, and the more easily it can therefore be thought through without much confusion; 2) the extensiveness of cognition §.263."},{"o":[288,335],"en":"25. 3) The thoroughness of cognition §.263."},{"o":[336,403],"en":"163. 4) The utility and use of learned cognition §.39."}],"265":[{"o":[0,156],"en":"An abstracted concept contains nothing that is not contained in the lower concepts §.259; if these are true, then, it cannot possibly be false."},{"o":[157,286],"en":"If one wishes, then, to prove an abstracted concept, one need only show that it was abstracted from true concepts."},{"o":[287,423],"en":"If one abstracts from a false concept the marks that contradict the rest, one obtains a true concept §.95."}],"266":[{"o":[0,280],"en":"A concept is made through learned arbitrary combination (combinatio conceptuum arbitraria, logica, erudita, philosophica, rationalis) when one represents to oneself as One two concepts of which one has cognized in a learned way that they are not repugnant to one another."},{"o":[281,600],"en":"To this end let one take 1) an abstracted concept, whether one has already abstracted it long ago, or only now abstracts it; 2) a difference of a lower concept, of which we are either already convinced, or are convinced only afterwards, that it does not contradict the abstracted concept."},{"o":[601,714],"en":"This difference has either been left out through the abstraction from the abstract concept, or not."},{"o":[715,983],"en":"In the first case we obtain a lower concept from which we have abstracted the abstract one; in the second, however, a new lower concept; 3) let one represent to oneself the abstract concept, taken together with this difference, as One concept."}],"267":[{"o":[0,117],"en":"A learned concept that has been made through arbitrary combination must be proved or refuted."},{"o":[118,318],"en":"Both happen 1) from experience, when one shows that its objects either are actual or are not actual; 2) from reason, either in a mediate or immediate way §.196."},{"o":[319,447],"en":"E.g. when one shows either that, and how, its objects can become actual, or that they cannot become actual."}],"268":[{"o":[0,118],"en":"A definition, or a logical definition (definitio, definitio logica), is a determinate concept of a thing."},{"o":[119,215],"en":"The object of the definition is the defined thing, or the defined concept (definitum) §.151."}],"269":[{"o":[0,180],"en":"A description (descriptio) is an indeterminate concept, and it is either a complete or a complete description §.151 (descriptio completa, vel incompleta)."},{"o":[181,276],"en":"No description is a definition, and still less must it be held to be one §.268."},{"o":[277,363],"en":"Meanwhile they can be very perfect, useful and necessary learned concepts."}],"270":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"Definitions must conform to six rules: 1) A definition must conform to the rules of the extensiveness of learned cognition."},{"o":[143,151],"en":"§.41-65."},{"o":[152,201],"en":"Consequently it must be a complete concept."},{"o":[202,212],"en":"§.268.151."},{"o":[213,378],"en":"55. Consequently a) it must contain in itself no fewer marks than are required for a complete concept; nor b) more than are necessary.§.269."},{"o":[379,797],"en":"Hence the definition, among all possible complete concepts of a thing, must be the very shortest, that is: contain as few marks as completeness permits. c) It must not be wider than the defined thing §.262, consequently its marks taken together must belong to no other thing than the defined thing alone. d) It must not be narrower than the defined thing."},{"o":[798,804],"en":"§.262."},{"o":[805,880],"en":"Consequently the defined concept and the definition are reciprocal concepts §.262."}],"271":[{"o":[0,264],"en":"If in a system one defines the higher concepts before the lower, then, when one is to define a concept that is not the highest, one can compose its definition from the nearest higher concept and from the difference §.261."},{"o":[265,477],"en":"Thereby the definitions become short and complete, the definitions hang together more systematically, they are more conformable to the nature of the lower concepts, and they pave an easier way to the demonstrations."}],"272":[{"o":[0,406],"en":"Because the defined concept and the definition are reciprocal concepts §.270, one can infer: 1) To whom the defined concept belongs, to him the definition also belongs; 2) to whom the definition belongs, to him the defined concept also belongs; 3) to whom the defined concept does not belong, to him the definition also does not belong; 4) to whom the definition does not belong, to him the defined concept also does not belong §.262."}],"273":[{"o":[0,83],"en":"2) A definition must conform to the rules of the magnitude of learned cognition."},{"o":[84,92],"en":"§.66-91."},{"o":[93,279],"en":"It must therefore consist of the greatest, most important and most fruitful marks, of the essence, essential parts and properties, and, as often as possible, of affirmative marks."},{"o":[280,290],"en":"§.115-121."},{"o":[291,440],"en":"It is therefore a fault a) if a definition consists of contingent attributes: then it would not even be a complete concept."},{"o":[441,536],"en":"§.121. b) If it consists of relations: then it also would be no complete concept."},{"o":[537,601],"en":"§.121. c) If it consists without necessity of negative marks."},{"o":[602,798],"en":"If in a system one is to define opposed concepts, then the opposition must be evident from the definitions, and so one of the two must be defined negatively."}],"274":[{"o":[0,85],"en":"3) A definition must conform to the rules of the truth of learned cognition."},{"o":[86,95],"en":"§.92-114."},{"o":[96,235],"en":"The truth of definitions moreover requires that they conform to all the rules that must be observed in definitions."},{"o":[236,436],"en":"A regular definition (definitio legitima) conforms to all the rules of definitions; one that is contrary to one or more of these rules is an irregular definition (definitio illegitima)."}],"275":[{"o":[0,91],"en":"4) A definition must conform to all the rules of the distinctness of learned cognition."},{"o":[92,102],"en":"§.115-155."},{"o":[103,150],"en":"Yet it can be an incomplete concept."},{"o":[151,157],"en":"§.147."},{"o":[158,412],"en":"Since all its marks must be clear §.137, one can presuppose them as clear either partly from common life, partly from other systems; or one must first define them before one puts them as marks into the definition §.268."}],"276":[{"o":[0,128],"en":"If a definition is absolutely obscure, or has absolutely obscure marks, then it is no definition at all §.275."},{"o":[129,334],"en":"125. But if it is obscure only relatively, or contains marks that are obscure only relatively, then it can indeed be a good definition, but not for him to whom it is obscure §.125."}],"277":[{"o":[0,57],"en":"The defined concept is not clearer than it is itself."},{"o":[58,279],"en":"So that the definition may not become obscure and indistinct §.275, one must take care that the defined concept be set neither as an immediate nor as a mediate mark into its own definition §.116."},{"o":[280,386],"en":"When the latter happens, this fault is called the circle in defining (circulus in definiendo)."}],"278":[{"o":[0,96],"en":"5) A definition must conform to the rules of the certainty of learned cognition §.155-215."},{"o":[97,340],"en":"Consequently one must prove of any definition whatever a) that it is either a correct concept of experience §.258, or a correct abstracted concept §.265, or a correct arbitrary concept §.267, b) that it is regular §.274."}],"279":[{"o":[0,94],"en":"6) A definition must conform to the rules of practical learned cognition §.216-248."},{"o":[95,368],"en":"Consequently one must choose for a definition the most important and most fruitful marks, from which can most easily be proved the most useful and serviceable things that we must investigate about the defined thing in order to cognize it in a practical way."}],"280":[{"o":[0,83],"en":"A definition either represents the essence of the defined thing, or not §.273."},{"o":[84,194],"en":"The former is a real definition (definitio realis, genetica), the latter however a nominal definition (definitio nominalis)."},{"o":[195,307],"en":"A nominal definition contains either the essential parts of the defined thing, or its properties §.273."},{"o":[308,438],"en":"The former is an essential nominal definition (definitio essentialis), the latter however an accidental nominal definition (definitio accidentalis)."}],"281":[{"o":[0,321],"en":"If one wishes to make a real definition, then 1) let one seek to cognize the essence of the defined thing, either through mediate experience, e.g. §.257, or through abstraction, or through a proof from reason, or through arbitrary combination; 2) let one make of the essence a determinate concept §.280."}],"282":[{"o":[0,276],"en":"If one wishes to make a nominal definition, then let one seek 1) to cognize the essential parts or properties of the thing to be defined, either from mediate experience §.256, or through a proof from reason, or from the essence, if it is already known to us."},{"o":[277,440],"en":"2) Let one select those essential parts and properties, and as many of them, as are necessary for a perfect regular definition §.270-279."}],"283":[{"o":[0,240],"en":"Because the things to be defined can have many essential parts and properties, one can select at pleasure those that make up a nominal definition §.282; if only one otherwise observes the rules of definitions."},{"o":[241,294],"en":"And insofar nominal definitions are arbitrary."}],"284":[{"o":[0,60],"en":"Just as we cannot define all concepts §.139.150."},{"o":[61,315],"en":"268, so we also need not define all concepts, but only those that belong within the horizon of our learned cognition, and without whose definition our learned cognition could not attain the required degree of its perfection."},{"o":[316,401],"en":"The excess in defining is called the mania for defining (pruritus definiendi)."}],"285":[{"o":[0,228],"en":"The logical division of concepts (divisio logica) consists in a distinct representation of all lower concepts that are opposed to one another and that are contained under one and the same higher concept."},{"o":[229,371],"en":"This higher concept is called the divided concept (divisum), and the lower concepts the members of the division (membra dividentia)."},{"o":[372,672],"en":"If one wishes accordingly to make a logical division, 1) let one take an abstracted concept; 2) let one find all differences of its lower concepts that are opposed to one another; 3) let one combine these differences, one after another, by an opposition, with the higher concept."},{"o":[673,734],"en":"E.g. all concepts are either obscure or clear concepts."}],"286":[{"o":[0,233],"en":"A division of a member of the division is called a subdivision (subdivisio), and different divisions of a concept, which are made in different respects, are called codivisions (codivisiones)."},{"o":[234,362],"en":"It is clear of itself what the divided concept of the subdivision (subdivisum), and of the codivisions (codivisum) is."},{"o":[363,486],"en":"Likewise what the members of the subdivision (membra subdividentia), and of the codivisions (membra codividentia) are."}],"287":[{"o":[0,381],"en":"If a logical division is to be correct, then 1) the divided concept must not be wider than the members of the division, when they are taken together by an opposition (disiunctive); or no member of the division must be left out, because otherwise not all lower concepts that are opposed to one another would have been adduced §.285."},{"o":[382,538],"en":"262. 2) The divided concept must not be narrower than the members of the division, when they are taken together by an opposition §.262."},{"o":[539,680],"en":"Otherwise one would mix among the members of the division a concept that is no lower concept of the divided concept §.285."},{"o":[681,901],"en":"Consequently the divided concept and the members of the division, when they are taken together by an opposition, are reciprocal concepts §.262, and the division must exhaust the divided concept."}],"288":[{"o":[0,101],"en":"3) The members of the division must be so opposed that none belongs to another §.285."},{"o":[102,221],"en":"260. 4) The divided concept must not contradict any member of the division: for it is contained in it §.285."},{"o":[222,408],"en":"260. 5) The members of the subdivision must not be placed among the members of the division: otherwise not all members would be opposed to one another n. 3. 4. §.286."},{"o":[409,569],"en":"6) The number of the members of the division must be determined solely by the nature of the divided concept, and the aim in which it is divided."}],"289":[{"o":[0,370],"en":"To whom the divided concept belongs, to him One member of the division also belongs; to whom One member of the division belongs, to him the divided concept also belongs; to whom the divided concept does not belong, to him none of the members of the division belongs; to whom none of the members of the division belongs, to him the divided concept also does not belong §.287."}],"290":[{"o":[0,114],"en":"Logical divisions 1) further the invention of learned concepts through arbitrary combination §.285."},{"o":[115,326],"en":"266. 2) Serve us to put our abstracted concepts into a proper order and connection, and to retain them the more easily; 3) convince us of the universality of our abstract cognition."}],"291":[{"o":[0,221],"en":"One must take care in divisions not to heap them up too much, together with their subdivisions, because otherwise a great confusion arises; and one must avoid in them everything forced."},{"o":[222,250],"en":"On learned judgments."}],"292":[{"o":[0,165],"en":"Concepts that belong to one another §.260 agree with one another; but those that do not belong to one another are repugnant to one another, or conflict with one another (repugnare)."},{"o":[166,292],"en":"The agreement and the conflict of several concepts are the logical relations of concepts (logica conceptuum relatio)."},{"o":[293,702],"en":"A judgment (iudicium) is a representation of a logical relation of some concepts; and insofar as everything is abstracted from it without which its truth can be cognized in a learned way, it is called a logical judgment (iudicium logicum), which is a learned judgment (iudicium eruditum) if it conforms, as far as possible, to all the rules of learned cognition."}],"293":[{"o":[0,271],"en":"That concept of which we represent to ourselves in a judgment that another belongs or does not belong to it, is the subject (subiectum); the other, on the contrary, of which we represent to ourselves that it belongs or does not belong to the subject, is the predicate (praedicatum)."},{"o":[272,362],"en":"The representation of the agreement of several concepts is the concept of connection (copula)."},{"o":[363,558],"en":"Negation (negatio) is the representation of the absence of a thing, and the representation of the absence of the concept of connection is the negation of the concept of connection (negatio copulae)."},{"o":[559,637],"en":"It is therefore the representation of the conflict of the predicate with the subject §.292."}],"294":[{"o":[0,123],"en":"In a logical judgment we represent to ourselves either that the predicate belongs to the subject, or does not belong §.292."},{"o":[124,252],"en":"293. The former is an affirmative judgment (iudicium affirmans, affirmativum), the latter a negative one (iudicium negans, negativum)."},{"o":[253,311],"en":"E.g. the soul can think, matter cannot think."},{"o":[312,392],"en":"In a negative judgment there is the negation of the concept of connection §.293."},{"o":[393,661],"en":"And if in a judgment there is a negation either in the subject or the predicate, or in both at once, provided only that the concept of connection is not negated, it is an affirmative judgment, which is called an infinite judgment (iudicium infinitum)."},{"o":[662,803],"en":"One can therefore transform all negative judgments into affirmative ones, if one moves the negation away from the concept of connection to the predicate."},{"o":[804,866],"en":"E.g. the soul is not mortal, the soul is immortal."},{"o":[866,958],"en":"The quality of judgments (qualitas iudicii) consists in their affirmation and negation."}],"295":[{"o":[0,262],"en":"An affirmative judgment is true if the predicate belongs to the subject, and indeed just as the former is affirmed of the latter; but it is false if the predicate does not belong to the subject, or at least does not belong to it in the way in which it is affirmed of it."},{"o":[263,517],"en":"A negative judgment is true if the predicate does not belong to the subject, and indeed as it is denied of it; but it is false if the predicate belongs to the subject, or at least is not so repugnant to it as it is denied of it §.294."},{"o":[518,635],"en":"99. If a true judgment is held to be false, and a false one to be true, it is an erroneous judgment §.109."}],"296":[{"o":[0,118],"en":"The truth and incorrectness of a judgment lies in the concept of connection and in the negation of it §.295."},{"o":[119,378],"en":"293. Consequently 1) the subject and predicate of a false judgment can be true; 2) the subject and predicate of a true affirmative judgment can be false; 3) the subject can be true and the predicate false, or conversely, in a true negative judgment."}],"297":[{"o":[0,88],"en":"All true judgments have a ground, and a sufficient ground, of their truth §.16."},{"o":[89,174],"en":"This ground is called the condition of judgments (hypothesis, conditio iudicii)."},{"o":[175,262],"en":"Consequently the truth and incorrectness of judgments can be cognized from it."},{"o":[263,332],"en":"It is therefore the criterion and the proof of truth §.94."},{"o":[333,337],"en":"191."}],"298":[{"o":[0,99],"en":"The conditions of judgments are 1) either sufficient or insufficient conditions §.297.119."},{"o":[100,163],"en":"191; 2) either internal or external conditions §.297."},{"o":[164,235],"en":"94; 3) either absolutely necessary or contingent conditions."},{"o":[236,400],"en":"The former are the essence, the essential parts, the properties or the definition of the subject, and the latter its contingent attributes and relations §.297."},{"o":[401,602],"en":"121. 273. If the contingent condition of a judgment is combined with its subject, it is called the determination or limitation of the judgment (determinatio et limitatio iudicii)."}],"299":[{"o":[0,156],"en":"If the condition of a judgment 1) is an internal, absolutely necessary and sufficient condition, then it is inseparable from the subject."},{"o":[157,324],"en":"Whether one thinks it or not, it is there nonetheless, and consequently the predicate also belongs or does not belong to the subject, according as it is either affirmed or denied."},{"o":[325,418],"en":"Truth then does not require that one combine this condition with the judgment §.295."},{"o":[419,550],"en":"But if it is 2) a contingent condition, then it is now there, now not there, and the judgment would be now true, now not true."},{"o":[551,641],"en":"Truth therefore requires that one combine these conditions with the judgment."},{"o":[642,854],"en":"A determinate judgment (iudicium determinatum, limitatum) is a judgment that has a determination; a judgment that is not determinate is an indeterminate judgment (iudicium indeterminatum, illimitatum)."}],"300":[{"o":[0,141],"en":"The analysis of a judgment (analysis, resolutio iudicii) consists in this, that one gives heed one after another to all parts of it."},{"o":[142,327],"en":"All judgments can be analyzed §.139, and through this work one finds not only the proofs of judgments §.297; but one also learns to grasp them properly, and to present them to others."}],"301":[{"o":[0,88],"en":"The subject of a judgment is either a singular or an abstract concept §.293."},{"o":[89,187],"en":"260. The former is a singular judgment (iudicium singulare), the latter a common judgment (iudicium commune)."},{"o":[188,300],"en":"In which the predicate is affirmed or denied either of all those contained under the subject, or of some."},{"o":[301,403],"en":"The former is a universal judgment (iudicium universale), the latter a particular judgment (iudicium particulare)."},{"o":[404,594],"en":"The latter is either at the same time universally true, a not merely particular judgment (iudicium non tantum particulare), or not, a merely particular judgment (iudicium tantum particulare)."},{"o":[595,654],"en":"All these judgments either affirm or deny §.294."},{"o":[655,958],"en":"The universal affirmative judgments are called A; the universal negative E; the particular affirmative I; and the particular negative O. The predicate of all universal judgments is, in respect of the subject, a universal concept, because negative ones can be transformed into affirmative ones §.294."},{"o":[959,1170],"en":"262. And because in singular as well as in universal judgments it is judged that the predicate belongs to the whole subject or not, one can count the singular judgments among the universal ones."},{"o":[1171,1305],"en":"Insofar as a judgment is either a singular or a common one, insofar one ascribes to it a magnitude (quantitas iudicii)."}],"302":[{"o":[0,112],"en":"A universal affirmative judgment is true if the predicate belongs to all those contained under the subject §.301."},{"o":[113,280],"en":"295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to not a single one of those contained under the subject, or does not belong to some, or even to only a single one of them."},{"o":[281,399],"en":"A universal negative judgment is true if the predicate is repugnant to all those contained under the subject §.301."},{"o":[400,552],"en":"295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to all those contained under the subject, or to some of them, or even to only a single one of them."}],"303":[{"o":[0,242],"en":"If the sufficient condition of a common judgment 1) is absolutely necessary in the subject, then it is inseparable from it, and is found wherever the subject is found, consequently in all those contained under it §.263."},{"o":[243,346],"en":"Then the judgment is therefore universally true, for where the condition is, there the predicate is also §.299."},{"o":[347,554],"en":"If, therefore, a predicate is affirmed or denied of an abstract concept on account of its essence, or essential part, or property, or definition, then the judgment is universally true §.298."},{"o":[555,794],"en":"299. 2) If this condition is a determination, then it becomes absolutely necessary as soon as it is combined with the subject, because thereby all things to which this determination does not belong are excluded from it §.299."},{"o":[795,845],"en":"And thus the determinate judgment is universally true."}],"304":[{"o":[0,72],"en":"A judgment has either only One subject and One predicate, or several."},{"o":[73,181],"en":"The former is a simple judgment (iudicium simplex), the latter however a composite one (iudicium compositum)."},{"o":[182,372],"en":"If the subject and predicate are composed of several concepts, then either some of them are thought for the sake of the rest, or none is thought for the sake of another."},{"o":[373,631],"en":"In the first case the concepts for whose sake the rest are thought are the principal subjects and principal predicates (subiectum et praedicatum principale), and the rest the secondary subjects and secondary predicates (subiectum et praedicatum minus principale)."},{"o":[632,734],"en":"In the other case the composite judgment is a copulative judgment (iudicium copulativum)."}],"305":[{"o":[0,201],"en":"A judgment that affirms that a judgment follows from the condition, without that or this being declared true or false, is a conditional judgment (iudicium hypotheticum, conditionale)."},{"o":[202,390],"en":"The condition of conditional judgments is called the first, or preceding (prius, antecedens), but the judgment that follows from it the last or following (posterius, consequens)."},{"o":[391,467],"en":"Not all judgments that have a condition are therefore conditional §.297."}],"306":[{"o":[0,207],"en":"For the truth of conditional judgments it is not required that the first and the last be true; but that it have a correct consequence, or that the first be the sufficient ground of the truth of the last."},{"o":[208,277],"en":"In the opposite case the conditional judgment is false §.305."},{"o":[278,282],"en":"295."}],"307":[{"o":[0,230],"en":"A disjunctive judgment (iudicium disiunctivum) is a judgment that affirms that among several judgments one is true and the rest are false, but in such a way that it is not determined which is true and which false."},{"o":[231,444],"en":"The several judgments of which it is composed are called the members of the disjunction, or of the opposition (membra disiunctionis, disiunctiva), e.g. the soul is either simple, or composite."}],"308":[{"o":[0,165],"en":"If a disjunctive judgment is to be true, then the members of the disjunction must relate to one another just as one represents to oneself in it §.295."},{"o":[166,223],"en":"Consequently 1) not more than one member must be true."},{"o":[224,658],"en":"If therefore all members or even only two are true at the same time, then the judgment is false; 2) One member must necessarily be true; if therefore all members are false, or one is true only contingently, then the judgment is false; 3) no member must be left out, for if the omitted one were also false, it would nevertheless not be evident from the disjunction that among those adduced One is necessarily true §.307."}],"309":[{"o":[0,177],"en":"The representation of the manner and way in which the predicate belongs or does not belong to the subject is the determination of the concept of connection and of the negation of it (modus formalis)."},{"o":[178,238],"en":"A judgment has either such a determination, or not."},{"o":[239,429],"en":"The former is an impure judgment (iudicium modale, modificatum, complexum qua copulam), the latter however a pure judgment (iudicium purum), e.g. this world exists necessarily, it does not exist necessarily."},{"o":[430,577],"en":"In the truth of impure judgments one must pay particular attention to the determination of the concept of connection and of the negation of it."}],"310":[{"o":[0,162],"en":"A judgment that is composed of an affirmative and a negative one in a very hidden way is called an exponible judgment (iudicium exponibile)."},{"o":[163,298],"en":"If it is to be true, then both judgments must be correct, and one must accordingly analyze it in order to make sure of this."},{"o":[299,348],"en":"E.g. GOD alone is absolutely immortal."}],"311":[{"o":[0,168],"en":"The judgments that at the same time represent the state of the mind with respect to a certain judgment are judgments that are not logical (iudicia non logica)."},{"o":[169,217],"en":"For example: O how greatly the sinner deceives himself!"},{"o":[218,407],"en":"Such judgments are very practical, and so that learned cognition may not become merely learned, one must take care that not all learned judgments be simple or merely logical."}],"312":[{"o":[0,123],"en":"All learned judgments are either judgments of consideration (iudicia theoretica), or judgments of practice (iudicia practica) §.217."},{"o":[124,281],"en":"The latter judge that something should be done or left undone, e.g. we must observe the laws; the former however do not, e.g. virtue makes us happy."}],"313":[{"o":[0,129],"en":"All learned judgments are either demonstrable (iudicia demonstrativa), or indemonstrable judgments (iudicia indemonstrabilia)."},{"o":[130,266],"en":"The truth of the latter is evident from themselves as soon as we cognize them distinctly; the former, however, cannot be certain without proof §.192."}],"314":[{"o":[0,111],"en":"The predicate of an affirmative judgment is either identical with the subject, or it is different from it."},{"o":[112,272],"en":"The former is an empty judgment (iudicium identicum), which is either wholly empty (iudicium ex toto identicum), or in part (iudicium ex parte identicum)."},{"o":[273,404],"en":"The predicate of the former is not at all different from the subject, but the predicate of the latter is only a part of the subject."},{"o":[405,566],"en":"Because no concept is repugnant to itself, we cognize the truth of all empty judgments as soon as we understand them §.295; they are therefore indemonstrable §.313."},{"o":[567,741],"en":"If a judgment is not empty, one must fear that between the subject and predicate there could be so great a difference that they do not belong to one another."},{"o":[742,853],"en":"Consequently they are not indemonstrable, and there are therefore, besides the empty ones, no indemonstrable judgments §.313."}],"315":[{"o":[0,81],"en":"Indemonstrable judgments are either judgments of consideration, or judgments of practice."},{"o":[82,156],"en":"The former are fundamental judgments (axioma), the latter however postulated judgments (postulatum)."},{"o":[157,228],"en":"One must hold no demonstrable judgment to be an indemonstrable one §.313."}],"316":[{"o":[0,132],"en":"As often as we affirm a concept of itself, or a part of it of it, so often we have a fundamental judgment §.315.314."},{"o":[133,393],"en":"One can therefore find fundamental judgments from definitions, if one affirms of the defined concept: 1) the whole definition, and insofar logical definitions are indemonstrable; 2) some marks of the definition; 3) the individual marks of it §.268."}],"317":[{"o":[0,241],"en":"As often as I represent to myself a thing that one can regard as an effect, and I affirm of him who wishes to produce it that he must produce it or a part of it, so I have a postulated judgment §.315.314."},{"o":[242,532],"en":"If therefore the defined concept can be regarded as an effect, and I affirm of him who wishes to produce it that he must produce 1) the whole definition, 2) or some marks, or 3) individual marks: so I find postulated judgments from the definitions §.268."}],"318":[{"o":[0,148],"en":"In a demonstration from reason all proofs must be completely certain §.193.204; they are therefore either demonstrable or not §.313."},{"o":[149,203],"en":"In the first case they must be proved again."},{"o":[204,318],"en":"Consequently a proof does not become a demonstration until I arrive at nothing but indemonstrable proofs."},{"o":[319,455],"en":"Empty judgments, fundamental judgments and postulated judgments are therefore the first beginnings of all demonstrations from reason §.314."},{"o":[456,564],"en":"315. Then the understanding comes completely to rest, when the proof has been carried back to such judgments."}],"319":[{"o":[0,85],"en":"Demonstrable judgments are either certain merely through experience, or not."},{"o":[86,186],"en":"The former are intuitive judgments (iudicium intuitivum), the latter however derivative judgments (iudicium discursivum)."},{"o":[187,326],"en":"The intuitive judgment consists of nothing but concepts of experience, and is an immediate experience §.201, and a singular judgment §.301."},{"o":[327,376],"en":"No intuitive judgment is indemonstrable §.313."},{"o":[377,532],"en":"314, for I must always recall a single case, and thereby cognize how and whether I have arrived at such a judgment without deception §.202."},{"o":[533,630],"en":"All intuitive judgments are the first beginnings of all demonstrations from experience §.202."}],"320":[{"o":[0,157],"en":"If one wishes to find an intuitive judgment, then 1) let one take the thing that one senses as subject; 2) let one analyze the sensation, according to §.142."},{"o":[158,224],"en":"257; 3) the marks discovered let one affirm of the subject §.319."}],"321":[{"o":[0,249],"en":"The predicates of intuitive judgments can be contingent attributes, alterations, relations, effects, causes, actions and passions; but never the essence, the essential parts, the properties, and negative marks §.256."},{"o":[250,527],"en":"No negative judgment is an intuitive judgment, although it can be derived from an intuitive judgment, in that the marks that are opposed to those that we sense in the subject can with truth be denied of it §.295."}],"322":[{"o":[0,244],"en":"From intuitive judgments universal ones are derived: 1) If one makes an intuitive judgment of all things of a species, according to §.320, and then infers that the predicate can be affirmed universally of the whole species §.263."},{"o":[245,552],"en":"2) If one wishes to derive a universal judgment from One intuitive judgment, then a) let one seek the higher concept under which the subject belongs, according to §.259. b) Let one seek the condition of the intuitive judgment. c) Let one investigate whether it is absolutely necessary, or contingent, in the higher concept."},{"o":[553,708],"en":"In the latter case let one combine it with the subject, and then in both cases one can affirm the predicate universally of the higher concept §.299."}],"323":[{"o":[0,91],"en":"Derivative judgments are demonstrated either from experience or from reason §.319."},{"o":[92,131],"en":"The former are mediate experiences §.203."},{"o":[132,299],"en":"204. A judgment of experience is any judgment that is certain through experience, whether it be an intuitive judgment §.319, or a derivative judgment."}],"324":[{"o":[0,81],"en":"Derivative judgments require either a shorter or a longer proof §.319."},{"o":[82,199],"en":"The former are called corollaries (consectarium, corollarium), and they can be either judgments of consideration or of practice §.312."},{"o":[200,373],"en":"They are for the most part adduced without proof, and easily found if one compares a few definitions, indemonstrable judgments and other truths with one another."}],"325":[{"o":[0,166],"en":"Derivative judgments that require a longer proof (either from experience or from reason) are either judgments of consideration, or judgments of practice §.312."},{"o":[167,233],"en":"The former are called theorems (theorema), the latter however problems (problema)."}],"326":[{"o":[0,518],"en":"If one wishes to invent a theorem, then one must 1) invent the judgment, or it can already be known; 2) one must invent a longer proof, consequently a) one must seek out all proofs, whether they are already known to us, or one may learn them from others, or invent them anew; b) one must put them into a distinct connection, and c) one must understand the art of composing a proof from many proofs without causing confusion §.325."}],"327":[{"o":[0,212],"en":"Whoever wishes to practice himself in the invention of theorems must begin with the easier ones, and with those that can be tested by experience, so that he may recognize his missteps the more easily."}],"328":[{"o":[0,186],"en":"For the sake of distinctness, a problem is analysed into three parts: 1) The question (quaestio problematis) is the representation of the action that is to be done or left undone."},{"o":[187,309],"en":"Whenever we represent to ourselves a thing that can be regarded as an effect, a question can be raised."},{"o":[310,390],"en":"2) The solution (solutio problematis) analyses the manner in which the question arises."},{"o":[391,589],"en":"It must therefore either cite all the actions of which the question consists, or all the causes, or both at once, and if one wants to find it, one must logically define the object of the question."},{"o":[590,732],"en":"3) The proof of the problem (demonstratio problematis), which demonstrates that by observing the solution the question becomes actual."},{"o":[733,868],"en":"It accordingly presupposes the solution as a condition, and the whole problem can be regarded as a conditional theorem §.305."},{"o":[868,1000],"en":"The test of the problem (proba, examen problematis) is that by which one is convinced that one has observed the solution."}],"329":[{"o":[0,240],"en":"If one can discover no impossibility in the question, one may venture upon the solution, and one can invent it either through experience, or from reason and through abstraction, or in an arbitrary manner."},{"o":[241,459],"en":"If one wants to find it through experience, one must 1) seek to be present at the thing when it arises, and form for oneself through experience a distinct concept of the manner of its arising §.257."},{"o":[460,585],"en":"2) One must observe precisely all the causes, and everything that takes place in the arising, namely when it falls within our senses."},{"o":[586,794],"en":"3) The remaining causes, and the remaining parts of the arising, which do not fall within our senses, one must seek to guess at, by means of that part of erudition within whose scope the object belongs."},{"o":[795,1021],"en":"4) One compares the effect, whose manner of arising we cannot experience, with another that we can resolve through experience, and infers that the former arises in a similar manner and through similar causes."}],"330":[{"o":[0,320],"en":"If one assumes an effect merely arbitrarily, 1) one must be well versed in those parts of erudition to which it belongs, form for oneself a distinct concept of it, and reflect upon all the powers and causes known to us, to see whether we may encounter something that belongs to the solution of the question."},{"o":[321,495],"en":"2) If one connects many actions and causes arbitrarily with one another, and attends to what comes out, one also finds many solutions to many problems."}],"331":[{"o":[0,378],"en":"In the midst of a system, through the pursuit of demonstrations from reason, one imperceptibly arrives at many solutions; and if one has already found the solutions of the lower questions, one need only separate out their differences, and one has the solution of the higher question, whether it speaks of an abstract action or of some other abstract effect."}],"332":[{"o":[0,198],"en":"For the sake of the distinctness of the problem, one must find, from the distinct concepts of the effect and of the causes contained in the solution, what each cause contributes to the effect."}],"333":[{"o":[0,366],"en":"To obtain brevity of the problems in a system, 1) one must resolve in the preceding ones the actions and effects of which the actions and effects of the following problems are composed; and 2) one must resolve in the preceding ones those actions and effects under which the questions of the following problems are contained."}],"334":[{"o":[0,365],"en":"A problem is 1) true (veritas problematis), if it contains nothing impossible in itself, consequently if the solution is not contrary to the question; 2) complete (completum problema), if by the observation of the solution that follows which the question concerns; 3) exact (problema accuratum, adaequatum), if it contains neither too little nor too much in itself."},{"o":[366,414],"en":"And these are three perfections of problems."}],"335":[{"o":[0,351],"en":"A problem is false, 1) if the solution is absolutely impossible; 2) if the solution is impossible in a certain respect, e.g. if it is not possible through the powers of human beings, or in certain circumstances; 3) if the solution can indeed become actual, but contributes nothing to the question; 4) if it even contradicts the question §.334."}],"336":[{"o":[0,280],"en":"A problem is incomplete, 1) if the purpose is not attained by the most exact observation of the solution; 2) if one does not know how the solution can actually be made; 3) if the solution cannot be carried out in all cases where it is necessary §.334."}],"337":[{"o":[0,129],"en":"A problem is not exact if it is indeed true and complete, but leads through detours, and contains too much in itself §.334."}],"338":[{"o":[0,172],"en":"Occult qualities (qualitates occultae) are properties of which we have no clear and distinct cognition, and which we assume without sufficient ground."},{"o":[173,240],"en":"Since they conflict with the nature of learned cognition §.21."},{"o":[241,314],"en":"168, whoever assumes them is either stupid or a deceiver."},{"o":[315,485],"en":"Consequently one must especially avoid them when one investigates the grounds of things, their nature and alterations, and hence also in the solution of problems."}],"339":[{"o":[0,174],"en":"A borrowed judgment (lemma) is a provable judgment which is assumed in a system without proof, because with its proof it belongs to another system."}],"340":[{"o":[0,181],"en":"Remarks (scholia) are judgments which in a system are neither proofs nor are proved, but which, for the sake of greater utility, have been mixed in among the rest."}],"341":[{"o":[0,171],"en":"Equipollent judgments (iudicia aequipollentia) are different judgments in which the logical relation of one and the same subject and predicate is the same."},{"o":[172,359],"en":"E.g. not all human beings are virtuous, some human beings are not virtuous; not no human being is learned, some human beings are learned; everything has a ground, nothing is without ground."},{"o":[360,467],"en":"Since now the truth and incorrectness of a judgment are found in the logical relation §.295."},{"o":[468,593],"en":"296, 1) all the remaining equipollent judgments are true if one is true; and 2) all the remaining ones are false if one is false."},{"o":[594,786],"en":"The equipollence of judgments depends on such alterations of the judgment as do not change its truth or incorrectness; otherwise they are not equipollent judgments."}],"342":[{"o":[0,162],"en":"Judgments which have one and the same subject, predicate and quality, and among which only one is universal, contain one another in themselves (iudicia subalternata)."},{"o":[162,294],"en":"The universal contains the others in itself (iudicium subalternans), and the rest are contained in it (iudicium subalternatum)."},{"o":[295,480],"en":"E.g. all human beings can err, some human beings can err, one human being can err; no human being is sinless, some human beings are not sinless, one human being is not sinless."},{"o":[481,732],"en":"1) If the universal judgment is true, then those contained in it are also true, but not conversely; 2) if the judgments contained in the universal are false, then the universal is also false, but not conversely §.302."}],"343":[{"o":[0,115],"en":"Judgments contradict one another (iudicia contradictoria) when the one accurately denies what the other affirms."},{"o":[116,254],"en":"E.g. the judgments A and O, likewise E and I, contradict one another if they have one and the same subjects and predicates §.301."},{"o":[255,600],"en":"341. Since every concept either belongs to every other concept or does not (for it cannot be thought at all that both at once or neither of the two could be the case), among all contradictory judgments either the affirmative is true and the negative false, or the negative true and the affirmative false §.295."},{"o":[601,796],"en":"Consequently one can infer 1) from the truth of the one among contradictory judgments to the incorrectness of the other; and 2) from the incorrectness of the one to the truth of the other."}],"344":[{"o":[0,155],"en":"If one judgment affirms particularly what the other denies particularly, they are opposed to one another in a particular manner (iudicia subcontraria)."},{"o":[156,234],"en":"Consequently the judgments I and O, if they have the same subjects and predicates."},{"o":[235,279],"en":"1) These judgments can be true at the same time."},{"o":[280,450],"en":"For since their predicate can be a particular concept with respect to the subject §.262, it can be both affirmed and denied of it particularly with truth §.295."},{"o":[451,511],"en":"2) These judgments can never both be false at the same time."},{"o":[512,577],"en":"For if I and O are false, then A and E are also false §.342."},{"o":[578,714],"en":"Consequently A and O, E and I, which have the same predicates and subjects, could be false at the same time, which is impossible §.343."}],"345":[{"o":[0,212],"en":"If one judgment affirms universally what the other denies universally, consequently if A and E have the same subject and predicate, they are opposed to one another in a universal manner (iudicia contraria.)"},{"o":[213,256],"en":"1) These judgments can both be false."},{"o":[257,341],"en":"For their predicate can be a particular concept with respect to the subject §.262."},{"o":[342,430],"en":"Consequently it can be neither universally affirmed nor universally denied of it §.302."},{"o":[431,478],"en":"2) These judgments cannot both be true."},{"o":[479,542],"en":"For if A and E were true, then I and O would also be true §.342."},{"o":[543,690],"en":"Consequently A and O, likewise E and I, although they had the same subject and predicate, could be true at the same time, and that is impossible §.343."}],"346":[{"o":[0,189],"en":"That alteration by virtue of which the predicate is made out of the subject of a judgment, and the subject out of the predicate, is called the conversion of a judgment (conversio iudicii)."},{"o":[190,359],"en":"The judgment with which the alteration takes place is called the converted one (iudicium conversum), and that which arises from it, the converting judgment (iudicium convertens)."},{"o":[360,437],"en":"In conversion either the quantity of the judgment is changed, or not."},{"o":[438,576],"en":"In the first case the conversion occurs accidentally (conversio per accidens), in the other simply (conversio simplex)."},{"o":[577,733],"en":"Insofar as a true judgment also remains true after the conversion, to that extent it can be converted (iudicium converti potest, iudicium reciprocabile)."}],"347":[{"o":[0,159],"en":"All affirmative judgments whose subject and predicate are reciprocal concepts can be converted simply, whether they be universal or singular judgments."},{"o":[160,235],"en":"For the subject also belongs to all contained under the predicate §.262."},{"o":[236,302],"en":"Consequently it can be universally affirmed of the predicate §.302."},{"o":[303,496],"en":"346. E.g. the judgments which affirm the definition of the defined concept, and the members of the division, taken together under a disjunction, of the divided concept §.270."},{"o":[497,501],"en":"287."}],"348":[{"o":[0,78],"en":"All true particularly affirmative judgments can be converted simply."},{"o":[79,224],"en":"For the abstract concept of the subject belongs to some of those contained under it §.260, and can therefore be affirmed particularly of them §.295."},{"o":[225,371],"en":"And since the predicate belongs to some of those contained under the subject §.295, it can be assumed as their higher concept §.260 for the subject."},{"o":[372,462],"en":"Consequently the former subject can be affirmed particularly of the former predicate §.346."}],"349":[{"o":[0,72],"en":"A universally negative judgment can be converted 1) simply."},{"o":[73,222],"en":"Otherwise the subject would not have to be contrary to all those contained under the predicate §.302, consequently it would have to belong to some of them §.343."},{"o":[223,311],"en":"Consequently the predicate would also have to belong to some of those contained under the subject §.348."},{"o":[312,418],"en":"Consequently the universally negative judgment would be false §.302, which is absurd; 2) accidentally."},{"o":[419,634],"en":"For since it can be converted simply, the particular judgment which is contained in the universal converting judgment is also true §.342, and through it the judgment is converted accidentally §.346."}],"350":[{"o":[0,76],"en":"All universally affirmative judgments can be converted accidentally."},{"o":[77,181],"en":"For if they are true, the particularly affirmative judgments contained in them are also true §.342."},{"o":[182,304],"en":"These can be converted simply §.348, and precisely thereby the universal ones are converted accidentally §.346."}],"351":[{"o":[0,195],"en":"If in the particularly negative judgments one sets the negation with the predicate, they become particularly affirmative judgments §.294, and can therefore then be converted simply §.348."}],"352":[{"o":[0,190],"en":"A universally affirmative judgment is contraposed (contrapositio) when one changes its predicate into a negative concept, and universally denies the former subject of it."},{"o":[191,340],"en":"All true universally affirmative judgments can be contraposed, that is, to whatever the predicate does not belong, to that its subject also does not belong."},{"o":[341,435],"en":"Otherwise the predicate would have to be contrary to some things, and the subject belong to them §.343."},{"o":[436,537],"en":"One could therefore deny the predicate with truth of some of those contained under the subject §.295."},{"o":[538,616],"en":"Thus the universally affirmative judgment would be false §.302, and that is absurd."},{"o":[616,652],"en":"On learned inferences of reason."}],"353":[{"o":[0,233],"en":"If some true judgments contain the sufficient ground of the truth of another, they are connected with one another §.15, and in this relation of true judgments consists the connection of truths (nexus veritatum)."}],"354":[{"o":[0,250],"en":"An inference of reason (ratiocinium) is a distinct representation of the connection of truths; which, if it is perfect in a higher degree, is called a learned or a logical inference of reason (ratiocinium logicum, eruditum)."}],"355":[{"o":[0,269],"en":"In an inference of reason we derive a truth from other truths §.353, 354. And since these other truths are therefore the proofs of the former §.191, we make an inference of reason when we distinctly derive a truth from its proofs."},{"o":[270,362],"en":"Consequently the distinct representation of the consequence of a proof is an inference of reason §.191."}],"356":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"That judgment which is derived from others in an inference of reason is the conclusion (conclusio, probandum, principiatum)."},{"o":[143,283],"en":"But those judgments from which the conclusion is derived are the premises (praemissae, data, sumtiones, principia)."}],"357":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"The subjects and predicates of the judgments of which an inference of reason consists are called the principal concepts of an inference of reason (termini)."},{"o":[141,285],"en":"The subject of the conclusion is the smaller principal concept (terminus minor), but its predicate the greater principal concept (terminus maior)."},{"o":[286,439],"en":"The principal concept which, besides the smaller and greater, is found in the premises, is called the middle principal concept (terminus medius)."}],"358":[{"o":[0,291],"en":"Since the premises contain the proof of the conclusion §.355.356, but in them, besides the parts of the conclusion, nothing further is contained than the middle principal concept §.357; the latter is the proof §.191, consequently the condition of the conclusion §.297."},{"o":[292,505],"en":"One therefore finds the middle principal concept following the guidance of the 297th and 298th paragraphs; and an inference of reason consists in this, that we derive a judgment from its condition in a distinct manner §.355."}],"359":[{"o":[0,190],"en":"The matter of the inference of reason (ratiocinii materia) consists in its premises, but its form (ratiocinii forma) in the consequence of the conclusion from the premises."}],"360":[{"o":[0,122],"en":"A correct inference of reason (ratiocinium verum) must be correct in the matter as well as in the form §.359.355."},{"o":[123,288],"en":"193. If therefore either the matter is false, or the form, or both at the same time, it is a false, incorrect inference of reason (ratiocinium falsum)."},{"o":[289,429],"en":"An erroneous inference of reason (ratiocinium erroneum) is a false inference of reason insofar as it is held to be a correct one §.109."},{"o":[430,537],"en":"From §.193 and 194 it is evident when an inference of reason has demonstrative certainty, or not."}],"361":[{"o":[0,211],"en":"Because the consequence in every inference of reason must be distinct §.355, but no distinctness is possible without order §.142, the judgments of an inference of reason must be duly ordered together."},{"o":[212,415],"en":"Since now no order is possible without rules, certain rules of inference are necessary, according to which an inference of reason must be arranged if it is to have a correct and distinct consequence."}],"362":[{"o":[0,65],"en":"It is impossible that something be and not be at the same time."},{"o":[66,178],"en":"Or, if one and the same thing is affirmed or denied of a thing at the same time, it is nothing."},{"o":[179,410],"en":"This proposition is called the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), and it is the first internal mark of truth §.95, on which all consequences of inferences of reason must rest if they are to be true §.361."}],"363":[{"o":[0,208],"en":"What can be universally affirmed or denied with truth of a concept can also be affirmed or denied with truth of any other concept whatever that belongs under it §.342."},{"o":[209,337],"en":"Otherwise two contradictory judgments would have to be true at the same time §.343, and that is contrary to the principle of contradiction §.362."},{"o":[338,441],"en":"This proposition is called the inference from the universal to the particular (dictum de omni et nullo)."}],"364":[{"o":[0,163],"en":"If the sufficient ground is true, its consequence is also true; otherwise the sufficient ground would not be a sufficient ground §.15, which is contrary to §.362."},{"o":[164,301],"en":"Consequently, if the consequence is false, the sufficient ground is also false, because otherwise the sufficient ground could be without consequence."},{"o":[302,439],"en":"This inference is called the inference from the sufficient ground to its consequence (a ratione sufficiente ad rationatum valet consequentia)."}],"365":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"If one of contradictory judgments is true, the other is false; and if the one is false, the other is true §.343.362."},{"o":[143,241],"en":"This proposition is called: the inference from the opposite (ab uno oppositorum ad alterum valet consequentia)."}],"366":[{"o":[0,181],"en":"If a judgment is true, then that which has arisen through a logical alteration of the first, which can be undertaken without prejudice to truth, must also be true."},{"o":[182,315],"en":"Otherwise the logical alteration would have to be prejudicial to truth and not prejudicial, which is impossible §.362."},{"o":[316,403],"en":"This inference is called: the inference from the logical alteration of a true judgment."}],"367":[{"o":[0,156],"en":"An ordinary inference of reason (ratiocinium ordinarium) is an inference of reason in which one infers from the universal to the particular §.363."},{"o":[157,351],"en":"For example: all virtues contribute something to my happiness; now all philosophical virtues are virtues, therefore all philosophical virtues contribute something to my happiness."},{"o":[352,451],"en":"All other inferences of reason are extraordinary inferences of reason (ratiocinium extraordinarium)."}],"368":[{"o":[0,123],"en":"All ordinary inferences of reason have two premises which have the middle principal concept in common with one another §.367."},{"o":[124,397],"en":"That premise in them which contains the greater principal concept is called the major premise of ordinary inferences of reason (propositio maior ratiociniorum ordinariorum); but that which contains the smaller principal concept, the minor premise of the same (propositio minor)."}],"369":[{"o":[0,215],"en":"That species of ordinary inferences of reason in which the middle principal concept stands in the subject of the major premise and in the predicate of the minor premise is called the first figure (figura prima ratiociniorum)."},{"o":[216,318],"en":"For example: all human beings can err; the learned are human beings, therefore the learned can err."},{"o":[319,391],"en":"In this figure the minor premise and the conclusion have one subject §.368."},{"o":[392,459],"en":"Consequently these two judgments always have the same quantity §.301."}],"370":[{"o":[0,175],"en":"That species of ordinary inferences of reason in which the middle principal concept stands in the predicate of both premises is called the second figure (figura secunda)."},{"o":[176,304],"en":"E.g. no absurd thing is true, everything that stands in the Bible is true; therefore nothing that stands in the Bible is absurd."}],"371":[{"o":[0,173],"en":"That species of ordinary inferences of reason in which the middle principal concept stands in the subject of both premises is called the third figure (figura tertia)."},{"o":[174,329],"en":"For example: all the learned have an improved understanding, some of the learned are vicious; therefore some of the vicious have an improved understanding."}],"372":[{"o":[0,235],"en":"That species of ordinary inferences of reason in which the middle principal concept stands in the predicate of the major premise, and in the subject of the minor premise, is called the fourth, or the Galenic figure (figura quarta, galenica)."},{"o":[236,352],"en":"For example: no stupid human being is learned, some of the learned are pious; therefore some of the pious are not stupid people."}],"373":[{"o":[0,309],"en":"The figures of inferences of reason (figurae ratiociniorum) are accordingly different species of ordinary inferences of reason, which arise from the different arrangement of the middle principal concept with the remaining principal concepts in the premises; and there are no more than four figures §.369."},{"o":[310,314],"en":"372."}],"374":[{"o":[0,236],"en":"From the inference from the universal to the particular §.363, the following rules of all ordinary inferences of reason flow: 1) In an ordinary inference of reason there can be neither more nor fewer principal concepts contained than three."},{"o":[237,343],"en":"Such an inference contains only three judgments, consequently only six places for the principal concepts §.368.357."},{"o":[344,536],"en":"Now it contains 1) a principal concept of which something is universally affirmed or denied, and of which it is said in the other premise that another concept belongs to it."},{"o":[537,608],"en":"It therefore occurs twice, because it is the middle principal concept §.368."},{"o":[609,696],"en":"2) The smaller principal concept occurs in the conclusion and the minor premise §.357."},{"o":[697,782],"en":"368; and 3) the greater principal concept in the conclusion and the major premise §.357."},{"o":[783,787],"en":"368."}],"375":[{"o":[0,77],"en":"2) The middle principal concept must not be put into the conclusion."},{"o":[78,368],"en":"For in ordinary inferences of reason the greater principal concept is affirmed or denied of the smaller in the conclusion, because according to the statement of the premises the smaller principal concept belongs to the middle one, of which the greater can be affirmed or denied §.364."},{"o":[369,456],"en":"Consequently the conclusion can consist only of the smaller and greater principal concepts."}],"376":[{"o":[0,268],"en":"3) The premises must not all deny, for one must affirm that the smaller or greater principal concept is connected with the middle one §.363, or from purely negative premises nothing follows, although from purely affirmative ones something follows."},{"o":[269,459],"en":"If the middle principal concept is a negative concept, at least one premise is merely infinite §.294, and then it merely seems as if all the premises denied."}],"377":[{"o":[0,253],"en":"4) The premises must not all be particular judgments; otherwise one would not be inferring from the universal to the particular §.363, or from purely particular premises nothing follows, although from purely universal ones something can follow."}],"378":[{"o":[0,261],"en":"5) If one premise denies, the conclusion must also deny: for then the inference of reason is directed by the negative part of the inference from the universal to the particular §.363, and it must therefore have a negative conclusion."}],"379":[{"o":[0,328],"en":"6) If one premise is a particular judgment, the conclusion must also be a particular one; for then one infers: because some things of a kind belong to that concept of which something is universally affirmed or denied, this can also be affirmed or denied of those some §.363."}],"380":[{"o":[0,237],"en":"7) The conclusion is always directed by the weaker part of the inference of reason: for the negative and particular premises are called the weaker part of the inference of reason (pars ratiocinii debilior) §.378."},{"o":[238,242],"en":"379."}],"381":[{"o":[0,88],"en":"8) In the conclusion there must not be contained less than in the premises."},{"o":[89,320],"en":"For otherwise the smaller and greater principal concepts would contain less in themselves in the conclusion than in the premises, and more than three principal concepts would therefore be found in the inference of reason §.374."}],"382":[{"o":[0,85],"en":"9) In the conclusion there must not be contained more than in the premises."},{"o":[86,280],"en":"Otherwise the smaller and greater principal concepts would contain more in the conclusion than in the premises, and there would therefore be more than three principal concepts in the inference of reason §.374."}],"383":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"Besides these rules, two further rules must be observed in the first figure: 1) The minor premise must always affirm in the first figure."},{"o":[143,296],"en":"For since it has as its subject the subject of the conclusion §.369, it affirms of it that it belongs to the middle principal concept §.363."},{"o":[297,420],"en":"If the middle principal concept is negative, the minor premise is infinite §.376, and so nevertheless an affirmative judgment §.294."}],"384":[{"o":[0,64],"en":"2) The major premise must always be universal in the first figure."},{"o":[65,287],"en":"For if the conclusion is universal, the major premise can only be universal §.379; but if it is a particular judgment, the minor premise is also of that kind §.369, and so the major premise must once again be universal §.377."},{"o":[288,462],"en":"Because singular judgments belong to the universal ones §.301, they make no exception to the rules which demand the universality of the judgments of an inference of reason."}],"385":[{"o":[0,240],"en":"The moods of ordinary inferences of reason (modi ratiociniorum ordinariorum) are different species of inferences of reason of one figure, which arise from the different quality and quantity of the judgments of an inference of reason."}],"386":[{"o":[0,82],"en":"If the conclusion in the first figure affirms universally, then the minor premise also affirms §.383."},{"o":[83,103],"en":"378 and universally §.379."},{"o":[104,189],"en":"369, and the major premise must also affirm §.378 and be universal §.384."},{"o":[190,432],"en":"379. The mood of inferences of reason in the first figure whose conclusion affirms universally is called Barbara, e.g. all truths are useful, all philosophical truths are truths; therefore all philosophical truths are useful."}],"387":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"If the conclusion in the first figure denies universally, the minor premise must affirm §.383, and universally §.369."},{"o":[101,174],"en":"379, and the major premise must deny §.380, and universally §.384.","m":[2,1]},{"o":[175,411],"en":"379. The mood of inferences of reason in the first figure which have a universally negative conclusion is called Celarent, e.g. no vice makes me more perfect, all pride is a vice; therefore no pride makes me more perfect."}],"388":[{"o":[0,98],"en":"If the conclusion in the first figure affirms particularly, the minor premise must affirm §.383. 378, and particularly §.369.","m":[1,2]},{"o":[99,175],"en":"380, and the major premise must affirm §.378, and universally §.384.","m":[3,1]},{"o":[176,407],"en":"377. The mood of inferences of reason in the first figure whose conclusion affirms particularly is called Darii, e.g. all persistent unbelievers are damned, some of the learned are persistent unbelievers; therefore some of the learned are damned."}],"389":[{"o":[0,169],"en":"If the conclusion in the first figure denies particularly, the minor premise must affirm §.383, and particularly §.369, and the major premise must deny §.380, and universally §.384.","m":[2,1]},{"o":[170,457],"en":"377. The mood of inferences of reason in the first figure whose conclusion denies particularly is called Ferio; e.g. no good action is punished by GOD, some mere natural works of human beings are good actions; therefore some mere natural works of human beings are not punished by GOD."},{"o":[458,537],"en":"In the first figure only four moods of inferences of reason are possible §.385-388."}],"390":[{"o":[0,137],"en":"All true judgments which can be conclusions in ordinary inferences of reason are either A or E, or I or O §.301."},{"o":[138,279],"en":"Consequently, in the first figure all these judgments can be concluded §.386-389, and for this reason it is called a perfect figure."},{"o":[280,324],"en":"Hence the remaining figures are not necessary."}],"391":[{"o":[0,224],"en":"If, contrary to the rules so far, one makes inferences of reason whose premises and conclusions are correct, the latter do not follow from the former necessarily, but are only accidentally true."},{"o":[225,303],"en":"Such inferences are therefore no objections against the correctness of these rules."}],"392":[{"o":[0,173],"en":"A conditional inference of reason (ratiocinium hypotheticum, conditionale, connexum) is an inference of reason which infers from the sufficient ground to the consequence §.364."},{"o":[174,392],"en":"Since now in no kind of judgments is the consequence of a judgment affirmed from its condition except in the conditional ones §.305, these inferences of reason have a conditional premise, which is called their major premise."},{"o":[393,521],"en":"It must have a correct consequence §.306, otherwise one infers from the stick in the corner (argumentum a baculo ad angulum)."}],"393":[{"o":[0,133],"en":"In a conditional inference of reason one infers either 1) from the correctness of the former to the correctness of the latter §.364."},{"o":[134,277],"en":"305. Then the minor premise affirms that the first is true, and the conclusion that the last is true (modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum ponens)."},{"o":[278,495],"en":"For example: if there is a providence of GOD, all anxious cares are in vain: now the first is true, therefore also the last; Or 2) from the incorrectness of the latter to the incorrectness of the former §.364."},{"o":[496,780],"en":"305. Then the minor premise affirms the incorrectness of the latter, and the conclusion the incorrectness of the former (modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum tollens), e.g. if there is a blind fate, there are no free actions; now the last is false, therefore also the first."}],"394":[{"o":[0,270],"en":"Because a thing can have several sufficient grounds, one cannot in conditional inferences of reason infer always from the incorrectness of the first to the incorrectness of the last, nor from the correctness of the last to the correctness of the first §.305."}],"395":[{"o":[0,145],"en":"Disjunctive inferences of reason (ratiocinium disiunctivum) are inferences of reason which infer from one opposite to the other §.365."},{"o":[146,241],"en":"Consequently they contain a disjunctive premise, which is called their major premise §.307."},{"o":[242,394],"en":"If this is contrary to one of the rules proved in §.308, it has no consequence, and the disjunctive inference of reason is incorrect in form §.360."}],"396":[{"o":[0,159],"en":"In disjunctive inferences of reason one infers either 1) from the correctness of one member of the disjunction to the incorrectness of the rest §.395."},{"o":[160,317],"en":"365. Then the minor premise must declare one member to be true, and the conclusion the rest to be false (modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum ponendo tollens)."},{"o":[318,516],"en":"E.g. matter can either think or not, now the latter is true, therefore the first is false; or 2) from the incorrectness of all members except one to the correctness of this one §.395."},{"o":[517,735],"en":"365. Then in the minor premise it must be affirmed of all members except one that they are false, and in the conclusion of this one that it is true (modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum tollendo ponens)."},{"o":[736,845],"en":"For example: matter can either think or not; now the first is false, therefore the other is true."}],"397":[{"o":[0,193],"en":"A dilemma (ratiocinium cornutum, crocodillinum, dilemma, trilemma etc.) is a conditional inference of reason whose latter part is a disjunctive judgment in which all members are false."},{"o":[194,386],"en":"The conditional judgment whose latter part is disjunctive is the major premise; the minor premise affirms that the latter is false in its entirety, and the conclusion affirms that the first is false."},{"o":[387,508],"en":"A dilemma must therefore conform at the same time to the rules of conditional and of disjunctive inferences of reason §.392."},{"o":[509,807],"en":"393. 395. For example: if this world were not the best, then either no best world would be possible, or GOD would have had no cognition of it, or he would not have been able to create it, or he would not have been willing to create it; now the last is false in its entirety, therefore also the first."}],"398":[{"o":[0,206],"en":"The inferences of reason which infer from the logical alteration of a judgment to the judgment arisen through the alteration are called immediate inferences (consequentia immediata) §.366."},{"o":[207,347],"en":"For example: all human beings can err, therefore some human beings can also err; or, there is a GOD, therefore it is false that there is no GOD."}],"399":[{"o":[0,285],"en":"An inference of reason is either so constituted that its correct form is manifest, or it is hidden: the former is a formal inference of reason (ratiocinium formale), the latter however a hidden one (ratiocinium crypticum), which can therefore seem to be incorrect in form."},{"o":[286,405],"en":"In order to conceal the logical art, and to avoid pedantry, hidden inferences of reason are to be recommended."}],"400":[{"o":[0,283],"en":"To the hidden inferences of reason belong chiefly the mutilated inferences of reason (enthymema), in which a judgment is omitted, that is, is not thought as distinctly as the rest, because it is entirely certain and so familiar to someone that it occurs to him at once."},{"o":[284,342],"en":"E.g. all human beings can err, therefore I too can err."}],"401":[{"o":[0,229],"en":"To the mutilated inferences of reason belong 1) the contracted inferences of reason (ratiocinium contractum), when one adds merely the middle principal concept to the conclusion, yet in such a way that it does not become a conditional judgment."},{"o":[230,302],"en":"For example: this world is the best, because it has been chosen by GOD."},{"o":[303,569],"en":"2) The inferences by analysis (inductio), which lay down the following major premise as a ground, but omit it: what can be affirmed or denied of every lower concept can be universally affirmed or denied of their higher concept §.263."},{"o":[570,706],"en":"If in the minor premise all lower concepts are cited, it is a complete inference by analysis (inductio completa)."},{"o":[707,836],"en":"For example: the first person of the Godhead is GOD, the second also, the third also; therefore all persons of the Godhead are GOD."},{"o":[837,1108],"en":"3) The inferences by example (exemplum in ratiociniis), when one affirms or denies, of another lower concept which belongs to the same higher concept, that which one affirms or denies of a lower concept for the sake of its higher concept."},{"o":[1109,1197],"en":"For example: human beings can sin, therefore all holy angels can also sin."}],"402":[{"o":[0,164],"en":"An inference of reason which is incorrect in form (paralogismus), when its fault is hidden, is called a fallacy (sophisma, fallacia, captio)."}],"403":[{"o":[0,189],"en":"A fallacy can arise: 1) if we connect separated things in an incorrect manner, and separate connected things in an incorrect manner (sophisma sensus compositi et divisi)."},{"o":[190,340],"en":"For example: where there are three thalers, there are two thalers; now three and two thalers make five thalers, therefore where there are three thalers, there are five thalers."},{"o":[341,435],"en":"2) If a principal concept is taken in a twofold manner (sophisma figurae dictionis)."},{"o":[436,568],"en":"For example: a philosopher is a species of the learned, Leibniz is a philosopher, therefore Leibniz is a species of the learned."}],"404":[{"o":[0,192],"en":"3) If one takes a principal concept once with a restriction and the other time without restriction (fallacia accidentis, seu a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter, aut vice versa)."},{"o":[193,345],"en":"E.g. whoever says you are an animal speaks the truth; now whoever says you are an ass says you are an animal, therefore he speaks the truth."},{"o":[346,421],"en":"4) If one assumes a wrong middle principal concept (fallacia medii)."},{"o":[422,519],"en":"E.g. whoever looks pale studies diligently; now Caius looks pale, therefore he studies diligently."}],"405":[{"o":[0,125],"en":"5) If one takes the judgment which one has proved for that which one was to prove (sophisma heterozeteseos)."},{"o":[126,232],"en":"For example: if one is to prove the immortality of the soul, and one proves its incorruptibility."},{"o":[233,363],"en":"6) If one takes the judgment which one has refuted for that which one was to refute (sophisma ignorationis elenchi)."},{"o":[364,474],"en":"E.g. if, against him who asserts that the soul can die, one proves that it lives eternally."},{"o":[475,602],"en":"7) If a composite judgment is regarded merely as a simple one in an inference of reason (sophisma polyzeteseos)."},{"o":[603,827],"en":"E.g. it is either true that dogs alone among all four-footed animals have reason, or it is not true; if the first, dogs have reason, if the last, all four-footed animals have reason."}],"406":[{"o":[0,87],"en":"An inference of reason consists either of several inferences of reason, or of only one."},{"o":[88,228],"en":"The latter is a simple inference of reason (ratiocinium simplex, probatio simplex), the former however a composite inference of reason (ratiocinium compositum)."},{"o":[229,374],"en":"If an inference of reason is composite, the several inferences of reason of which it consists are either connected or not §.353."},{"o":[375,539],"en":"In the latter case it is a ratiocinium copulatum, e.g. all spirits are immortal, GOD and all human souls are spirits, therefore they are immortal."},{"o":[540,729],"en":"In the former case the premise of the one is the conclusion of the other, and it is called a series of connected inferences of reason (ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, probatio composita)."},{"o":[730,1003],"en":"For example: what accords with the laws of nature makes me more perfect, virtue accords with the laws of nature, therefore virtue makes me more perfect; that which makes me more perfect, to that I am obligated, virtue makes me more perfect, therefore I am obligated to it."}],"407":[{"o":[0,189],"en":"In a series of connected inferences of reason there occur not only some whose conclusions are premises of others, but also such whose premises are conclusions of others §.406."},{"o":[190,272],"en":"The former are called prosyllogisms (prosyllogismus), and the latter episyllogisms (episyllogismus)."},{"o":[273,341],"en":"And some judgments occur twice in such a series §.406."}],"408":[{"o":[0,224],"en":"In order to avoid all confusion in a long series of connected inferences of reason, 1) one divides a long proof into many parts, by proving separately the chief premises as particular theorems."},{"o":[225,311],"en":"2) One does not connect with one another purely ordinary and formal inferences of reason."},{"o":[312,396],"en":"3) One proves either only the major premises of the prosyllogisms, or only their minor premises."},{"o":[397,490],"en":"4) One derives the conclusions from contracted inferences of reason §.401 (epicherema)."},{"o":[491,614],"en":"5) One omits all judgments that occur twice §.407, and that is called a heaped inference of reason (sorites)."},{"o":[615,807],"en":"E.g. youth accords with the laws of nature, what accords with the laws of nature, that makes me more perfect, what makes me more perfect, to that I am obligated; therefore I am obligated to virtue."}],"409":[{"o":[0,226],"en":"If the inferences of reason of which the heaped inference of reason consists are of the first figure, and the minor premises of the episyllogisms are omitted, it is a common heaped inference of reason (sorites communis)."},{"o":[227,263],"en":"E.g. the example cited just above."},{"o":[264,362],"en":"But if it consists of purely conditional premises, it is called a conditional one (sorites hypotheticus)."},{"o":[363,631],"en":"E.g. if the human being has a limited understanding, he has confused representations; if he has confused representations, he can err; if he can err, he can sin: therefore, if the human being has a limited understanding, he can sin."}],"410":[{"o":[0,155],"en":"If in a series of connected inferences of reason one or more judgments are omitted, a leap in the proof is committed (saltus in probando)."},{"o":[156,300],"en":"The omitted judgments are either certain to him who is to be convinced by the proof, and occur to him easily; or not."},{"o":[301,422],"en":"In the first case the leap is legitimate (saltus legitimus), in the other however illegitimate (saltus illegitimus)."}],"411":[{"o":[0,170],"en":"If a conclusion is derived from premises which are just as uncertain as it itself, the proofs are begged (petitio principii seu quaesiti.)"},{"o":[171,320],"en":"But if a conclusion is assumed as its own premise, this fault is called the return in the proof (circulus in probando)."}],"412":[{"o":[0,280],"en":"A proof proves too little (probatio minus probans) if the whole conclusion does not become certain through it; but if something follows from a proof besides the conclusion which is false or even too different from the conclusion, it proves too much (probatio plus probans)."}],"413":[{"o":[0,109],"en":"Through inferences of reason one can 1) attain the most distinct certainty that is possible to a human being §.355."},{"o":[110,329],"en":"2) Resist errors most easily; 3) discover new truths; and 4) discover all proofs that are necessary in order to erect a learned system, etc. On the method of learned cognition."}],"414":[{"o":[0,79],"en":"Method (methodus) is a more noticeable or greater order of thoughts."},{"o":[80,311],"en":"Insofar as several thoughts are together in one and the same manner, or follow upon one another, to that extent there is an order among them, which arises when different thoughts are coordinated with one another according to one and the same rules."}],"415":[{"o":[0,41],"en":"Method is, in cognition §.414."},{"o":[42,171],"en":"11, and so is either an order of common cognition, the common method (methodus vulgaris), or of rational cognition §.17."},{"o":[172,221],"en":"18, the rational method (methodus rationalis)."},{"o":[222,389],"en":"To the former belongs the aesthetic method §.19, to the latter, however, the learned method (methodus erudita, logica, philosophica), the method of learned cognition §.21."}],"416":[{"o":[0,154],"en":"The learned method promotes 1) the distinctness of learned cognition §.142 n. 4. 2) Truth in a system and thoroughness §.105."},{"o":[155,224],"en":"3) The unity and thoroughgoing connection of a system."},{"o":[225,316],"en":"4) The perfection of learned cognition: for all order is a perfection."},{"o":[317,373],"en":"5) The retention of truths by memory §.414."}],"417":[{"o":[0,187],"en":"The more thoughts are connected with one another, and the more and the more important common rules according to which this connection takes place, the greater and more perfect is the method §.414."},{"o":[188,313],"en":"Consequently, for the greatest perfection of a learned cognition it is required that it be as methodical as possible §.416."}],"418":[{"o":[0,114],"en":"A method connects thoughts either according to only a single common rule, or according to several."},{"o":[115,221],"en":"The former is a simple method (methodus simplex), the latter, however, a composite method (methodus composita)."},{"o":[222,260],"en":"The latter is more perfect than the former §.417."}],"419":[{"o":[0,337],"en":"The difference of methods derives from the difference of the rules according to which the thoughts are coordinated with one another §.414, and the difference of the rules flows from the difference of the cognitive faculties, and from the different constitution of the cognition that is to be thought according to a method."}],"420":[{"o":[0,76],"en":"Since learned cognition is a distinct cognition from grounds §.21."},{"o":[77,219],"en":"17, the thoughts in the learned method must follow upon one another in such a way that their connection thereby becomes distinctly certain §.415."},{"o":[220,274],"en":"419. This is the fundamental law of the learned method."}],"421":[{"o":[0,93],"en":"The method of dogmatic truths is different from the method of historical ones §.419."},{"o":[94,238],"en":"104. In the former the thoughts must be proved from one another, either through a demonstration or through an insufficient proof §.191."},{"o":[239,380],"en":"If the first is the case, it is called a demonstrative or scientific method (methodus demonstrativa, scientifica, apodictica)."}],"422":[{"o":[0,219],"en":"In the learned method of dogmatic truths, only thoughts are coordinated with one another that are either the grounds of the truth, distinctness and certainty of others, or the consequences, or both at once §.421."},{"o":[220,227],"en":"420.15."},{"o":[228,300],"en":"Thus the grounds are either put before the consequences or placed after them."},{"o":[301,406],"en":"The former is the synthetic method (methodus synthetica), the latter, however, the analytic method (methodus analytica)."},{"o":[407,530],"en":"Consequently the learned method is either synthetic or analytic, whether it be demonstrative or not §.421."}],"423":[{"o":[0,121],"en":"The analytic method is very inconvenient if one wants to treat or learn an extensive system according to it."},{"o":[122,525],"en":"But it is very well suited 1) when one wants to draw up an outline for a system, in order afterwards to treat it according to the synthetic method; 2) when one wants to discover, from the consequences and conclusions, the grounds and premises; 3) when one wants to repeat a system already learned often; 4) when one wants to think through properly the individual shorter parts of a system §.422."}],"424":[{"o":[0,173],"en":"The synthetic method is always attended with many inconveniences if one makes use of it in such cases where the analytic has been recommended §.423."},{"o":[174,465],"en":"But it has great advantages if one makes use of it: 1) when one wants to think through and treat a system already discovered, considered as a whole; 2) when one wants to learn it; 3) when one wants to discover the consequences and conclusions from the grounds and premises §.422."}],"425":[{"o":[0,97],"en":"In the synthetic method the grounds must always be thought before the consequences §.422."},{"o":[98,262],"en":"Consequently 1) the concepts that are marks of other concepts must be explained before the others §.115, and thus the higher before the lower §.261."},{"o":[263,346],"en":"2) The unprovable truths must precede the provable ones §.314-317."},{"o":[347,478],"en":"3) The premises must be thought before the conclusions §.356, and thus the prosyllogisms before the episyllogisms §.407."},{"o":[479,574],"en":"4) Those provable truths from which others follow must precede these."},{"o":[575,638],"en":"In the analytic method everything stands the reverse way §.422."}],"426":[{"o":[0,181],"en":"The synthetic method, which has merely the aim of promoting the mathematical certainty of cognition §.161, is called the mathematical method (methodus mathematica)."},{"o":[182,445],"en":"It must therefore not only conform most exactly to the rules of the synthetic §.425, but by virtue of them one also either omits everything that is not indispensably required for mathematical certainty, or puts it into remarks §.340."},{"o":[446,677],"en":"It is of great use in very difficult demonstrations, especially for beginners, but if one accustoms oneself to it alone, one acquires a merely learned cognition §.161, and it is accordingly not to be advised §.40."}],"427":[{"o":[0,87],"en":"Art (ars) is a sum of rules that are thought according to an order."},{"o":[88,219],"en":"The artificial method (methodus artificialis, scholae) is the method insofar as it is learned and practiced through art."},{"o":[220,393],"en":"The natural method (methodus naturalis) is the method insofar as it is not artificial, but follows from the nature of the cognition and of him who thinks."},{"o":[394,508],"en":"The mixed method (methodus mixta) is the natural method insofar as the artificial comes to its aid."}],"428":[{"o":[0,200],"en":"Among the artificial learned methods the best are 1) the inferential method (methodus syllogistica), when one analyzes all proofs into nothing but formal inferences, and commits no leaps."},{"o":[201,238],"en":"It does a beginner good service."},{"o":[239,307],"en":"Otherwise it is too extensive, too tedious and not distinct enough."},{"o":[308,532],"en":"2) The method by tables (methodus tabellaris), when one arranges together all the members of the divisions and parts of the whole so that it becomes evident to what higher concept and whole each thought belongs."},{"o":[534,764],"en":"It promotes the distinct concept of an entire system, and memory; but it often falls into the forced, attention is overburdened, and thoroughness cannot be properly attained through it."},{"o":[765,962],"en":"3) The method according to a partition of an entire system, when one lets the judgments follow one another according to the difference of their subjects; hence chapters, sections, etc. arise."},{"o":[963,1099],"en":"When it is combined with the synthetic, it does not hinder thoroughness, and provides the benefit of the method by tables."}],"429":[{"o":[0,144],"en":"If one wants to treat a system, this must be done according to the synthetic, and in some cases according to the analytic method §.423."},{"o":[145,212],"en":"424, for they suit the nature of a system §.420."},{"o":[213,463],"en":"Now whatever of all the remaining artificial methods can be combined with it, whether to promote distinctness and certainty, or memory, or to increase order, that one should connect with the synthetic and analytic method."},{"o":[464,642],"en":"Consequently, affectation in method (affectatio in methodo) is a fault 1) when the artificial method is good for nothing, is contrary to nature, and is wholly arbitrary."},{"o":[643,754],"en":"2) When one binds oneself too exactly to a simple method, and will never make an exception from it."},{"o":[755,828],"en":"3) When one observes the rules of method far too exactly and anxiously."}],"430":[{"o":[0,64],"en":"The learned method must be natural and unaffected §.429."},{"o":[65,171],"en":"It must therefore conform to the nature of learned cognition, and be observed without anxious effort."},{"o":[172,250],"en":"Consequently it must conform to the head of him who wants to think according to it."},{"o":[251,749],"en":"Whoever has the gift for it can therefore make use 1) of the Socratic method (methodus socratica), by virtue of which the thoughts follow upon one another as they occasion one another, as questions and answers in the conversation of several persons; 2) of the Platonic method (methodus platonica), by virtue of which the thoughts follow upon one another as in a free speech, so that all kinds of learned thoughts are mixed together, without one's making formal and manifest inferences."}],"431":[{"o":[0,310],"en":"If one wants to treat a system according to the synthetic method, one must 1) lay only One truth at the foundation, from which everything else is derived, so that the system obtains a unity and a thoroughgoing connection, so that it contains neither more nor less than is necessary."},{"o":[311,486],"en":"2) One lays a partition at the foundation, and divides the system into several sections, yet in such a way that no exception from the synthetic method is thereby made."},{"o":[487,826],"en":"3) The learned thoughts themselves must follow upon one another as follows. a) First come the definitions; b) then follow either the unprovable truths or the intuitive judgments; c) then the provable truths. d) The corollaries and remarks are scattered throughout, wherever they are necessary and useful §.420."},{"o":[827,831],"en":"425."}],"432":[{"o":[0,241],"en":"The method of historical truth §.421 must conform to the nature of historical truths §.419; consequently one must think them in the order in which their objects are connected with one another either according to space or time."},{"o":[242,596],"en":"To this belongs, then, the chronological method (methodus chronologica), when one thinks the events in just the sequence in which they actually followed upon one another; and the geographical method (methodus geographica), by virtue of which one thinks the events that happen, or have happened, in a place according to the chronological method."},{"o":[597,830],"en":"Since the actual events of the world also cohere, and a human being can often see this connection, one can, at least often, also make use of the rational method in the case of historical truths §.415."}],"433":[{"o":[0,202],"en":"The best learned composite method can seem a disorder to a human being who does not know its rules, or who has accustomed himself to another method, or only to a simple one."},{"o":[203,282],"en":"But for that reason no well-founded reproach can be made against such a method."}],"434":[{"o":[0,128],"en":"A doctrine (doctrina) is a sum of dogmatic truths that have one and the same object."},{"o":[129,212],"en":"A discipline (disciplina) is a doctrine insofar as it is cognized methodically."},{"o":[213,293],"en":"A demonstrated discipline is a science (scientia obiective spectata)."},{"o":[294,506],"en":"Learned cognition is always at the beginning a doctrine, then one gives it the shape of a discipline, and finally the shape of a science, and then it has attained its greatest perfection."}],"435":[{"o":[0,76],"en":"Whoever thinks, thinks either according to a method or according to none §.414."},{"o":[77,225],"en":"The former thinks methodically (methodice cogitare, methodicum, acroamaticum, disciplinale in cognitione), the latter, however, tumultuarily (tumultuaria cognitio)."},{"o":[226,289],"en":"No tumultuary cognition can be properly learned §.416."}],"436":[{"o":[0,151],"en":"Meditating (meditatio) is that occupation of our cognitive faculties through which we reflect on a thing according to the rules of a method."},{"o":[152,340],"en":"If it takes place according to the rules of the learned method, it is a learned meditating (meditatio erudita), which is as manifold as there are different kinds of the learned method."},{"o":[341,422],"en":"Without this learned meditating we cannot attain any learned cognition."}],"437":[{"o":[0,265],"en":"Whoever wants to meditate in a learned way 1) let him choose an object to which he directs his attention; 2) let him think, one after another, the marks of it, which make up its definition, the unprovable, and finally the provable predicates of it."},{"o":[266,493],"en":"These one has either already learned, and then one does well to write while meditating; or one wants to learn them, and then one must read in a book, or listen to oral presentation; or one wants first to discover them."},{"o":[494,732],"en":"3) One should examine whether what one thinks is a concept, a judgment, a theorem, etc., and consider it until one has satisfied all the rules that logic prescribes for each kind of learned thoughts."}],"438":[{"o":[0,256],"en":"It is a disgrace when, out of pedantry, one despises all ways of thinking that are not cut over the last of that method with which one has fallen in love, and when, like a charlatan, one makes too much ado of the method one loves."},{"o":[256,283],"en":"On the learned presentation."},{"o":[284,312],"en":"On the use of words."}],"439":[{"o":[0,199],"en":"Logical characteristic (logica characteristica heuristica) is the science of the rules that one must observe if one wants to designate learned cognition in a skilful way."},{"o":[200,368],"en":"Logical interpretation (hermeneutica logica) is the science of the rules for how one is to cognize, in a learned way, the things designated from the signs."}],"440":[{"o":[0,227],"en":"A sign (signum, symbolum) is a means by whose use the actuality of another thing can be cognized, which other thing is called the thing designated or the meaning (signatum, significatus)."},{"o":[228,391],"en":"An expression (terminus) is a sign of cognition; the expressions that ordinarily consist in a human voice are called words (vocabulum)."},{"o":[392,522],"en":"The cognition designated through the expressions and words is called their meaning (significatus vocabuli et termini)."}],"441":[{"o":[0,151],"en":"That meaning which he who uses the word wants to designate by the word is the true meaning (significatus hermeneutice verus)."},{"o":[152,235],"en":"Every other meaning, however, is the false (significatus hermeneutice falsus)."},{"o":[236,323],"en":"The true meaning can be a false concept, and the false a true concept."}],"442":[{"o":[0,105],"en":"Discourse (oratio) is a series of words that designates representations that are connected with one another."},{"o":[106,365],"en":"The series of representations is called the sense of the discourse (sensus orationis), which is either a true sense (sensus hermeneutice verus), if the speaker wanted to designate it, or a false (sensus hermeneutice falsus), if it is not the true."},{"o":[366,543],"en":"Presentation (propositio) is the production of a cognition in others by means of a discourse, whether it be an oral or a written presentation."}],"443":[{"o":[0,103],"en":"As many kinds of cognition as there are, so many kinds of discourse and of presentation there are also §.442."},{"o":[104,217],"en":"There are therefore common discourses, aesthetic discourses, rational discourses, and likewise such kinds of presentation §.17."},{"o":[218,224],"en":"18.19."},{"o":[225,477],"en":"A learned discourse (oratio erudita) is a discourse whose sense is a learned cognition §.21, and we present something in a learned way (erudite proponere) when, through a learned discourse, we produce a learned cognition in others."}],"444":[{"o":[0,200],"en":"The learned discourse must, together with all its parts, be so constituted that the highest perfection of learned cognition is not hindered, but promoted and designated §.442."},{"o":[201,353],"en":"One must therefore observe in it 1) all aesthetic rules of discourse that are not contrary to the logical perfection of cognition §.34."},{"o":[354,476],"en":"2) Avoid all aesthetic perfections that are contrary to the required logical perfection of discourse §.34."}],"445":[{"o":[0,227],"en":"A discourse is either merely learned (oratio mere erudita), if it designates nothing further than a merely learned cognition, but in the opposite case it is not a merely learned discourse (oratio non mere erudita)."},{"o":[228,283],"en":"And likewise the learned presentation is different §.443."},{"o":[284,318],"en":"The latter are the best §.40."},{"o":[319,322],"en":"32."}],"446":[{"o":[0,49],"en":"The learned expression must be extensive §.25."},{"o":[50,177],"en":"444. Consequently 1) a scholar must know as many expressions as are required to designate all his learned thoughts."},{"o":[178,313],"en":"2) One must know all the necessary technical terms of one's main science, and in case of need it is permitted to invent new expressions."},{"o":[314,441],"en":"A technical term (terminus technicus) is an expression that may not be used at all outside an art or discipline."},{"o":[442,551],"en":"3) One must know all expressions of equal meaning (synonyma), or all expressions that signify one and the same."},{"o":[552,673],"en":"4) The learned expression must designate everything that one thinks, and designate neither more nor less than one wants to say."},{"o":[674,806],"en":"5) One must know all meanings of an expression that it has already received through common and learned usage of speaking."},{"o":[807,1004],"en":"The common usage of speaking (usus loquendi) is the agreement in common life by virtue of which people who speak a language connect one or more meanings with certain words."},{"o":[1005,1162],"en":"The learned usage of speaking (receptus terminorum significatus) is just such an agreement of those who apply themselves to a discipline."}],"447":[{"o":[0,105],"en":"The learned expression must be great, important and decorous enough for learned cognition §.444."},{"o":[106,272],"en":"26. Consequently one must avoid in learned presentation all base, ridiculous and vulgar expressions that are in use only among the rabble."}],"448":[{"o":[0,46],"en":"The learned expression must be correct §.444."},{"o":[47,219],"en":"27. A true expression (terminus verus) must correctly designate its true meaning §.441: an expression that is not true is a false expression (terminus falsus)."}],"449":[{"o":[0,347],"en":"An expression is false: 1) if its meaning is a false concept, and he who uses the expression is convinced of the incorrectness of the meaning; 2) if we believe we understand an expression when we nevertheless do not understand it; such a false expression is an empty expression, or an empty word (terminus inanis)."},{"o":[348,443],"en":"We understand an expression (terminum intelligere) when we cognize its meaning from it."},{"o":[444,714],"en":"An empty word arises: a) when we take the concept of the word itself for its meaning §.440. b) When the meaning of the word is an erroneous or deceptive concept (conceptus deceptor), a concept that we believe we have, when we nevertheless have none."},{"o":[715,777],"en":"3) When it does not correctly designate a true concept §.448."}],"450":[{"o":[0,212],"en":"A familiar expression (terminus familiaris) is an expression that we have grown accustomed to, or that we believe we understand, although we do not understand it, because we do not attend to its meaning."},{"o":[213,482],"en":"Such expressions can be true expressions, but in using them we stand in the greatest danger of hatching empty expressions, if we connect with one another a pair of familiar expressions whose meanings are impossible or contradict one another §.449."},{"o":[483,624],"en":"Hence people can speak of nothing and yet seem to understand one another, namely if they use expressions that are familiar to them."}],"451":[{"o":[0,274],"en":"Although in learned discourses one cannot avoid the false expressions of the first kind §.449 when one must refute errors; one must nevertheless avoid all other false and empty expressions, because thoughtlessness and errors arise from their use."},{"o":[275,459],"en":"To that end one must use no expression 1) whose meaning one has not cognized, and 2) whose meaning one has not examined as to whether it is a true or false concept."}],"452":[{"o":[0,107],"en":"A coarse expression (terminus crassus) is an expression whose meaning is a coarse cognition §.102."},{"o":[108,234],"en":"The use of empty expressions in learned discourses is the word-mongering of scholars, in which one sells words for things."},{"o":[235,422],"en":"E.g. if a scholar is to give the ground of a thing, and he has designated the thing with an expression that he afterwards adduces as the ground, he sells words for things."},{"o":[423,461],"en":"All this one must avoid §.451."}],"453":[{"o":[0,43],"en":"The learned expression must be clear §.444."},{"o":[44,190],"en":"28. One must therefore not only be able to distinguish it properly from other expressions oneself, but its meaning must also be sufficiently clear."},{"o":[191,465],"en":"An obscure expression (terminus obscurus) has an obscure meaning, a clear one (terminus clarus) a clear one, etc. An obscure expression is for that reason not an empty one §.449, and an expression that is obscure to me is for that reason not absolutely obscure also to other people §.126."}],"454":[{"o":[0,82],"en":"From an expression its meaning can either be clearly cognized or not."},{"o":[83,212],"en":"The former is an intelligible expression (terminus intelligibilis), the latter, however, an unintelligible expression (terminus non intelligibilis)."},{"o":[213,566],"en":"Although in a learned discourse one cannot use nothing but expressions that are intelligible to all people; one must nevertheless use nothing but such expressions as can be intelligible even to people of mediocre understanding, who have a mediocre command of the language, if they pay only mediocre attention to the discourse §.453."}],"455":[{"o":[0,234],"en":"Whoever wants to make use of the most intelligible expressions in a learned discourse §.454, 1) must prefer the better-known and more usual expressions to the less known and less usual, if they are otherwise equal to one another."},{"o":[235,870],"en":"2) He must always connect with the expression that meaning which is connected with it by the usage of speaking, in common life as well as in the disciplines §.446, until necessity forces him to the contrary; 3) he must, if the usage of speaking is manifold, connect with the expressions the most usual meanings (significatus famosissimus), that is, those that are connected with the expression by most people in most cases; 4) if in a certain case the three preceding rules do not suffice, then one should explain that meaning in which one takes an expression §.268."}],"456":[{"o":[0,45],"en":"The learned expression must be certain §.444."},{"o":[46,225],"en":"29. A certain expression (terminus certus) is so constituted that one knows not only for certain that it has a meaning, but also that it has precisely this and no other meaning."},{"o":[226,379],"en":"An expression is uncertain (terminus incertus) if it is not certain; and through the use of uncertain expressions the discourse is put on screws."}],"457":[{"o":[0,171],"en":"An ambiguous expression (terminus ambiguus, homonymus, vagus) does not always have One meaning; as soon as it gets one, its meaning is fixed (terminus fixus)."},{"o":[172,280],"en":"The ambiguity of the expression hinders its certainty §.456, one must therefore prevent all ambiguity."},{"o":[281,405],"en":"And if the usage of speaking does not suffice for this §.446, one must fix the meanings through logical definitions."}],"458":[{"o":[0,157],"en":"The learned expression must please so much that one can thereby be attracted to cognize from it the cognition lying hidden beneath it §.444.30."},{"o":[158,325],"en":"Consequently it must be adorned with all perfections that are not contrary to learned cognition, so that one reads or hears it gladly and with pleasure."}],"459":[{"o":[0,193],"en":"In the use of words in logical definitions four further rules are to be observed: 1) One must also observe the usage of speaking in definitions, as long as it is possible §.455."},{"o":[194,627],"en":"2) One must put into the discourse that designates the definition neither the expression of the defined concept, nor its equivalent expressions; for then the definition would be no more distinct than the defined concept §.275, 446. 3) A definition must be expressed with as few words as can be done without detriment to distinctness; so that attention not be distracted by too many words."}],"460":[{"o":[0,267],"en":"That meaning for the sake of which a word was invented and is used, even if it should otherwise have no meaning at all, is called the proper meaning (significatus proprius), the other meanings are improper (significatus improprius)."},{"o":[268,465],"en":"If one uses a word to designate the former, it is a proper word (terminus proprius); but if one uses it to designate the latter, it is an improper word (terminus improprius)."},{"o":[466,671],"en":"In an improper word we always think, besides the improper meaning, the proper one, although one could have a detailed concept of each without thinking of the other."},{"o":[672,1096],"en":"Hence one must 4) avoid in the expression of logical definitions all improper expressions, because otherwise the definition would be too extensive §.270, and if we sometimes cannot find proper expressions in the language, we must first transform an improper word into a proper one, through the definition of its improper meaning, before we use it in another definition."}],"461":[{"o":[0,222],"en":"If one is to explain the meaning of a word of many meanings, we may explain one among its several meanings that pleases us, if the several meanings are usual to an equal degree."},{"o":[223,283],"en":"And in this respect definitions are often arbitrary."}],"462":[{"o":[0,95],"en":"A judgment that is designated through expressions is called a proposition (propositio, enunciatio)."},{"o":[96,231],"en":"A proposition in which not all concepts are designated with particular expressions is called a hidden proposition (propositio cryptica)."},{"o":[232,321],"en":"The expressions that designate the quantity of the judgment are called the signs of quantity."},{"o":[322,422],"en":"In a proposition the signs of quantity are either present or not (propositio definita, et indefinita)."},{"o":[423,647],"en":"The latter are understood as universal by linguistic usage, and whoever, therefore, does not want to be misunderstood must not omit the signs of quantity in the propositions that he does not want understood as universal."},{"o":[648,743],"en":"A proposition is understood when all the expressions found in it are understood §.449."}],"463":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"An inference of reason, when it is designated through expressions, is called a syllogistic discourse (syllogismus)."},{"o":[101,212],"en":"One should beware of presenting the syllogistic discourses always in one order, and with the same technical terms."},{"o":[213,394],"en":"And if one hides or truncates the syllogistic discourses, one should be guided by the hearer, so that he is not led astray and can recall all the omitted propositions."},{"o":[395,574],"en":"Those fallacies that rest merely on the ambiguity of expressions (sophisma ambiguitatis, amphiboliae) are hardly worth the trouble of mentioning."},{"o":[574,604],"en":"On the learned style of writing."}],"464":[{"o":[0,142],"en":"The learned style of writing (stilus eruditus, philosophicus), is the agreement, or similarity, of the manner and way in which one speaks in a learned fashion."}],"465":[{"o":[0,67],"en":"Since the learned style of writing is a part of the learned discourse §.464."},{"o":[68,189],"en":"443, it has the same aim as the latter, namely to promote learned cognition in oneself and in others."},{"o":[190,422],"en":"A good learned style of writing (stilus eruditus perfectus) must therefore promote learned cognition and its perfections, while that which hinders them is a bad learned style of writing (stilus eruditus imperfectus)."}],"466":[{"o":[0,301],"en":"To the perfections of the learned style of writing belong 1) all its constitutions without which the perfections of learned cognition cannot be maintained; 2) all remaining beauties of the style of writing, if they are not contrary to the perfection of learned cognition."},{"o":[302,355],"en":"Without these the style of writing would be merely learned."},{"o":[356,756],"en":"To the imperfections of the learned style of writing belong: 1) all its constitutions by which the perfection of learned cognition is hindered, even should they be beauties; 2) all constitutions by which imperfections in learned cognition are caused; 3) all imperfections that cannot be tolerated in any style of writing §.465."}],"467":[{"o":[0,176],"en":"The learned style of writing must be arranged in such a way that through it the distinctness of the cognition of truth from grounds is promoted in the best manner §.465."},{"o":[177,230],"en":"This is the fundamental law of the learned style of writing."}],"468":[{"o":[0,53],"en":"1) The learned style of writing must be distinct §.467."},{"o":[54,259],"en":"The distinctness of the learned style of writing (perspicuitas stili eruditi) is its perfection by virtue of which it does not hinder, but promotes the distinctness of the cognition designated."},{"o":[260,345],"en":"The opposite imperfection is its obscurity (obscuritas stili eruditi)."},{"o":[346,643],"en":"To make the style of writing distinct, one must 1) use nothing but clear expressions §.453-455; 2) one must not speak too briefly, but copiously; 3) one must connect the parts of the discourse with one another so that the connection of the thoughts can be cognized from it most distinctly and easily."},{"o":[644,956],"en":"Consequently one must a) avoid all rare and difficult constructions; b) not reject the constructions; c) not omit too frequently the expressions that designate the connections of thoughts; d) not make too many insertions; e) not make the periods far too long."},{"o":[957,1045],"en":"A proposition that is not a part of another proposition is called a period (punctum)."},{"o":[1046,1109],"en":"One must therefore mix shorter and longer periods with one another."}],"469":[{"o":[0,75],"en":"2) The learned style of writing must, as far as is possible, be pure §.467."},{"o":[75,220],"en":"The purity of the learned style of writing (puritas stili eruditi) consists in its agreement with the improved nature of the language."},{"o":[221,316],"en":"The opposite imperfection is its impurity (impuritas stili eruditi)."},{"o":[317,482],"en":"Since a pure style of writing pleases, it attracts the attention of the reader and hearer, and it therefore promotes the distinctness of learned cognition §.142."}],"470":[{"o":[0,124],"en":"If the purity of the style of writing should hinder its distinctness, one must make an exception from the former §.35."},{"o":[125,234],"en":"21. Such a necessary impurity is only an apparent imperfection of the learned style of writing §.466."}],"471":[{"o":[0,553],"en":"For the sake of the purity of the learned style of writing one must avoid: 1) all hybrid words (vox hybrida), whose parts are taken from different languages; 2) all grammatical faults; 3) all barbarous expressions; 4) all expressions from foreign languages, and foreign constructions, unless they have already received the right of citizenship; 5) all obsolete expressions and constructions; 6) all new words and technical terms; 7) all multiplicity of expressions, if one can make use of One expression instead of them, although it is impure §.469."},{"o":[554,620],"en":"From all these rules one can make exceptions for the sake of §.470."}],"472":[{"o":[0,413],"en":"With regard to the purity of the learned style of writing there must be avoided: 1) the negligent and heedless barbarism of pedantic scholars, by virtue of which they neglect the purity of the style of writing altogether; 2) the far too great love of purity of the style of writing, when one seeks to maintain the purity quite forcedly, even to the detriment of the perfection of learned cognition §.466."}],"473":[{"o":[0,72],"en":"3) The learned style of writing must, as far as is possible, be fitting."},{"o":[73,224],"en":"The fittingness of the style of writing (concinnitas stili) consists in the proportioned agreement of the parts of a discourse, e.g. when one forms periods."},{"o":[225,481],"en":"In a merely learned discourse this perfection cannot be obtained, but in a learned discourse that is not merely learned, one must, in those places where one at the same time thinks beautifully, also take care for this perfection as far as possible §.467."},{"o":[482,532],"en":"445, because it makes the expression more agreeable §.458."}],"474":[{"o":[0,78],"en":"4) The learned style of writing must be elegant, as far as is possible §.467."},{"o":[79,282],"en":"The elegance of the style of writing (ornatus stili eruditi) consists in this, when in place of certain expressions others are used by which just the same concepts, but in a more beautiful way, are designated."},{"o":[283,492],"en":"In those parts where the discourse is merely learned, no elegant style of writing is possible; but if the learned discourse must at the same time be beautiful, the learned style of writing must also be elegant §.445."}],"475":[{"o":[0,86],"en":"5) The learned style of writing must, as far as is possible, be euphonious §.467."},{"o":[87,217],"en":"The euphony of the style of writing (sonoritas stili) consists in that perfection by virtue of which it pleases the ears §.458."},{"o":[218,354],"en":"One must seek this perfection especially in those places of a learned discourse where the discourse is not merely learned §.445."}],"476":[{"o":[0,87],"en":"Nothing but simple propositions, and far too extensive periods, obscure the style of writing §.468."},{"o":[88,198],"en":"In a learned discourse, therefore, also for the sake of the fittingness and euphony of the style of writing §.473."},{"o":[199,393],"en":"475, the simple and composite periods must alternate with moderately beautiful periods, according as the thoughts designated are merely learned, or at the same time more or less beautiful."}],"477":[{"o":[0,81],"en":"6) The learned style of writing must, as far as is possible, be suitable §.467."},{"o":[81,306],"en":"The suitability of the style of writing (congruentia stili) is that perfection by virtue of which the style of writing suits the cognition designated, the speaking person and the hearers, and conforms to them."},{"o":[307,457],"en":"Consequently the learned style of writing must be important enough for learned cognition §.447, and thus not ridiculous, vulgar, base."},{"o":[458,548],"en":"And it must be natural to him who makes use of it, and thus not forced."}],"478":[{"o":[0,282],"en":"For the sake of the suitability of the learned style of writing it must 1) in those places where one expresses merely learned thoughts, not be so pure, fitting, elegant and euphonious; as 2) in those places where thoughts are expressed that are at the same time beautiful §.477."},{"o":[282,307],"en":"On a learned discourse."}],"479":[{"o":[0,136],"en":"The learned presentation is either an oral presentation, or a written one, that is, a book or a writing (liber, scriptum)."},{"o":[137,353],"en":"Whoever gives a learned presentation is a teacher, and whoever attains his learned cognition from the presentation of another learns it, and is a pupil, whether he be a reader or a hearer."}],"480":[{"o":[0,216],"en":"The sense of a learned discourse must be a learned cognition that conforms to all the rules of its perfection §.10-438, and the expression must be so constituted as we have shown thus far §.439-478."},{"o":[217,411],"en":"We must therefore present in a learned discourse all the parts of learned cognition, and in the sequence in which they must follow upon one another if a learned cognition is to arise."}],"481":[{"o":[0,73],"en":"A learned discourse must be extensive in a detailed way §.480."},{"o":[74,80],"en":"41-65."},{"o":[81,393],"en":"It must therefore be neither too brief (oratio erudita nimis brevis), if it says less about the object than suffices for the most perfect learned cognition; nor far too extensive (oratio erudita nimis prolixa), if it says more than is necessary for the attainment of a perfect learned cognition."}],"482":[{"o":[0,150],"en":"A learned discourse can be too extensive with regard to one, too brief with regard to another, and detailed with regard to a third §.481."},{"o":[151,337],"en":"Consequently the less capacity a pupil possesses, the less he has already learned, and the less he applies himself to an object, the briefer the learned presentation must be for him."},{"o":[338,441],"en":"A pupil of the opposite kind must be instructed through a more extensive presentation."}],"483":[{"o":[0,50],"en":"A learned discourse must be important enough §.480."},{"o":[51,57],"en":"66-91."},{"o":[58,66],"en":"447.477."},{"o":[67,139],"en":"Its object must therefore be no worthless trifle."}],"484":[{"o":[0,66],"en":"A learned discourse must be as correct as possible §.480.92-114."},{"o":[67,75],"en":"448-452."},{"o":[76,213],"en":"A scholar who himself errs, and moreover spreads his errors, is a seducer of other people, and doubly imperfect."},{"o":[214,313],"en":"One must therefore examine one's own cognition most carefully beforehand, before one presents it."}],"485":[{"o":[0,61],"en":"A learned discourse must be distinct and intelligible §.480."},{"o":[62,70],"en":"114-154."},{"o":[71,293],"en":"468. It is therefore a fault 1) if the teacher speaks without understanding, or if his discourse has no sense at all; 2) if his words and constructions are obscure and too difficult; 3) if he himself cannot be understood."},{"o":[294,467],"en":"A speaker is understood (proponens intelligitur) if one connects with his words just the series of representations that he wanted to designate by them."},{"o":[468,544],"en":"One can therefore often understand the discourse without understanding the speaker §.449."},{"o":[545,782],"en":"A teacher must therefore either explain himself in what meanings he takes his words, or he must connect with them no other concepts than those of which he knows that the pupil already has them, and connects them with the words."}],"486":[{"o":[0,235],"en":"If a teacher presents confused cognition, his pupil must either already possess it, and he brings it to him through the presentation only into memory, or he must impart it to him in another way than through the presentation."},{"o":[236,326],"en":"Distinct cognition can be produced in others only through the presentation §.14."},{"o":[327,389],"en":"A teacher must therefore possess a very distinct cognition."}],"487":[{"o":[0,78],"en":"The learned presentation becomes distinct: 1) through a distinct style of writing §.468."},{"o":[79,133],"en":"2) Through the observation of a convenient method §.416."},{"o":[134,205],"en":"3) When one illustrates abstract cognition through beautiful examples."},{"o":[206,386],"en":"4) One should present a matter, if it deserves it on account of its importance, in a manifold and varied way, and make use of aesthetic illustrative arguments."},{"o":[387,489],"en":"5) The agreeable and lively in the presentation promotes attention, and thus also distinctness."}],"488":[{"o":[0,61],"en":"A learned discourse must be convincing and thorough §.480."},{"o":[62,70],"en":"155-215."},{"o":[71,488],"en":"It is therefore a fault 1) if a teacher offers no proofs at all, when he presents a provable cognition; 2) if his proofs are not certain in the degree, and of the kind of certainty, that the cognition to be presented requires; 3) if he throws blue smoke at his pupils (fucus logicus), that is, if instead of conviction he produces in them a mere persuasion §.184."}],"489":[{"o":[0,45],"en":"A learned discourse must be practical §.480."},{"o":[46,54],"en":"216-248."},{"o":[55,177],"en":"Everything that we have so far remarked about the learned discourse must also be applied to the learned presentation §.443."}],"490":[{"o":[0,179],"en":"The gift of learned presentation (donum didacticum) is the sum of all skills of the soul and of the body without which no perfect learned presentation is possible."},{"o":[180,232],"en":"Without this gift no one can be a teacher §.479."}],"491":[{"o":[0,85],"en":"A teacher must, in his learned presentation, be guided by his pupils §.479."},{"o":[86,412],"en":"Consequently one must recommend to him 1) condescension (condescendentia), when he has pupils of lesser capacity before him, and arranges his presentation in such a way that it is not too high for them; 2) elevation (coadscendentia) to the greater capacity of his pupils, so that the presentation not be too poor for them."}],"492":[{"o":[0,297],"en":"A pupil of learned cognition 1) must by nature be disposed to learned cognition; 2) he must already have exercised his understanding; 3) he must often already possess a store of learned cognition; 4) he must apply the necessary diligence to the learning of learned cognition."},{"o":[298,639],"en":"A scholar must therefore present learned cognition to no one except one who is a skilful pupil of it; and he must not become angry when he sees that some people learn nothing from his presentation, unless he turn his anger against himself, because he sees that he is applying his effort to the wrong persons."}],"493":[{"o":[0,196],"en":"In a learned presentation one has either chiefly the aim of refuting someone and his error, and it is then a refutation of another (refutatio alterius), or not."},{"o":[197,457],"en":"In the latter case one regards the reader or hearer as a pupil who is ignorant, but not as one in error, and one therefore seeks to produce a perfect learned cognition in him; such a presentation is called instruction (docere)."}],"494":[{"o":[0,376],"en":"In learned instruction the following rules must be observed: 1) One must make the beginning with the definition or description of all concepts that occur in the object, if they are not otherwise already sufficiently clear to the pupil; and one must convince him of the correctness of these concepts according to the rules of the 258. 265. 267. and 278th paragraph."}],"495":[{"o":[0,186],"en":"2) In instruction about an unprovable proposition one needs nothing further than to analyze it, and, if it can be done, to illustrate it through an example §.313-318."},{"o":[187,196],"en":"300. 487."}],"496":[{"o":[0,235],"en":"3) In instruction about an intuitive judgment one must either call back into the pupil's memory all the sensations of which this judgment consists; or one must impart them to him in the instruction §.319-321."},{"o":[236,357],"en":"202; or one must convince him through probable testimonies that others have had these sensations §.206-215."}],"497":[{"o":[0,291],"en":"4) In instruction concerning provable propositions, a) one should analyse the proposition and the solution of a problem, so that they may be properly understood; b) one should present the proof according to the analytic method, and, if it is long, bring it into one or a couple of formal syllogisms §.423."},{"o":[292,519],"en":"203. 204. 206-215; c) one should carry the whole proof on until one arrives either at unprovable and intuitive judgments, or at such provable judgments of which the other is already sufficiently convinced."},{"o":[520,574],"en":"Hence the citation of the paragraphs in writings."}],"498":[{"o":[0,174],"en":"5) One should seal the conviction of the truth by a practical representation of its practical character: for what the heart loves, the understanding believes §.216-248."}],"499":[{"o":[0,253],"en":"A correct proof that an error is an error is a true refutation (refutatio vera); but an incorrect proof that an error is an error, or that a truth is an error, is an apparent refutation (impugnatio)."},{"o":[255,301],"en":"Only errors can be correctly refuted."},{"o":[302,477],"en":"He who undertakes the refutation is the attacking party (adversarius opponens), and he who refutes the refutation is the defending party (adversarius defendens)."},{"o":[478,534],"en":"The defence is therefore either correct or false."},{"o":[535,637],"en":"A learned dispute (controversia) consists in the refutation and defence of an opinion."}],"500":[{"o":[0,288],"en":"The proposition that the attacking party refutes and the attacked party defends is called the disputed proposition (thesis controversa), and the proposition of the attacking party that cannot be true at the same time as the disputed proposition is called the counter-proposition (antithesis)."},{"o":[289,407],"en":"It is therefore opposed to the disputed proposition either in a contradictory manner §.343, or in a general manner §.345."},{"o":[408,594],"en":"The point at issue is determined (status controversiae, seu quaestionis formatur) when the attacking party clearly demonstrates which is the disputed proposition and which is the counter-proposition."}],"501":[{"o":[0,310],"en":"If the point at issue is not correctly determined, then the opponents either conduct no dispute at all, or they fall into a dispute about words (logomachia), that is, into a dispute that arises because one does not understand the other §.500, and that is always a foolish and futile business."},{"o":[311,486],"en":"Different from these, however, are disputes about words (controversia philologica), which are true disputes, whose object, however, is words or other expressions."}],"502":[{"o":[0,239],"en":"Learned disputes must be conducted according to the following rules: 1) The attacking party should seek a fault in the proof of his opponent §.194, or in general a deviation of the opponent's thoughts from the rules of logic."},{"o":[240,337],"en":"He has then indeed not refuted the matter itself, but yet his opponent's way of thinking."},{"o":[338,552],"en":"Against such an attack one cannot defend oneself at all, if it is correct; or one must show that one has not violated the rules of logic, the transgression of which has been imputed to us."}],"503":[{"o":[0,132],"en":"2) If the attacking party demonstrates the counter-proposition, it follows from this that the disputed proposition is false and that the opponent errs §.500."},{"o":[133,301],"en":"Against this attack one can defend oneself only if the opponent has committed a fault in his demonstration, and this one must show according to the first rule §.502."},{"o":[302,464],"en":"If one party can show the other that it accepts provable propositions without proof, this objection remains unanswered until this proof is furnished."}],"504":[{"o":[0,93],"en":"3) The attacking party refutes the disputed proposition by an apagogic proof §.196."},{"o":[94,264],"en":"To this belongs also the case when one shows that the disputed proposition contradicts another opinion of the opponent, although we do not accept that opinion ourselves (argumentatio ad hominem)."},{"o":[265,415],"en":"Against such an attack one can defend oneself only if there is a fault in the apagogic proof, and this one must show to the opponent §.502."}],"505":[{"o":[0,315],"en":"4) If one of the opponents abandons the point at issue and brings forward things by which the disputed proposition is not refuted and the counter-proposition is not proved, then one must not engage with such matters but concede them in the present dispute, so that it does not become far too prolix §.481."},{"o":[316,514],"en":"The other party can in this case defend itself only if the first has conceded something through haste, and then it must show that what was conceded does indeed concern the point at issue."}],"506":[{"o":[0,187],"en":"5) The attacking party can show, by a limitation §.299 that he adds to the disputed proposition, that it is not as universally true as the opponent gives it out to be."},{"o":[188,447],"en":"Against this attack we can defend ourselves only by showing either that this limitation has no influence on the present point at issue, or that it is false, or by soundly proving that our proposition is true without limitation."}],"507":[{"o":[0,266],"en":"6) The attacking party brings a distinction (distinctio) to bear against his opponent, or he shows the difference between the things that the opponent has confounded, and through the confounding of which he has been misled into entering a dispute, or into erring."},{"o":[267,364],"en":"Against a distinction one can defend oneself just as against a limitation §.506."}],"508":[{"o":[0,100],"en":"7) The attacking party can refute the universality of the disputed propositions by instances."},{"o":[101,228],"en":"An instance (instantia) is an exception to a proposition, from which it sufficiently appears that it is not universally true."},{"o":[229,506],"en":"Against an instance one can defend oneself only if one either accepts it, or shows that it does not fit, or so limits the disputed proposition that it does not fit; or demonstrates the universality of the disputed proposition without regard to the instance."}],"509":[{"o":[0,215],"en":"8) The attacked party uses reprisals (retorsio) when he shows that what the opponent has derived from the disputed proposition by the apagogic proof §.504 follows rather from the counter-proposition."},{"o":[216,356],"en":"And then the attacking party can defend himself in no other way than by showing that his opponent commits a fault in his proof."}],"510":[{"o":[0,245],"en":"9) After the refutation is completed, one should seek to instil in the opponent an abhorrence of the error, by showing, yet without violating the duties one owes to the opponent, that it is dangerous, harmful and ridiculous."},{"o":[246,373],"en":"Against this attack one can defend oneself only by showing that the opponent regards our opinion from the wrong side."},{"o":[374,472],"en":"Through a correct observance of these rules, refutations become at the same time practical §.489."}],"511":[{"o":[0,254],"en":"If one derives bad and absurd consequences from the disputed proposition, not in order to convince the opponent of his error, but to offend him in some way, then one is a consequence-maker (consequentiarius)."},{"o":[255,441],"en":"Consequence-making is not necessarily a fault against logic, but it is always a sin, and therefore deserves to be abhorred by all honest people."}],"512":[{"o":[0,417],"en":"The defence against a consequence-maker is called the justification (apologia); one can conduct it either with logical weapons, if the consequence-maker has committed faults against logic §.511, in which case one must at the same time show him his folly and wicked heart, yet without consequence-making; or one must avail oneself of the weapons of law, and then logic has nothing more to remark on the matter."}],"513":[{"o":[0,319],"en":"Although learned disputes have utility §.178, a scholar must nevertheless 1) make no trade of refuting others; 2) begin no dispute of his own over a trifle; 3) conduct the important and necessary refutations with perfect thoroughness, and 4) not defend himself against all attacks."}],"514":[{"o":[0,211],"en":"Two disputing parties dispute with one another (disputatio formaliter sumta) when the counter-proposition is presented and assessed in a learned manner, and when both take place orally and in the presence of both parties."},{"o":[213,413],"en":"He who presents the counter-proposition in a learned manner is the opponent (opponens), and he who assesses it, that is, discovers what is false and uncertain in it and in its proof, is the respondent (respondens)."},{"o":[414,598],"en":"So that disputing may not become disorderly and far too prolix, only the opponent must furnish proofs, and the respondent must do nothing further than assess the proofs."}],"515":[{"o":[0,217],"en":"Since the speeches that are delivered in disputing do not belong to the domain of logic, it is clear that the opponent makes the beginning of the disputation, and then he must 1) determine the point at issue §.500."},{"o":[218,325],"en":"501. If he does not understand the opinion of the respondent, he must have it explained to him by the latter."},{"o":[326,405],"en":"2) He should attack the respondent according to one of the rules set out in §.502-510."},{"o":[406,586],"en":"3) For the sake of brevity and clarity, he should present his proofs in formal syllogisms §.399, and 4) for the same reason he should make use of the analytic method §.423."},{"o":[587,686],"en":"The Socratic and Platonic method makes disputing too prolix and burdensome §.430."},{"o":[687,791],"en":"Unless one were to see oneself compelled, by the first, to keep the other party to the point at issue."}],"516":[{"o":[0,202],"en":"The respondent accepts the argument (assumere argumentum) when he repeats the objection of the opponent, and this is useful in order to consider it properly, so that he can answer it soundly."},{"o":[203,552],"en":"For the sake of brevity the respondent must do nothing further than show where the opponent has committed a fault either in the matter or in the form of his proof §.502, and then he must demand that the opponent either change his inference of reason, or prove the premise that seems to the respondent false or uncertain."}],"517":[{"o":[0,147],"en":"The assistant of the respondent (praeses) is to prevent the respondent from conceding the counter-proposition that has not yet been sufficiently proved."},{"o":[148,265],"en":"He must therefore, when the respondent knows nothing more, or nothing skilful, to answer, take over his office §.516."},{"o":[265,289],"en":"On learned writings."}],"518":[{"o":[0,68],"en":"The presentation of past events is history (historia)."},{"o":[69,159],"en":"Learned writings present either history or dogmatic truths §.104."},{"o":[160,264],"en":"The former are historical (scripta erudita historica), and the latter dogmatic writings (scripta dogmatica)."},{"o":[265,365],"en":"These present either a common cognition of dogmatic truths, or a learned one §.18."},{"o":[366,600],"en":"21. The former are historical-dogmatic (scriptum historico-dogmaticum), the latter, however, systematic writings (scriptum systematicum), which are scientific writings (scriptum scientificum) if they present a science §.434."},{"o":[601,693],"en":"Every writing is, in regard to its content, either very extensive or very brief."},{"o":[694,764],"en":"The former is a great work (systema), the latter, however, an epitome (compendium)."}],"519":[{"o":[0,303],"en":"History narrates either things that belong to nature and art, insofar as one considers in them neither the free actions of human beings nor their closer effects; or it narrates the free actions of human beings, together with everything that stands in a closer connection with them."},{"o":[304,438],"en":"The former is the history of nature and art (historia naturae et artis), the latter, however, history simply (historia stricte dicta)."},{"o":[439,1133],"en":"To the last belongs 1) political history (historia politica), which narrates the civil and political actions of human beings; 2) ecclesiastical history (historia ecclesiastica), which narrates the fortunes of the churches and the actions of human beings that have a noticeable influence on them; 3) private history (historia privata), which narrates the course of life of individual persons, insofar as it has no noticeable influence on the state, the church and learning; 4) learned history (historia litteraria), which narrates the events of the learned world, and it is either a history of the disciplines, or of the learned, or of learned writings."}],"520":[{"o":[0,96],"en":"A historical writing must be 1) detailed §.481.482, neither too prolix nor too brief."},{"o":[97,298],"en":"It can therefore be detailed for one purpose and for one reader, too prolix for another purpose and for another reader, and too brief for a third purpose and for a third reader."},{"o":[299,344],"en":"With time every history becomes too brief."},{"o":[345,453],"en":"2) Important, both as regards the whole content and as regards that which is narrated of it §.483."},{"o":[454,471],"en":"3) Correct §.484."},{"o":[472,539],"en":"4) Distinct §.485, hence paintings and copperplate engravings are often necessary."},{"o":[540,625],"en":"5) Thorough §.488, consequently proving everything according to the rules of paragraphs 206-215."},{"o":[626,683],"en":"6) Practical or pragmatic (historia pragmatica) §.489."},{"o":[684,703],"en":"7) Methodical §.487."},{"o":[704,708],"en":"432."}],"521":[{"o":[0,182],"en":"If history is to be pragmatic and detailed at the same time, it must be presented in such a way that a purpose is attained that is practical enough §.520."},{"o":[183,446],"en":"Now since everything that is actual in this world, if it can be cognised by us human beings, aims not only at the honour of GOD but also at our happiness, a writer of history must seek to attain these two purposes through history."},{"o":[447,757],"en":"In particular, in natural history one must have a twofold purpose §.519: 1) to come to know better the perfections of GOD from natural things, and 2) to impart to readers distinct concepts of natural things, and thereby to pave the way to learned cognition of them."}],"522":[{"o":[0,234],"en":"History in the narrower sense §.519 redounds to the honour of GOD if one recognises, from the events of human beings, the divine government of the human race, and the perfections that GOD reveals in it."},{"o":[235,712],"en":"To the happiness of human beings contributes 1) civil history, if one can learn from it political prudence in the affairs of war and peace; 2) ecclesiastical history, if one can learn from it the prudence of governing the church, and if it develops the marks of the true and the false church; 3) learned history, if it promotes learning; 4) private history, if one can learn from it human prudence in private life."}],"523":[{"o":[0,75],"en":"The historical-dogmatic writings are not learned writings §.518."},{"o":[76,366],"en":"But if they are detailed, important, comprehensible, correct and practical, they are very useful to people who cannot and will not be made learned, likewise also to children who are to be introduced to the sciences, in order to instil in them a foretaste of learning."}],"524":[{"o":[0,55],"en":"A systematic book must be 1) detailed §.481."},{"o":[56,276],"en":"482. It must therefore either contain everything that was known of a doctrine at the time the book was written, or it must contain as much of it as the particular purpose of the author requires."},{"o":[277,670],"en":"The most detailed writings can in time become undetailed, and one must therefore carefully distinguish from one another the detailedness of a dogmatic book in respect of time, author and reader; 2) important §.483; 3) correct §.484, in that not only are all the thoughts contained in it true, but each is also in conformity with the rules of logic."},{"o":[671,706],"en":"4) Distinct and comprehensible §.485."},{"o":[707,804],"en":"5) Thorough §.488, it must therefore be written according to the learned method and coherently."},{"o":[805,824],"en":"6) Practical §.489."},{"o":[825,849],"en":"7) Methodical §.414-438."}],"525":[{"o":[0,210],"en":"Botching together (compilatio) consists in this, that an author writes together everything he finds here and there on the matter he wants to write about, without properly connecting it with one another."},{"o":[211,353],"en":"Although a botched-together work can be useful to other scholars, it nevertheless remains, in regard to the author, a wretched work."},{"o":[354,505],"en":"A writing is stolen together (plagiarius) when the author gives himself out as the inventor of those thoughts that he has learned from others."}],"526":[{"o":[0,267],"en":"He who makes an epitome of a great work (epitomator), by omitting from it everything that is superfluous with regard to his smaller aim, can write a very useful and necessary book, provided he has chosen his purpose reasonably §.518."},{"o":[267,301],"en":"On the character of a scholar."}],"527":[{"o":[0,175],"en":"The character of a scholar (character eruditi) consists in the sum total of those of his marks that are the nearer grounds and causes of learned cognition."}],"528":[{"o":[0,346],"en":"The general character of a scholar (generalis character eruditi) is the character of a scholar insofar as it contains the nearer grounds of learning in general; the special, however (character eruditi specialis), insofar as it contains the nearer grounds of this or that kind, and of this or that part of learning."}],"529":[{"o":[0,251],"en":"The first part of the general character of a scholar consists in the learned natural disposition (natura erudita), or in that proportion of all powers of the soul by virtue of which a human being is fitted and inclined for learned cognition §.528."}],"530":[{"o":[0,188],"en":"To the learned natural disposition belongs 1) native wit, the learned head (ingenium eruditum), the proportion of the powers of cognition by which a human being is fitted for learned cognition."},{"o":[189,466],"en":"To this belongs a) reason (ratio), the faculty of distinctly seeing the connection of things §.21, which must be naturally disposed to become sound, widely extended, strong, thorough and beautiful. b) The understanding (intellectus), the faculty of distinct cognition §.21."},{"o":[467,563],"en":"17, which must be naturally disposed to become widely extended, profound, pure and beautiful."},{"o":[564,669],"en":"Consequently great attention, reflection, consideration and abstraction belong to native wit §.142."},{"o":[670,815],"en":"2) A beautiful mind (ingenium pulchrum), the proportion of the powers of cognition by virtue of which a human being is disposed to beautiful thinking §.32."}],"531":[{"o":[0,405],"en":"To the learned natural disposition belongs 2) the learned temperament (temperamentum eruditum), or the proportion of the faculties of desire, and their agreement with native wit, by virtue of which a human being is not only inclined to attain a learned cognition, but by virtue of which also the powers of cognition are duly exerted to effect the most perfect learned cognition §.529."}],"532":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"The learned natural disposition is a mere gift of fortune, which must be innate in a human being, and without which it is impossible to attain learned cognition anywhere by any art."}],"533":[{"o":[0,242],"en":"Native wit is called the innate natural logic (logica naturalis connata), but the science of learned cognition and of learned presentation, scholastic wit, or artificial logic (logica artificialis)."},{"o":[243,556],"en":"The indistinct cognition of the rules of native wit, together with the skill of observing them, which one acquires by the mere use of native wit, is called acquired natural logic (logica naturalis acquisita theoretica et practica), and one reckons it to native wit in the wider sense."},{"o":[557,689],"en":"Native wit does not contradict scholastic wit; it is indispensable to the latter both in theory and in practice."},{"o":[690,737],"en":"It is also sufficient outside the disciplines."},{"o":[738,1005],"en":"But without scholastic wit no learned cognition can be attained; it improves native wit, and if a great scholastic wit is joined with a great native wit, a human being can get much further than by the best native wit taken alone."}],"534":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"He who wants to become a great scholar must follow nature, and apply himself to learned cognition only if he possesses the learned natural disposition, and insofar as he possesses it §.532."}],"535":[{"o":[0,316],"en":"One can recognise whether one possesses the learned natural disposition: 1) if one has a reasonable inclination to learned cognition, because its perfections delight us; 2) if it goes easily for us whenever we think in a learned way; 3) if one becomes aware by an attempt that one can actually think in a learned way."}],"536":[{"o":[0,140],"en":"The second part of the general character of a scholar consists in the skills that he gradually attains through exercises §.527."},{"o":[141,413],"en":"To the skills of a scholar belong: 1) The extension of the understanding and of reason (extensio intellectus et rationis), the skill of cognising many things distinctly and in a manifold connection, and of cognising many clear marks of each thing §.25."},{"o":[414,613],"en":"2) The strength of reason and of the understanding (intensio intellectus et rationis), the skill of cognising things in a great degree of completeness and mathematical certainty §.27."},{"o":[614,620],"en":"28.29."},{"o":[621,877],"en":"3) The skill of thinking beautifully §.32 4) A great heart §.91 (magnitudo pectoris), the skill of thinking and acting in accordance with the learned temperament §.531, which presupposes the skill of thinking in no other way than greatly and practically §.26."},{"o":[878,881],"en":"30."}],"537":[{"o":[0,133],"en":"The learned exercises are A) the more general (exercitia generaliora), the frequent repetitions of learned meditation in general."},{"o":[134,286],"en":"To this belongs 1) learned naturalising, the exercises one undertakes without art, and which make up acquired natural logic §.433."}],"538":[{"o":[0,183],"en":"2) The more perfect, regular and artificial exercises (exercitia oculatiora, logica artificialis practica), or the frequent observance of the rules of artificial logic."},{"o":[184,475],"en":"To this end a) one should learn a good artificial logic; b) one should seek to practise all its rules; c) when one has produced and written down a series of thoughts according to the logical rules, one should hold them against these rules, and examine where one has observed them or not."},{"o":[476,646],"en":"Through these exercises one must finally bring it about that one observes the rules of artificial logic in thinking and speaking without being conscious of them."}],"539":[{"o":[0,224],"en":"B) The special learned exercises, or studying (exercitia erudita specialiora), consists in all actions by which learned cognition arises and is improved in him who undertakes these actions."},{"o":[225,256],"en":"Six exercises belong to this."}],"540":[{"o":[0,44],"en":"I) Learning from an oral presentation."},{"o":[45,134],"en":"Because this exercise is the easiest and surest, one must make the beginning with it."},{"o":[135,288],"en":"He who wants to be taught by this exercise must 1) have prepared his head for learning through the lower and beautiful sciences."},{"o":[289,454],"en":"2) He must seek out a teacher at least moderately competent, from whose oral presentation he can hope to attain a good learned cognition."},{"o":[455,570],"en":"3) He must pay proper attention to the oral presentation, so that he may rightly understand the teacher and his presentation."},{"o":[571,684],"en":"4) He must meditate through everything the teacher presents, or at least the main points of his presentation §.436."},{"o":[685,887],"en":"437. 5) He must judge logically (logice diiudicare) everything he has heard, that is, seek to recognise what logical perfections or imperfections are found in it."},{"o":[888,1210],"en":"Hence he must investigate to what kind and species of learned cognition that belongs which he wants to judge; he must recall the logical rules of this kind or species, and seek to recognise, from the comparison of the object with these rules, whether it is in conformity with them or not."}],"541":[{"o":[0,34],"en":"II) The reading of learned writings."},{"o":[35,151],"en":"To this end one must a) set before oneself a reasonable purpose for the sake of which one wants to read learned writings."},{"o":[152,557],"en":"A student must therefore read books 1) in order to review a discipline that one has already learned; 2) in order to improve our learned cognition more and more; 3) in order to learn a part of learning that one does not yet understand; 4) in order to practise oneself in the observance of the rules of logic. b) One must choose a book that is suited to promote this purpose."},{"o":[558,646],"en":"Consequently one must 1) not read all books as they happen to fall into one's hands."},{"o":[647,803],"en":"2) In the beginning read only such books as one has found, after a logical judgment, to be suited to promote our purpose."},{"o":[804,1206],"en":"3) If one already possesses a skill in thinking in a learned way, one can also read with profit such books as are full of defects and faults. c) One must seek to understand the book and the author rightly. d) One must meditate through and logically judge every thought presented in the book, as in §.540. n. 4. 5, so that one may learn rightly to see into the things presented."}],"542":[{"o":[0,173],"en":"The useful reading of learned writings is promoted by the observance of the following rules: 1) One should read with the greatest attention, without tolerating foreign thoughts."},{"o":[174,252],"en":"2) One should read the book through from the beginning, if it is written systematically."},{"o":[253,331],"en":"3) One should never interrupt the reading in a closely connected matter."},{"o":[332,415],"en":"4) One should read the book through hastening slowly, and neither too quickly nor too slowly."},{"o":[416,490],"en":"5) One must not become weary of reading before one has come to the end."},{"o":[491,588],"en":"6) One must not read too many books, especially of differing content, intermingled with one another."},{"o":[589,755],"en":"7) One should make the beginning with the shorter epitomes, proceed to the greater works, and finally read writings that treat particular matters in detail."},{"o":[756,866],"en":"8) One should read the writings that belong to a principal part of learning according to the synthetic method."}],"543":[{"o":[0,256],"en":"The learning of learned cognition is greatly promoted, partly if at first one learns the elements of the learning to which one has devoted oneself from only One teacher; partly if one at once employs the truths learned and brings them to practice."}],"544":[{"o":[0,90],"en":"III) Frequent repetition (repetitio), or recollection of what one has learned."},{"o":[91,221],"en":"For this it is required that one observe the following rules: 1) One must not make of this repetition a mere learning by heart."},{"o":[222,424],"en":"2) In the repetition one must meditate through and judge every thought just as if one wanted to learn it for the first time, even though this always goes more briskly and easily."},{"o":[425,555],"en":"3) One should repeat the truths learned according to the synthetic method according to the analytic and inferential method §.423."},{"o":[556,628],"en":"428. 4) One should repeat the repetition often, with some variation."},{"o":[629,694],"en":"5) One should always learn more besides, and practise the truths learned."},{"o":[695,803],"en":"6) If one writes down one's meditations in the repetition, this exercise is thereby greatly promoted."},{"o":[804,894],"en":"7) One should present the truths learned, in writing or orally, in a learned manner."},{"o":[895,957],"en":"8) One should converse with others about the truths learned."}],"545":[{"o":[0,257],"en":"The repetition and the learning of learned cognition is greatly promoted if one writes down briefly, and with a reasonable choice, that which one fears to forget, and which at the same time brings the rest back to our memory."}],"546":[{"o":[0,126],"en":"IV) Investigation (investigatio), or the reasonable imitation of other scholars in their learned thinking and presentation."},{"o":[127,209],"en":"To this end 1) one should choose a great scholar as a model to follow."},{"o":[210,442],"en":"2) One should investigate how he manages to think so learnedly, so thoroughly, so orderly, etc. 3) One should investigate how he discovered the truths, or how they could be discovered if they had not yet been found.","m":[2,1]},{"o":[443,508],"en":"4) One should seek to think and to speak in the same way in similar cases."}],"547":[{"o":[0,98],"en":"V) One should present the disciplines to others in a learned manner, either orally or in writing."},{"o":[100,200],"en":"Or learned presentation is an exercise in learned cognition; for by teaching we learn."}],"548":[{"o":[0,261],"en":"VI) The invention of new truths (inventio), when we cognise something in a learned way without learning it from other human beings; whether we be the first human beings to cognise it, or others may already have known it before us."},{"o":[262,331],"en":"This exercise is the most difficult, and must be put off until last."}],"549":[{"o":[0,198],"en":"An autodidact is a scholar who has been taught without a teacher, whether he has either invented all his learning §.548, or has merely received no oral instruction."},{"o":[199,524],"en":"A reasonable human being chooses the easiest and surest way when he first seeks to learn from an oral presentation, then reads books, and rather applies his power of invention to discovering wholly new truths than such as have long been known, if all this is otherwise only possible for him."}],"550":[{"o":[0,245],"en":"Just as it is a fault to want to be made learned without native wit §.534; so it is a negligent way of thinking in a learned manner (negligens eruditionis genus et impolitum) if one does not duly combine art with native wit §.533."}],"551":[{"o":[0,419],"en":"The affected and forced way of thinking in a learned manner (affectatum et coactum eruditionis genus) arises when scholastic wit is combined with native wit in a bad manner: 1) if the rules of artificial logic are false and ungrounded; 2) if its correct rules are badly applied, in that they are not suited either to the truths or to the natural disposition of the scholar."}],"552":[{"o":[0,222],"en":"A cognition and a presentation that are merely learned are the dry, sober, rough, scholastic and pedantic kind of learning (macilentum, ieiunum, pedanticum, scholasticum eruditionis genus)."},{"o":[223,353],"en":"In order to prevent this, a student must daily devote some time, in the hours of recreation, to the beautiful sciences."}],"553":[{"o":[0,312],"en":"The third part of the general character of a scholar consists in learned diligence (diligentia erudita), or in as great an exertion of all acquired learned skills as is required in each case when one wants to think and present in a learned way in a certain degree of perfection §.527."},{"o":[313,357],"en":"To this end one must observe ten rules."}],"554":[{"o":[0,194],"en":"1) A student must, above all things, mark out the horizon of his learned cognition as accurately as possible, so that he may know in each case on what he has to direct his learned diligence."},{"o":[195,370],"en":"And so that this horizon may be assumed neither too small nor too large, one must gradually extend it further, as our powers and skills increase."}],"555":[{"o":[0,219],"en":"2) A student must judge his powers most exactly, as to whether they suffice for learning in general, and for that part of it to which he applies himself, and for the cognition of this or that truth."},{"o":[220,706],"en":"To this end a) he must test himself as to whether he possesses the learned natural disposition §.529-535. b) One should transform that with regard to which one wants to test one's powers into a problem §.328-339, and examine whether one has powers enough to actually effect the solution. c) One should guard against the prejudice of too great confidence and of mistrust in the judgment of one's powers §.170. d) One should test one's powers by a faithful trial. e) One should let oneself be judged by others."}],"556":[{"o":[0,203],"en":"3) In studying one must not apply too much diligence, and must not direct learned diligence to things that are found above, below and outside the horizon of our learned cognition §.554."}],"557":[{"o":[0,212],"en":"4) One must also not apply too little diligence, but as much as is required to undertake all learned exercises duly, and gradually to study through the whole horizon of our learned cognition."}],"558":[{"o":[0,101],"en":"5) One must begin to study as early as is possible; what one wants to do, one should do soon."}],"559":[{"o":[0,81],"en":"6) One must, if it is otherwise possible for us, study daily, and think in a learned way."},{"o":[82,133],"en":"He who does not go further forward goes backward."}],"560":[{"o":[0,72],"en":"7) One must, in the midst of the course of studying, always hasten slowly."},{"o":[73,243],"en":"So that one may not hasten too much, one must not leave a learned occupation until one has satisfied all the rules of logic in it."},{"o":[244,337],"en":"But so that one may also not be too slow, one must duly spur on the desire to study."}],"561":[{"o":[0,130],"en":"8) One must never cease to learn, in that one either always learns something new besides, or learns to cognise better what has already been learned."}],"562":[{"o":[0,132],"en":"9) One must constantly seek to improve the learning one has already attained, and to remove its still outstanding defects and faults."}],"563":[{"o":[0,271],"en":"10) One must combine with studying daily experience, intercourse with the respectable world, and the use of the very things on which one meditates in a learned way; so that one may not, as a mere learned worm, live on school-dust, and dream of Platonic republics."}]};
